      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>MY DEAR NELLIE</head>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE</p>
        <p>Many and various will apply to the headgear adopted just now. Very popular are the toques of stitehed cloth, but many prefer toques oZ panne, of which some delightful examplee are to bk. soon. Panne is so soft and lustrous, and draDhs so amiably, that it is an ideal material for millinery but its high prico keeps the panne toque from popularity. In two shades of green panne, with lines of mink tails and bunches of violets, was a charming little toque I saw the other day. The latest rumour is that the hat of the immediate future will display ribbon or velvet streamers at the back, bedizened with paillettas and the like. Generally speaking, hate with:feathers and ribbons are still worn. •</p>
        <p>Long-walking costumes are to the fore just now, seeing that so much rain has been falling. Some have rows of machine-stitching and the piping of red cloth beneath them. The skirt is bordered with about twice as many rows of stitching, and the line of piped red cloth, the material of the dress itself being a dull grey-green cloth. Tho vest and and revers, as well as the lining of the collar, are red velvet stitched with black. The sleeve trim- ming consists of two or three very narrow tucks grouped closely together, and is introduced merely for the purpose of relieving the plainness of the sleeve. Rifle green is the colour of some costumes. Tue toque worn is white felt with a bandanna trimming suited to the embroidery.</p>
        <p>Afternoon and evening bloused are notabls attractive. For these glace, silk is the al but in•</p>
        <p>dispensable foundation, although it is in not a few instances entirely concealed by chiffon, lace, or - sequin net. For ordinary wear, however, a useful model is of uncovered glace in a pretty shade of apple green ; both back aad front are finely tucked, the tucks arranged in groups of five running from neck to waist, divided by bands of narrow black velvet ribbon, and rosettes of black velvet and green ribbon, exactly matching the Bilk, being placed down the side opening of the blouse. The tight plain glace sleeves are finished with velvet bands and little silk frills, the neck arrangement corresponding. Such a blouse as this could be sucoessfally copied in other ehades. In primrose glac6, with stripes of chesimut-brown velvet, it would look exceedingly well worn with a skirt of chestnut-brown silk repp or fine face-cloth, while in rose-pink and black it would have a stylish appearance with a skirt of black or very deep crimson brocade or silk. Another pretty blouse is</p>
        <p>of turquoise glace covered with horizontal rows of narrow black baby-ribbon slightly gathered along the top edge. There iH a yoke of black jetted lace carried over the tops of the arms, according to the latest fashion, and outlined with a ruching of lace.</p>
        <p>The new styles of beirdressing allow the fcrehead to be seen again and about the temple:l the coiffure recalls that adopted many years ago. The size of the coiffure was about hall the Ffz-: in those old days to what it is now, and the preent elaboration was unknown. The torturing of the hair with hot irons had not bNon. Ir seem-4 now to have reached its height. Rt,lgo upon ridge of undula- tions rise above the brows, and other ridges etretch away behind the ears. Sew of the very 6martest ladies dress their hair thickly and heavily on one aide, scraping it clolely away from the other. To aid the one-sided effect, a long lace sleeve is worn on the coiffure side, and none at all on that of the plain. This is one of the strangest freaks of fashion. A great loop of hair is often brought down over the left eye, and it will be found that the bulk of the trimming on the hat or toque will be on that side. The forehead is very seldom wholly uncovered, a curl at one side or other, or in the centre, obviating any hardness of effect. Your loving, JENNIE.</p>
