      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>A few years ago it was generally acknowledged that if one would see fine carriages and home, and smart servants, it was necessary to go to Paris. Nowadays things are different. It wouldlbe difficult to beat London in the tresses, in this particular direction. Take for in- stance Lord Lonsdale's, Lord Cadocan's. Lard London- derry's, Lord Ireaeli's, Lard Burton's, Lady Stasoon's, Lady Warwick's, Georgina, Lady Dudley's, and Mrs. Bischoffsheini's cquipages and s-rvants. The latter'n equipages rank quite in the first flight. Her landau is beautifully turned out, the big cheßnut horses and dark green liveries being especially well known in London. Lady Londonderry's landau ie smartness itself, the ser- vants in dark blue and silver liveries with pewdered hair and wigs, making a brave show. In the seventeenth century the fashion of powdering the hair was almost universal, among both sexes of the higher rank of society. It originated in France, and gradually became fashionable in Europe. The hair was well creased with pomade to make the powder stick. In 1795 hair powder was taxed and yielded about £20,000 per annum—but it had the effect of altering the fashion by a return to unpowdered hair. The tsx.latterly yielding less than £l,OOO a year, was ahtlished in 1869. According to an authority on till.) subject those who drive in carriages and carts, pay altogether £510,890 a year for the privelege. If 000's carriage has four or more -wheels, and is fitted to be drawn by two or more horses,one must pay into the National Exc.hequnr £2 2i. a year for it. If one's carriage has bur or morn wheels and is drawn by one horse, one can have the thing for balf:this amount, and if one's 'carriage has less than fratir wheels, one can get off with an annual oayment of 15s. In the natter of armorial hearings. £2 2s. must he paid each year. and " Jeames " the Exchequer values at another 15s. per annum. Last year no leas than t o hundred and five thousand aril thirty-six licenses for men vrvante were issued,and brought in a revenue of £153,770.</p>
        <p>Miss Cbaplin's marriage will, after all, tske place on the 28th inst., the date fixed before the death of Lord Reginald Stewart, the brictrgroem's brother—Lord and Lady Londonderry unselfishly not wishing to have it altered. Wedding presents have been pouring in at Stafford House, during th. past two or three weeks,and it would seem as though the popular young couple would " beat the record" in this direction. Lord Castle- reagh's gilt 3 to his future wife are magnificent. As in the case of bumbler individuals, wedding gifts have, of late years become a very heavy tax to the Prince and Princess of Wales. The former constantly ssurie a souvenir of the kind, even to those with whom he is not intimately acquainted, and both the Prince and Princess are always, ot course, handsmely represented at these weddings which el frequently take place among their relations.</p>
        <p>Transparent trains have not " caught on "among fashionable brides, the prevailing idea kiting that they are too flimsy and unsubstantial to be either graceful or comfortable wear. ha the matter of bodices, many of the hest modistes are building the law Court variety, so that it can be worn cvar a high slip, as this leaves the gown ready for presentation at Court, or for the ball room.</p>
        <p>Tulle veils are to longer correct,Brussels lace specimens being quite the smartest wear. No doubt real lace does give a cachet to a Watts that no amount:of tulle could achieve, still worn over the face the lace veil is heavy and often unbecoming, while thrown back, it usually has the appearance of weighing down the head. Clouds of tulle are, if less distinguished looking decidedly more becoming to the average bride. Some fashlon papers are edit picturing the long cling- ing skirt, emerging from the tunic to swirl in many frilled folds around the feet, but these particular gar- meats are really only to he seen in the fashion papers. Skirts are no longer built a quarter of a yard on the groond all round• For house wear they just touch in front, and trail a little behind, for walking they clear the around all round. The box pleated skirt is gaining favcur, and arranged by a first class modiste is a very fascinating item—seen on a alender wereen. But like so many other modes it is only possible for the elim, and graceful. It is difficult to imagine how we could ever have got alone without Virgella flannel. The " boon and bless- ing "of this particular fabric has surely exceeded that of the pen of fame. And now another blessing hae desoendeo upon us in the shape of Virgella hosiery and underwear. This it manufactured from the same yarn precise'y ae is used in the weaving of the famous flume, and the garments are therefore absolutely unshrinkable. Nightdresses, censhima'ions, bodice', etc., are now b•ing ehown in all leading shops, built of this particular yara. and in colour, shape, and finish, leave nothieg to be desired. There is an old proverb vehicle says that where the sun does not eater the doctor must, and like other say- ings of the axiom kind, it has some truth at the bottom. Better far, spoil the carpets and hangings, although this evil can easily be averred by the judicious use of covers, than spoil one's health. Housewives should make a point of catching every gleam of sunshine available, and on no account allow cold winds to interfere with the daily airing of each room. Fe r slee ring pusposes the wiadow should be allowed to remain open just the " bar," the bed being so placed as to escape any chance of draught. Fewer headaches, fewer colds, and better appetites and complexions woulsi be en evidence if this rule were catefully carried out. Talking of sleep, an insufficient amount of the same is one of the eryieg evils of the day. Not only do parents fail to take sufficient sleep themselves, hut they deny childr• is their proper share by kesping them up late. A healthy infant sleeps most of the time during the first few weeks, and in the early years parents are disposed to let children eleen as they will, a sensible course to pursue. But when they reach the age of six er seven. and commence going to echool,this sensible polies comes to an 7 end in many households, crsd children are kept up late doing home lessons. At the age of ten or eleven a child very often gets only eight or nine hours &amp;rep, when it requires at least ten or eleven hours, whinh number the parent should insist upon its having. The amount ef sleep a chi4l gets when young determines to a great extent, its physical and enental health when it attains maturity. Much excitement is said to have been caused in Tripol and other countries by a rely''e that the world wruld come to an end on Neeeenteir 13 Among ether ree‘ers of this belief, we are told debtors refused to ray ties ir debts, and most of the werkmen ceased work. Three debtors it may he remarked seem to have added a tcp(h of native duplicity to the carefulre,s of the divine, who, possessing a sirrilsr I)clirf, is said to have oid-r,d Ili, coals by the lark. Apparently Tripoli wonlil be a good field for a profrtsiora! prophet, but perheps it would fiercely do tor him to stay too long, seeing that the tat ople might raise, objections when ho explained after each failure that there had been a mis- take in the date. In tact it is possible that he might meet a fate similar to that whiP h betel a missionary years ago in New Z- eland. "Where is Mr. en sne or?" asked reimehedy of a Caief. "Oh wan the reply, "he too much talkee, so we care. him " .</p>
        <p>" AIADGE."</p>
        <p>According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazeete," the. num- ber of failures in England aud Wstes gszoted Otniag the k ending November lit h, was 93 Tho uuenher in the corresponding week of last year wsiv 98, snowing a dscresse of 5, being a !act decrease in 1899, to date, of 207.</p>
