      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>(Copyright.) _ NOBLER THAN REVENGE.</p>
        <p>BY ESA.If] STUART, Author of "In the Dark," "Inscrutable," "A Woman of Forty," "Sara's Mother," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: On his way to visit his cousin, Sir Harry Beauworth, Alec Beauworth, heir to the title, is set upon by two men and knocked senseless. Sir Harry has a bad reputa- tion. Bob Rabbitts, son of the evil-looking landlord of the Dog and Duck, revives Alec, and determines, as he has the chance, to enter Sir Harry's service for a time to see that Alec comes to no harm. Sir Harry has one daughter, Ruby, a beautiful and unsophisticated girl, who has never been allowed to see the world, and whom her father intends to marry to an Australian, James Felton, who with his faller, Tom Felton, appears to have a powerful Influence over him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>' Bob tapped at the back window of the Dog and Duck and his mother let him in. "You mun take off your boots off, Bob, your father's only just up abed. The squire has been here. There's mischief follows when he comes."</p>
        <p>"I've changed my mind, mother; I'll go to the squire." _</p>
        <p>"You changed your mind, Bob, and you 'chiding so over it!"</p>
        <p>"The squire won't kill me, and father kep' on ao."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Rabbitts had never known her son give way before. She became suspicious. "Don't you be playing tricks on the squire, Bob.l It's as much as my-" "Don't take on, mother. Did you see him?" "No; he came in with two of his friends, but he didn't stay over long, and a good thing, too, for your father--" "Did you bear what father said of me?" "No, I didn't go nigh them, Bob, but you'll come back of nights?" "Just put the lights o' nights the same as usual,, mother, and somehow I'll get to you, spite of tilt; squire." .</p>
        <p>"It's not the squire's way to like for folks tti see much. If one could see back behind him-" said Mrs. Rabbitts, with a solemn shake of her head.</p>
        <p>"Well, I'm off, mother. If father's worse just send Black Patch to me, and mind Dobbin gets her fodder right and--" "Well, well, I never! You are a queer 'un, Bob, though you are my boy." Bob grinned. "Do you think, mother, there's any as can out- wit the old gentleman ?" "I reckon not," said Mrs. Rabbitts, meditatively.\ "Well, I'll hey a try," and with that Bob made off again towards Yewdale</p>
        <p>In the meanwhile the new arrival was receiving a welcome from the squire. In the central hall a cold but sufficient supper was spread, well seasoned with apologies from the host. "I was suddenly called away, so suddenly) indeed, that I forgot to tell Rudd to make my excuses. As you did not arrive as soon as--" "I met with an accident onithe way," said Alec,' remembering Bob's wishes and anxious not to get any of his friends into trouble. "I was walking t along the road when I suppose I must have fallen,l for I can remember nothing till I found myself in a shed. I made my way out, and, happening td meet a boy who said he was coming here, I followed him through devious ways which did not improve my garments." The 'quire gave a quick 71r lAI who</p>
        <p>squire gave a quic- glance a, iec, wno, for Bob's sake, did not flinch. "That was Bob, the son of mine host of the Dog and Duck. Did you pass the little inn?" "No, I believe not, but I am only now getting back all my wits. I must have fallen and knocked the commonsonse out of my brain."</p>
        <p>"That is all you rememlier ? " "Bob's prattle has hardly remained on my muddled memory."</p>
        <p>"You will be *anxious to know, Mr. Beanwlrth, why I have asked you to take this journey in mid- winter to see a relation who is quite unknown to you, and who to you must merely represent the man who keeps you out of an estate. To say the truth, when I saw the advertisement, I had long been forgotten by the world and cared little to return to it. I had more than once been reported as dead."</p>
        <p>"Indeed. I had never given you up," answered Alec, smiling, "so I was not greatly disappointed. Besides, you might have left a son.' "I have but one daughter, and I must tell you that she is engaged, I should say promised, to a cousin—a very distant cousin." "But I hope the lovers are happy ? " said Alec, wondering if he had taken this journey merely to be told that the young lady was engaged. "Per- sonally, I fear, I cannot tell her, by experience, that the married state is perfect bliss. ' The squire took no notice of the joke. His lips hardly parted in a faint smile. I "My daughter has not seen her lover. She is a good child, however, and will obey me." Alec lifted his eyebrows, but as he gazed at the handsome, impassive face before him, he felt instinctively that the young lady had best obey. _ . . .</p>
        <p>. _ j "Am Ito have the pleasure of acting as best man ? "</p>
        <p>"Matters have not gone so far as that," answered the squire. "You will see my daughter to-morrow, and also the man to whom she is destined. On her marriage Ruby will, I hope, not leave me, but as I have no son and you are the heir to the property, it is as well that we should meet. I naturally wish to leave her as much money as it is in my power; but there is very little of this property that I can dispose of. In case of your death I believe, as there is no other direct heir, that you can leave it by will. You are unmarried. Is there any prospect of your-" Alec smiled and shook his head. "No, I have not had time. )rha no inclina=</p>
        <p>Pek 'Ps Con, to take a wife. I am living on my own earnings, and in theso days a wife expects more from her husband than daily bread and no butter." "I want you to become acquainted with us. have never made advances before for fear of what the world might say. In spite of our better judg- ment we are afraid of having motives imputed to us."</p>
        <p>"You mean that people would say that you wanted to catch me for your daughter, and now that she is engaged you no longer fear vulgar comments., You have acted most honourably." Alec was feeling that his host's former neglect was well explained. He now saw that he was expected to make friends with the squire's daughter,. and to interest himself in her future. This was a little tame but natural. The squire was going to marry her to a man of his own choice, and he hoped that the next heir, being unmarried, might make a will in her,favour and that of her children,' if she had any. "You must need rest," said the squire, when the short meal was ended. "I am sorry that you did not find the way to Yewdale Hall as easy as I would have made it. Certainly this neighbourhood is very lonely. I sometimes think that I hawk done wrong to keep my daughter ignorant of all outside things, but I prefer a quiet life. I have had enough of the world." r "I envy you your experiences. Did I gather that the boy who led me here was to enter your service? If so, will you allow me to go out with •a gun to-morrow and to borrow him to act as keeper ? " "Bob Rabbitts is the innkeeper's son. He is ij ,very rough specimen, but my old servant is getting past work. Certainly take Bob, he knows all the country round. You will stay the week, and by, that time we shall, I hope, have arranged some- thing about the future of the property." ' Left alone in his large room, lighted with a' br4ll- fire, Alec was well contented with the turn of affairs. The squire had been open with hirn.l He had made no pretences, he was not unduly, atfectionate, nor had he been cunning enough to have invited him sooner to the Hall, with the chance of making a match with his daughter, and in this way securing the property for her. i He could have done this easily without much' fear of adverse criticism. Well, everything depended on the young lady herself; and though he would have preferred that a touch of romance could have been possible, Alcc dismissed this possi- bility from his mind and turned towards his bed.l Only then did he remember his promise to Bob.! He went to the window, and drew back the heavy curtain. The hasp was very old and quaint. Alec opened it, feeling a new interest in this place which might eventually be his. • As he turned away he was startled from his pleasant dream by a sudden shadow, then he re- membered Bob, and at that moment the strange boy tumbled into the room. i "Well, Bob, this can hardly be your destine= Lion." exclaimed Alec. laughing. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>NOVEMBER 17, 1899.</head>
        <p>NOVEMBER 17, 1899.</p>
        <p>FRI D A Y,</p>
        <p>"I'm going to sleep on the floor, sir. You , I promised, and this house is awful haunted." "But, Bob, what will the squire say? He has been most kind." "Tain't his general way, then." "But lam a relation. Come, my friend, they will expect you in the loft or in the stable." "There's hay in the loft, Rudd said, but I likes this better." Alec felt a little annoyed. "I'm going to bed, so good-night. However, , perhaps its thanks to you that I am here at all. Look here, Bob, I repent of my promise ; the squire will be only too glad to frighten the fellows who--" Bob's face shewed well-marked consternation. "But you promised." "I have never yet broken a promise, so sleep easy, Bob, but release me as soon as you can. If ' your friends make themselves scarce in the country, why then—" "They ain't scarce yet, sir. Good-night; I'm to be up early, and I don't think that Rudd will hurry hisself. There's mole visitors than you in the house. Her you seen Miss Ruby? She ain't like the squire." "No, Bob, but pray retire, for I am weary." Alec slept the sleep of the weary. When he awoke it was already light, but Bob had disappeared, and the events of yesterday appeared to Alec to be almost impossible. He half fancied that he had dreamt them all, and that he was only now awaking. In due time Rudd brought him hot water, and Alec hurried to dress. When ready, he went downstairs, and looking down into the central ball, he saw a blazing fire. The logs were burning on the hearth, and a girl was kneeling down to warm herself. She turned suddenly, and then Alec was astonished to see that he was looking at a girl best described by the word "charming.' He said to himself that the Cousin had not chosen badly, and that it was rather stupid to come to Yewdale mgrely in the part of fairy godfather. "Papa!" she exclaimed, and then,she saw that she was speaking to a stranger, and she jumped up and smiled with every feature of her face. "You are my cousin. I have heard that you came last night. It is cold this morning, and I was trying to make the fire burn. We never have had company before!" As Alec shook her hand he thought that only to see her was worth his journey. She appeared to him like a maiden in an enchanted castle, and yet she looked perfectly happy. Her surroundings were very feminine, and Alec, who had great ideas about a girl's education, was set wondering, as he said simply: "Your father asked me to come and see him. I hope that you will not mind making the acquaint- ance of a new cousin." "Mind! lam so glad you have come. I want to hear so many things, and papa always says I am too young to know the world. Do you think that seventeen is too young ? " "It is the right age for everybody," answered Alec, laughing. "Where do you live, and have you seen London, and have you any sisters ? " "You have asked me three questions in one breath." Ruby laughed. "It is because I have so much to say, and when papa comes he will tell me that lam still a child, and must not ask questions." "Surely youth is tiie time to ask questions." "But you have not answered one of them." "My history is simple. lam your cousin, my name is Alec Beauworth, and I am a poor architect in a London office. Your father asked me to come and make his acquaintance, and here I am. I lost my way last night, and so I arrived rather late. That is all." Ruby looked up and gave Alec a searGhing glance. Evidently her inspection was satisfactory, for she smiled, and Ruby's smile was a thing to remember. "I am glad you are my cousin. One may talk as much as one likes to cousins, mayn't one?" "Yes, one may tell a cousin everything." "Papa said that I must not talk to strange men. But a cousin isn't a strange man." "Certainly not." "Papa once said that I had no relations, and now this week he has told me of two cousins." "Two! I think one would be enough!" Ruby laughed again. "You mean yourself. But tell me everything else I asked." "I have no brothers or sisters; I am all alone in the world." "Poor Alec. One does call cousins by their names, doesn't one ? " "Always." "Well, Cousin Alec, I am glad that vow are a , cousin. I want to ask you so many things. Do I you think that I am a woman ? " "Certainly—a charming woman." "Well, not quite a woman yet, perhaps; but just on the way. I wish I were a man, and then I should go out into the world. When I was a child in Australia, I went about in the country. I had a mother, I can remember it well, but papa does not like me to talk about it; here I am always kept in with Nancy. If I go to the village, she has to come too. Do you think I may come with you alone ? " "With a cousin one can do anything, and—" Alec stopped suddenly. His conscience pricked him a little. Evidently this lovely child was kept tinder lock and key. She was destined to marry a ! man she had never seen and— A wave of pity filled his mind; but had he any right to offer her forbidden fruit? At that moment he heard steps, and in another instant the squire entered. He seemed a little annoyed at finding his guest down before himself. Ruby went up to him and kissed him. Alec saw that at all events the squire loved his child. "0 papa, here is one of the two cousins pop spoke of. He has been telling me he lives in London. I think we shall like him, shan't we,</p>
        <p>papa?" "I am sorry that you again find me too late, but I was out; there are so many things that require attention on the property. You will forgive me?" "You never told me what my cousins would be like, papa," exclaimed Ruby. "I am so glad that I have some; we so seldom see anyone here that— why, only last night I was thinking that it was like being a prisoner.' The squire gave a quick glance at his daughter, but though Alec saw it, the girl was quite uncon- scious that she had said anything to displease her father. "Your second cousin will be coming in later, and we must make the gentlemen feel welcome. Come, Ruby, how shall we amuse them ? " "I don't know what does amuse grown-up men," said Ruby, earnestly. "Now let us have breakfast. Papa does not always care to breakfast with me." The breakfast was laid in the large, panelled dining-room. On the walls were hung ancient Beauworths, and the furniture seemed to belong to those who had for many years found rest in the silent grave. ' "Look, Cousin Alec—papa, isn't Alec a pretty name ?—here are all my grandmothers and grand- fathers. Here is a lady who must have been pretty. Papa once said that she was like me, but I don't see any likeness, do you ? " "You can never be like an old picture," Alec answered, looking at Ruby with surprise and wonder. He said to himself he had never seen anything so fresh and original as Ruby Beauworth. "I think the old pictures want a good cleaning," he added, addressing the squire. Sir Harry looked up at the robust ancestors. "I have never touched them since I entered the house." "Indeed! How long is it since you first took possession? I have forgotten." "It is ten years since Ruby entered these walls. She was a tiny child." "But I remember our journey, father, across th sea, and I remember--" "Nothing else, I am sure," added the squire, smiling at his daughter. The girl looked up with a puzzled expression on her face. "The old and the new get so much mixed up and then I Bee so few people that—" "Don't chatter so much, Ruby, you will tire your cousin. Ah, I see more visitors coming up the rath." "And do you see Bob, papa? Nancy says that he has come to help poor old Rudd. I like Bob." "His father declared that Bob would not come, but the boy sings to another tune." Alec was interested in this remark, as he knew why it was that Bob had changed his mind. "Foolish lad," he said to himself, "he thinks I am in danger of my former enemies. At all events, here I shall be safe from drunken marauders. The squire would not stan4 any nonsense; but I wish he had not promised his daughter's hand in this old-fashioned way. The girl is too young to be--" At this moment Alec's attention was drawn to the entrance of two men. The foremost was a middle-aged man, whose calling it would have been difficult to guess, the second was younger. Father 1 and son, thought Alec. Oddly enough, the eyes</p>
        <p>or the two young men met in one quick, searching look, as if each were anxious to fathom the other. On Alec's side it was curiosity to see what the man was like who was to be Ruby's husband. He saw before him a smooth-faced man, about thirty years of age. He was certainly not plain, but his lips were so firmly set, and his small eyes looked so hard and inscrutable that Alec at once took an unreasoning dislike to him. "Does he think lam a rival? But that is not possible: be has the squire's promise, and that, I think, should satisfy him. What a difference between Ruby and her father!" But the squire was speaking. "Come, Ruby, this is your Australian cousin to whom you must give a warm welcome. Do you remember him ? No, that is impossible." Ruby went up to the two men and shook hands. "Are you both cousins ? " she asked, with her Irresistible smile. "Yes, both at your service," said the elder man. "Yes, both cousins," added her father. "James Felton and his father, Cousin Tom Felton. Now let me introduce Mr. Alec Beauworth to our Australian cousins." The two strangers gave but the slightest acknow- ledgment to this introduction. The conversation became general, but Alec noticed that James Felton, in a vulgar off-hand manner, paid great at- tention to Ruby, and that he tried in every way to ingratiate himself with her. Ruby was not shy and so inexperienced that she talked freely to them, enjoying the unusual attentions showered on her. The longer Alec sat there, the more he felt indignant that this child should be given away without power of personal choice to a man of this sort. A man who had seen too much life, who bad no illusions left, and who, in Alec's eyes, was not even worthy to touch Ruby's hand, and who dared to adopt on the strength of cousinship a too familiar tone and manner with her. "Mr. Beauworth has only consented to give us a week of his company," said the squire. "Ruby must show her English cousin how we welcome rare visitors in this country place." "Indeed I will, papa. Cousin Alec must first see the house and all the old curiosities in the north wing." "We must all make your week a pleasant remembrance," murmured the elder Felton. Suddenly Alec had a strange feeling, that the voice was familiar to him. Where had heard it before? In vain he searched his memory. He must be mistaken. "Thank you, I am most grateful," he answered. "Take your cousin to see over the house, whilst we go to the stable and find Bob," said Sir Harry. "Come, Cousin Alec, you shall see my rooms first," said Ruby, and Alec followed her, suddenly feeling more than sorry that he was, and must always be, only her Cousin Alec. The other three men walked towards the stable. "A week is not long," remarked Torn Felton, as they sauntered towards the stable. "Ruby must make the most of the time. You must keep out of the way, James, to-day." "I'm not jealous! You know that my cousin Ruby is too good for me." He laughed un- pleasantly. "But she is a means to an end." The squire's face expressed annoyance. "You promised that the child should not suffer,* he said, in a low voice. "You know that I am very fond of her." "We all know that," said James, ironically. "Your only daughter and all that sort of thing. She must get round Beauworth, we can do the rest." "James was not easy to bring, I assure you," remarked Felton. "The ladies of the other hemisphere have great attractions for him, especially-" "It is a pity you did not stay there," said the squire, impatiently. "Don't lose your temper, Sir Harry, you know we wanted you. An empty pocket recalls old friends." As they entered the stables an eager pair of eyes was watching them. "Bob!" called out the squire, but there was no answer. "I can send Bob about with him and he can act as guide. He is Rabbitts's son, and will ask no questions. The trio stood a minute by the door. "You undertake the rest, said the squire, with some hesitation, "and pray remember that we are in England." "England or Australia. But this is a more desolate place than you could find on the other side." "Get his signature and do your part, then leave us to do ours. Hulloa 1 there is the boy," said James. Bob came stupidly out of the stables. "Why didn't you come when you were called?' said Sir Harry, roughly. "I was in the loft, sir." The squire glanced at the loft door above his head, which was open and then at Bob's face. He was satisfied. The boy was slow-witted and had heard nothing. "Get yourself tidy to take the gentleman out this morning. He wants to be shewn the way about the woods, but mind, boy, that you are not to take him beyond the Birch Wood. We shall be coming to join him by-and bye." "Yes, sir," said Bob, sullenly. "Come, no shirking of work, or your fatheii shall hear of it." Bob was silent, but as the trio sauntered away his face lost its dull expression, and he grinned with silent pleasure. "I'll ask no questions," hi said, "no, I shan't."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHAPTER VI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VI.</p>
        <p>"So you have never been from this place, Ruby?" said Alec, as they stood side by side in the sitting. room, and looked out over the dull expanse of the park, which had, for so many years, bounded the girl's vision. "Never since we came here, and the past seems so unreal that I have almost forgotten it."</p>
        <p>"But you can go into the village?" Ruby shook her head. "Nancy is always with me. Sometimes Ido wish she were younger. Cousin Alec, you will often come and see us. It is so nice to have you to talk to."</p>
        <p>"But you will not always stay here, Ruby. Some day you will have a home of your own." "I should not like to leave papa. But I do wish he would let me go out alone or take me to London. Come and look at the passage that leads down to the rooms that are shut up. It is odd that Nancy is not here. I wish you and I could just go out that way now. It would be so delightful. Ido want somebody to explore with me."</p>
        <p>Alec caught her mood. She was like a lonely child who has longed for a companion and has found one. .</p>
        <p>---- ----- "This door is locked," he said.</p>
        <p>"No, here is the key, I saw Nancy put it there. Do you know that I think she must go out some- times when I am asleep." Ruby laughed, and then Alec turned the key. Only narrow, bad.ly-lighted stairs met their view. "Let us conic down, there are only some big rooms below which papa shut up when we came." "But will your father object to our voyage of discovery? You must bring a a candle." "Oh, papa will not mind. This is fun." They opened the door and the cold draught down the stairs made the candle flicker.</p>
        <p>"Nancy goes down sometimes and says it is to air the rooms, but once I saw her, no, I heard her, talking to a man! I did not tell papa, he would have been angry. Poor Nancy finds me dull, I expect."</p>
        <p>Alec went down the stairs followed by Ruby. Ile would have done anything she asked him. It was almost like visiting the Princess of the fairy tale who has been asleep for a hundred years. Ile felt like the Prince who had come to awaken her. At the foot of the stairs they found themselves in a wide passage. Opposite to them was a door leading into the garden. _</p>
        <p>"Cone to the -rooms first," said Ruby. "Papa said I was to shew you the house. Here is a room which used to be a library." She tried the door "Why, it is open," she exclaimed. The shutters had been taken down and a fire lighted. "How strange! It must have been opened for our Australian cousins. Look, Cousin Alec, at all these old books. Don't you think that our old Beauworth relations must have been very fond of reading ? " Alec mentally lingered on the word "our," then he looked at the damp, dull room, which felt as if it had not seen the light of day for many years. The leather was pealing off the old chairs, and there was a layer of thick dust on the pictures. "It must have been a nice room once," he said, taking note of some valuable prints which were quite ruined from neglect. It seems a pity that these rooms are shut up." "Here is a photograph on the table," remarked Ruby. "What a nice face, but how sari. Look, Cousin Alec, it is a man rather like you, with just your curly heir."</p>
        <p>Alec saw the likeness to himself and smiled. • "Let us come on to the other rooms uow, before father comes back."</p>
        <p>Before replacing the photograph on the table Alec looked at the back. On it was written, in faded ink, "Harry Bea.uworth."</p>
        <p>"This is some relation," he said. "Let me see why, it must be your father before he went out to</p>
        <p>Atistralia."</p>
        <p>-------- "How funny that I should not know him. He has got so grey now." "Time is not a flattering artist," laughed Alec.</p>
        <p>"This next was a billiard-room. There are more pictures on the wall. Oh, look, that is the same face as the photograph, only younger. I don't think it looks like papa." "An older generation, most likely. So this room is also opened." "Yes, it is strange, for I have never seen these shutters opened before. What a pretty lady on the wall."</p>
        <p>"It is very like you, Cousin Ruby," said Alec. "Did you ever hear of the fairy no distributed gifts to the ladies of various nations? To the English she gave the AurOra Borealis, with which to paint their cheeks and their lips." "Mamma was pretty. I remember I fused to say so to her. She liked it, though she always said: 'Silly child, I afn too sad to be pretty.'" "In Australia?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, papa was not there; I can't remember him till mamma died; then one day he came and took me away. Mamma used to tell me about him, and say that he would come back; but he never did till she died. She told me if he came back, to love him very much, and I always have. He is so good to me I could not help it; but if he were cross, I should love him for her sake."</p>
        <p>This innocent talk interested Alec, who knew nothing of the family history except that the uncle of the last baronet had been lost sight of, and that he had been very delicate as a youth. "And you canierover with your father. Did you like the journey ? " "I was fretting for my mother so much that it took a long time for me to feel happy."</p>
        <p>"And nCo'w you are happy?" 4 "Very happy, happy, except that I should like to see the world, and papa does not like me to go any- where; but he knows best, I suppose. Look at this old cupboard; I never noticed it before. Can you open it ? " Alec tried, but failed; then he exclaimed: "This is a sliding panel. I have seen one before. Indeed, I think that this very cupboard is mentioned in one of my mother's old letters, which I shall read with more attention now that I have been here. When she was a child, she used to stay here." "I wish I had known your mother," said Ruby, looking at Alec. Her eyes had a soft, beseeching look in them which went straight to his heart. He began to feel that this girl was already promised against her will—and yet that was certainly not his business.</p>
        <p>The two between them opened the panel. The dust of years covered everything within ; but the contents, chiefly packets of old letters, did not look inviting. Ruby took one down and blew away the dust as she read: "Letters from dear Harry on his voyage out to Australia!" "How amusing! That must be papa's letters to his mother. He told me she died soon after he landed in Australia. He cannot remember her very well." Alec shut back the sliding door. He thought that perhaps he had better not explore further without leave.</p>
        <p>"Let us go back to your rooms now, Cousin Ruby." "Very well, but I must shew you the door I want to use ; sometimes, when Nancy is dozing, I want to run out. Oh, I wish you were going to live here."</p>
        <p>"But I daresay your other cousins will stay sometime? " said Alec.</p>
        <p>"You are going to shoot this morning ? " said Ruby, suddenly. "Yes, but why should you not come later and onet me? We could explore the woods together.' "Here is papa; I will ask him." (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
