      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NEW MAYOR FOR WARRINGTON. ELECTION OF ALDERMAN ROBERTS.</head>
        <p>THE NEW MAYOR FOR WARRINGTON. ELECTION OF ALDERMAN ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>More than usual interest was taken in the elec- tion of a Mayor for the Borough of Warrington held in the Town Hall on Thursday last week. From what had previously transpired it was conti hint-Ay anti- cipaed tha., we were going r.o have a Liberal Mayor this year, and there were many desirous of being present at the function which was to decide this important matter. It was therefore not surprising to find the portion of the Council Chamber alloted to the public, inconveniently crow led. Amongta those present were Mrs Henry Roberts, Mrs John Yonge, Mrs Robert Garnett', the Rev John Yonge, Rev i T Reed, Messrs Ztba Armitage, John White, T Birchall, Robert Garnett,R G Henshall, James Rep- herd, W D Jeans, sen.,eind W D Jeans, jun. Promptly at twelve o'clock noon Alderman Wright occupied the Mayoral chair for the last time during his term of office. There were also present: Aldermen Fair- dough, Sutton, Roberts, Vrancomb, Hutchinson, Bolton, Councillors Shaw Green, Smethurst, Ben- nett, John i‘vans, Ellison, Duncan, Burton, James Evans Baird, John Crosfield, William Pierpoint, A J Jolley, Tinnion, L Oreening, R Roberts, Pea- cock, A Jolley, Rogers John Roberts, Lee, W Monks,li Heaton,with "Lyon Whittle (town clerk), S Wilson (deputy town clerk),J Fairnurst (borough treasurer), Dr Gornall (medical officer of health), I` Longdin (borough surveyor), J Deas (water and sanitary engineer), L Talbot (Chief Constable), G F P White (deputy magistrates' clerk), WLi Wood- cock (assistant overseer), J Sutcliaa (assistant , borough surveyor.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ELHCTION C•F MLYOR,</head>
        <p>ELHCTION C•F MLYOR,</p>
        <p>Cauncillor PIERPANT said tlaat as that was the first opportunity he had nad of thanking the Mayor for the onerous duties he had performed as Mayor of that borough, he should like to take the opp )rtunity of doing so, not only as a member of the Chamber, but as a rate- payer of the borough. He thouvsht he was perfectly correct in saying that the Mayor had carried out the duties of his high office with credit to himself and to the satisfaction aud benefit of his fellow townsalen. As a Councillor he must personally thank him for the unvarying courtesy and kindness with which his Worship had always treated him, and he thanked him most sincerely for having done so. It might be rather a wonder to some people why ho should occupy the office of proposer of the Mayor for the ensuing year. I!, must be within the memory of all gentlemen present that the party he had had the honour of belonging to for a great number of years was in a minority, and it was because of the generous conduct of the C onservative party that he was enabled that day to propose a successor to Alder- man Wright. (Applause,) Awl he thought the election would be unanimous. He hoped the Liberal party in Warrington would never forget the conduct of the Tory party, and that they would go on from now in a mbre amicable maaner,than they badperhaps done in the past. It had been recently very truly said that the Lib iral 'ion .had lain down with the Conservative lamb. (Laughter.) He thought he would reverse that. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) He hoped at any rate that their sleep would go on undisturbed for a great mmy years, and he was sure the people of Warriugton would have no cause to regret it. There was a curious coinei fence to which he should like to allude; and it was that 50 years ago that very day James Piarpoirit was elected the third Mayor of Warrington and he(the speaker)felt an especial pride, being his only lineal uescendant in that chamber, in calling attention to the fact that he occupied the chair, as Alderman Wright had done, with a great deal of dignity and with usefulness to his fellow townsmen. (Applaue.) He hoped they would pardon him that slight di -ression and the pardonable pride which had prompted it. (Hear. hear.) With regard to the props 1 which it was his pleasure to submit, any words on his part were entirely unnecessary to paint the abilities and virtues of the genteman whom he had the honour to propose as Mayor. They knew that in Alderman Roberts—(applause)—they had a gentleman who (luting the long time he had been a member of the Council had paid unremitting afteation to the duties of all the com- mittees on which he had est% and he had no hesitation in saying that as Mayor elf Warringen he would p- rform the duties of his high office wire faithfulness, sincerity and assiduity, and would bring to bear upon all he did that high business ability which he had ne need to re- mind that assembly he posseEsed in a pre-lminent argree. He would also be ready at all limes to extend to every- one that courtesy which Mayors of Warrington had always teen proud to exercise. (Hear, hear.) He had great pleasure in proposing the election of Alderman Roberts as Mayor for the ensuing year and until the acceptance of office by his_suoce.sor. (Applamte.)</p>
        <p>Alderman Surrorr seconded. Ele said.that perhaps no two members of the Council had been so much at vari- ance or had indulged in so meth wordy warfare as Alderman Rolserts and he—(laughter)—but some how or another alter those battles had taken place they seemed to have settled down and thought no more about the matter. (Hear, hear.) If they asked for the reasoia ef that, the only one he could give was that it was in a great measure owing to his (Alderman Sutton's) usual generosity and forgiving dhoosition. (Loud laughter.) At all events he bad great pleasure in second- ing the resoltrion so ably proposed be Councillor Pier- point, if for nothing else in order to keep him (Alder- man Roberts) quiet—(laughter)--and compel him to be- have himself towards him (Alderman Sutton) and the rest of her Majesty's subjects who represented the rate- payers in that chamber. (Laughter.) Putting personal questions aside, he must sae that Alderman Roberts we.uld, in his opinion, make a very good Mayor. (Ap- plause.) He was possested ot abilities of no mean order, and those added to his experience, and the vial and de- termination he always brought to bear on all matters of importance connected with the Council fully qualified him to be head of a Corporation whose work was con- stantly increasing and becoming more important every year, and consequently demanded more attention, time and trouble, not only from the Mayor, but from every member of the Corporation. (Hear, hear.) Us had pleasure in seconding the proposition. (Applause) _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT said it was his priYile-go to &amp;nand the proposal of Alderman Roberta for the Mayoralty at the caucus meetingetn I he should now like to say a few words. It would be superfluous to speak of Alderman Roberts's clam to the high position of Mayor, but he could not refrain_ from alluding to his eminent basine is capacity, his eloquence—which he thought WAN unequalled in the Council, if indeed it was approached ; his industry—having fee many years hen most active on many committees and a member of the Council for 14 years, and finally his pronounced and transparent local patriotism. (Applause). They all claimed to be proud of their town, but of all th,,, members he did not think there was one who had a mere profound regard for the town or a more pronounced local patriotism. (Rear, hear.) Many people thought that local patriotism was a small thing when compared with the larger patriotism of the country and empire, bat he thought one quite as good and necessary as the other. They all particularly honoured Alderman Roberts for the way in which he had always put forward the claims of Warrington and for the acknowledgment of the pride and pleasure it had given him to offer those ser- vices. (Hear, hear.) The Mayoralty was the highest civic honour. It was no mean honour to be the leading citizen in a town of the size and importance of Warring. ton. He was sure Alderman Roberts woule prove equal to the task before him, and among the illustrious men who had preceded him he did not think there was one who had performed his duties with greater sincerity and ability than Alderman Roberts .would exercise. (Hear. hear) There was even a stronger reason for ceogratu- lation than a purely personal one. The election ot Alderman Roberts marked the dawn of a hotter feeling, among the members. The abominable politiea,aB Cow,- cillor Croafield said at a recent gathering, had been eli- minated, and he was sure it would prove of advantole to the town and welfare of the whole community. (Ae- plause.) Councillor ChOSFIELD said he thought the friendship which had existed between Alderman Roberts and him- self dated bank farther than between any two members of the Council. He had watched Alderman Robert's career with interest, and always admired 113 s sterling uprightness, and the energy he put into all the week he undertook. He felt confident that he would make an excellent Mayor, and he telt certain also i•harOike Alder- man Wright, he would be impartial. (Applause ) He thought it was a great requisite for a chief magistrate of any borough not to show favour either on one side or the other. (Hear, hear.) He lei iced that day that they were all 'minim ous in the election, and he thanked the Conservative party for their courtesy and kindneiss in sinking those ahosninable politics—(laughteri—and joining heartily in electing his friend Alderman Roberts as Mayor. (Applauss) Aldelman 'WRIGHT heartily congratulated Alderman Roberts. He said he was perfectly satisfied that all that was said of him would he carried out. He had been most loyal to him (Alderman Wright), and he should be equally loyal to him. (Hear, hear.) He honed that every member of the Council would extend to him the evmpatl-y and kindness which the Mayer at times requir d. He thanked t"em all for the kindness they had extended to hiir.arid hoped they would act similarly towards Alderman Roberts. (Applauie). The reantution was then ptV to the meeting, and carried with apphitme. The newls-elected slays), (accempanied by his pro- poser and secsnder, as well as hv Alderman Wright, Alderman Fairclotteh, and the sword and mace bearer,) then left t he room, and when he retu-n- d to the Council Chambtr, wearing his robe and chain of office, he was heartily cheered.' Having signel the declarati m accepting the ,ffice of Mayor until such time as a sucriessor is appointel, the Mayor said he had been in some tight corners in his time hut he confessed hr neer had grater difficulty than he had that morning in finding words to ade- quately exoress his feelings. 'rho kindness which had prompted the gentlemen who had spoken that day and</p>
        <p>he generous recognition of services which had been far too lavishly spoken of by men who were, under other circum tances, opoosea to hitn, m ide the difficulty all the ereaosr. He c mid assure th-qn taet to be Mayor of this town ws.s to his mind the greatest honour and highest dignity to which any citizen could aspire. He appreciated fully the honour they had heite wed upon hisn and at the seam time he appreciated quite as fully not oaly the difficulties and responsibilities but the lahour which would be entailed during the year on which he had entered that day. Le would be quite impossible for him , to be able t3do justice to the plea ion if he did not feel that he was entitled to and should receive the loral and , undivided support of every member of that Corpora- tion. It was not his intention to take up their time more than a few minutes but be might fitly refer to the fact that perhaps it was the most important year in the history of their Borough. (Hear. hear.) They had to deal with a vast expenditure proposed to be incurred,as provided in the Act of Parliament obtained in toe last session, and which, he was advised by the Town Clerk, was the most important and largest measure granted during the last session to any community, and als3 to other expenditure bound to be incurred and which he believed would result in the moral and general improve- ment of the weole of the commanity. (Hear, bear.) He found that under the Bill they were authorised to spend close amen £300,000. This sum miget be very easily divided into two classes, the income producing section, and that which while net directly pro ducing income was calculated in its effect to be of immediate benefit to the community. In the income producing section referred to there were the waterwerks extensions at Win wick and Appleton. They had power to spend something like £150,000. He thought the most adverse critic would admit that they had not got it a moment too soon. When he reminded them that during the last year they were within twelve tionrii of being short of a supply for the iequirements of the Borough, he thought all would aeree that they had not set their hands to that work a minute earlier than necessary. (Hear, hear.) In the income producing section they were ab.ci authorized to spend a further small sum for river wharves. Outside the Act. the Corporation was incurring an expenditure of £45,000 in the provision of electric light—s much needed requirement. and one far too long deferred for a growing and prosperous town like theirs. (Hear, hear.) He hoped to see commenced during his year of office or at least provision made for electric tramways. (Hear, hear.) That would mean an expenditure of something like £40,000 more. They seemed very large items and they were very large, and required the serious and care- ful attention of the committee&amp; having the control of that expenditure, but he had no doubt that if these sums were judiciously expended, everyone of them would tend to promote the further prosperity of the town. (Hear, hear.) Then there was other necessary expenditure, part of which had already been incurred. Tnere were new police eourts at a cost of £25,000; the purchase of Victoria Park, £lO,OOO, and which he typed to see very shortly open for the public use ; £14,000 for hoopital extensions and further exteusiens of Longford Depot, two items they could not possibly avohl, made necessary by the growth of the p Ipu- latine of the town. Then there was anotner subject which was very dear to his heart, —the provision of suitable premises for the pur- poses of techical instruction. (Hear, hear.) There was aliio the improvement of Slutcher's-lane, the continuei improvement and widening of Bridge-treet—(hear, hear,)—which be hoped would be successfully pressed forward during the year, and the erection :if a central derot. These totted up together made the large sum ' of £362,000. This by no means completed the pro- giamme which they had entered upon and which they required at once or at least in the course of a few years from now. He sincerely desired Warrington to he made a County Borough. (Hear. bear.) Of course he was aware that he now spoke with a greater sense of responsibility but still he had the same intense feeling of dissatisfaction with the treatment meted out to them by the County Council with regard to Technical In- struction and he dreaded the possibility of Warrington coming under their control in regard to secondary edu cation with which the present GJV, rarnent were 'nos likely to deal, he limped in a satisfactory manner. (Hear hear.) He trusted it was not too much to hope tha they might see before long a new bridge across th Mersey at Bridge Foot and that they might hay Knutsford-road widened to the approach of th bridge ; also that they might have a new bridg to Victoria Park from Hewley, in order that it might be come easy at approach from the south eastern portion of the town. Then there was another mattdr, a small matter in itself, but a very desirable one, and one which was mentioned to him by Alderman Francomb. It was quite time that .Warringtoa was provided with Turkieh baths. (Hear, hear) Ha sincerely hoped it might be included in their programme before long. In regard to the health of the town their physical situation made the ideal that some of them would like to see in connec- ti eit with sanitary matters almost impossible. The fact that Warrington lay in the valley of the Mersey, that they were a manufacturing town,and other conditions inseparable from it made it extremely difficult to keep down the death-rate to a point whore they should like to see it. There was still a necessity for con- tinued attention in the rudimentary knowledge of the laws of public health. Additional hospital accommoda- tion was being provided. They required the adoption of modern science in all their sanitary arrangements and the vigilant assistance of their officials in seeing that the laws of public health were observed. These were bound to produce a lower death rate and that was a most important and necessary and desirable thing to aim at. He bad simply epitomised a few of the impertant matters which appeared to his mind with the object of asking them for their . co-operation. He would like to say how much he re- gretted that illness had kept away twe or three of their older members that day. He thought they might send to them their sincere sympathy in their absence and tee cause of it. (Hear, hear.) Permit him to say in con- clusion that an administrative authority, such as was the Corporation of this ancient borough, to which was entreated in a large and ever increasing measure the comferts and conveniences, the education and social en- vironment, the health of the body and mind, and in innumerable instances possibly the very lives of their citizen., was an as4embly with which any man who desired to he of service to his fellows might be juatly proud to belong. To be a conscientious and capable member of this Council ought to be, and undoubtedly was not only a position of great responsibility hut an office of equal honour and dignity. Let them then with a full sense of the gravity of their responsibilities bring to bear upon them such time as they could devote, and all the ability of which they were capable, and lot them at the same time be fully determined that neither prejudice nor passion, personal interest,or any other unworthy motive should intervene, but that, with a single eye to the progress and pros- perity of theientire community,they would do all in their power during the year which bad now begun to make their town and its people more and more worthy of the traditions which they had inherited.and of the immense possibilities of which even t'se Immediate future seemed fully pregnant. (Applause)</p>
        <p>THE DEPUTY MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR then app)ity.ed Alderman Wright to be deputy mayor. Alderman WRIGHT said he thanked the Mayor for the honour done him in applinting him his deputy. He should like to mention a matter he forgot a few minutes ago and that was to express his hearty thanks to the people of Warrington for their generous reaponse to the Transvaal fund. (Hear, hoar ) At that moment they had at least £l.lOO contributed. (Applnure It was nit necesaary for him to say more. He should have great pleasure in assisting the Mayor in every possible way he could. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
