      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MAYOR AT CHURCH.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR AT CHURCH.</p>
        <p>LARGE CONGREGATIONS AT WYCLIFFE AND THE PARISH CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>In place of the customary visit of the Mayor and Corporation to the Paris Church, on what is now known as " Mayor's Sunday," this year Wycliffe Congregational Church was visited in the moraing, and the Parish Church in the evening. The innovation had been brought about by Alder- man Roberts, the newly-elected Mayor, who, being a member of Wycliffe Church, gave precedence to the church to which he is attached. The attend- ance at both the morning and evening parades exceeded anticipations, and never before in the history of the borough has "Mayor's Sunday "been so generally recognised by townsmen of all creeds and religious opinions. The weather was favour- able for such a ceremonial visit, and the procession as it proceeded along the streets was wit- nes ied by a large number of spectators. Excellent arrangements had been made by the Chief Constable. who marshalled the procession. At the head was the band of the Volunteers under the conductorship of Mr. Thomas Armstrong. followed by a muster of Volunteers under the com- mand of Major and Adjutant Roome. They ,were succeeded by members of the Police Force in charge of, Chief Inspector Phillips. Then followed a number of firemen headed by Superintendent Turner, and the St. John ambulance brigade which was accompanied by Captain Waring and Mr. Hulse. Sergeants Chantler and Dean—the mace and sword bearers, immediately preceding the Mayor—pre- sented quite a picturesque appearance,and attracted a good deal of attention. The Mayor in his robes and chain of office was accompanied by the Town Clerk on his right and his chaplain (Rev. J. Yonge) on his left. Then followed three honorary freemen of the Borough—Alderman James Fairclough,Coun- cillor John, Crosgeld and Mr. F. Monks They were succeeded by the following members and officials of the Corporation :—Aldermen Wright (deputy Mayor), Sutton, Hutchinson, Councillors Baird, Bennett, Burton, Duncan, James Evans, John 14v ens, Ellison, Heaton, Shaw Green, A Jolley, A J Jolley, Lee, Monks, Pierpoint, Plump- ton, Peacock, J Roberts, F R Roberts, Rogers, Shill- cock, Smethurst ; official., Mr Stanley Wilson (de- puty town clerk), Mr John Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr W H Woodcock (assistant tr -asurer and collector), Mr T Longdin (borough surveyor), Mr J Sutcliffe (assistant borough surveyor), Mr G F F White (deputy magistrates' clerk), Dr J H Gornall (medical officer of health), Dr Reginald Smith (police surgeon), Mr Madeley (curator, librarian, &amp;e.), Mr Taylor (secretary of the Gas Department), Mr W S Haddock (gas engineer), Mr W S ho ward (cashier), Mr Dees (water engineer. &amp;c.), Mr Robert Young (cemetery registrar), Mr E Place (market inspector) Mr WII Wallington (assistant), Mr R Norman (chief nuisance inspec- tor, &amp;c.,) Mr Jones (-school attendance inspector). The magistrates present were : Messrs. H P Fair- clough, T Birchall, Ziba Armitage, William Owen, .3- W Hayward, and S Wright. Representing the Board of Guardians there were Mr A J Jolley (chairman). Mrs Clay, and Messrs. T H Ashcroft, S Davies, T Halton, W Hickman, William Houghton. George Pilling, Mr 8 Sutton (assistant clerk), and Mr A Wrigan (superintendent of the Padgate Schools). There was also a large attendance of local gentlemen including Messrs. A II Crosfleld, Arthur Browne, J Longland, T Birtles. William Thompson,Joseph Harrison, R 0 Henshall, William Roberts,H roft.W Brough,R Wyllie,George Walling ton, Joseph Podmore, Thomas Hawthorn, PT Win- skill, William Beswick, K H Pearson, H M Ashton, E England, J W Bouchier, B Farrar, J Haviour, E V Walker, D Fanshaw, C II Bickford, J Clayton, W Hayes, Crampton, J Titchett, Gandy, Haley, George Clay, A II Dunn, J.P. Jones. J Critchley, W Mounfield.N Legge,Lewis, and Captain Savage. The service at Wycliffe Church was commenced with the singing of the hymn " MI people that on earth do dwell." It was heartily sung to the accompaniment of the band by the large congre- gation which completely filled the building. The band also played the accompaniment to the hymn "Forward be our Watchword." The choir under the conductorship of Mr W H Payton Mus. Bee. very ably led the singing, and gave a creditable rendering of the difficult anthem by Dr J F Bridge" Blessed be the Lord." At the close of the service the "National Anthem" was heartily sung. The first verse was renderedlbv Mrs. Pearson, and Miss F Booth sang as a solo the altered second verse by the Dean of Rochester.</p>
        <p>SERMON BY THE REV. J. YONGB.</p>
        <p>The Rey. J. YONGE took for his text the wards " The powers that be are ordained of God," Romans xiii., part of the Ist verso.—ln the course of his sermon he said he ventured to think that the subieet was one that mani- festly befitted the occasion. What was the real mean- ing of this service ?" They were observing a custom that was time-honoured ; and it was one which, speak- ing for himself and for most, if not all, who were pre- sent, he trus'ed would never fell into desusg. This annual vi.it of public authorities, in their official capaci- ties, to the House of God was designed as a suitable [Walla recognition of the religious sacredness of pubtc office. The custom was of value just in proportion as this design of it could b kept Lk view. For the precious and invaluable quiet of the weekly day of rfe, to be broken by the parade and excitement of a civic procession needed the sanction of a high and religious motive. That motive undoubtedly existed. It was a distinctly goad thing for tho leaders and officers of a community itself as a community to be publicity re- minded of DEPENDENCE UPON, AND RESPONSIBILITY TO GOD.</p>
        <p>It was good for all who were called to p.culiar service and prominent position, to have special occasion pro- vided for considering the higher aspects of their calling, and for realizing the fact that in serving the community if their service be righteous and true, they were still more really fulfilling the purposes of the King of Kings. No doubt there were individuals who deprecated, and even disapproved such an occasion as this. They spoke of it as a vain show, a pompous parade, even an or- ganized hypocrisy. Possibly it might easily become all this. Just as all Church attendance,even of private per- sons, might become a " vain show " if it be mado occasion for unseemly and ostentatious parades or rivalries of personal attire, and just as the individual observance of religious rites might in any ease become a contineal hypocrisy. There was, however no need for this to be so. And it was not consistent with Christian charity to think or assert that it was so. What needed more general apprehension was the fact that to all human relations, all positions and offices and duties, whether personal or social, there was a Divine side, which was necessarily the supr(me side. The intelligent and devout recognition of God was the salt that preserved the sanctity of all life,and every relation ship. Consevevtly, it was of great value that he wis- was elected to be chief magistrate of his town, president of its corporate consultations, recognized head of its affairs, and the councillors wha were his colleagues in the control of its interests, should tee&amp; such an occasion as this in which to look at the diviner side of their duty. And the occasion became yet more impressive when magistrates and guardians of the poor, when volunteers and police, and members of fire and ambulance brigades', with other public persons. came rniz,ther into the house of worship to acknowledge their relation to God, and to remember that in their public capacities they were riot only ministers and ser- vants to men, but the ministers and servants of God. Perhaps they would allow him at this point to make re- ference to a SPECIAL FEATURE OF THIS PRESENT OCCASION</p>
        <p>which called for At least a pass:n..7 4'-rd. The custom- ary visit of the Corporation to the house of God on what was known aa Mayor's Sunday," was made that day not to the Parish Church as had ben usual, but to a Nonconformist Church. Their new Mayor had in- vited tivlat to accompany him to the Church</p>
        <p>of his own birth and inheritance, in connection ' with which he had risen up into manhood, and to which he felt himself intelligently and ardently attached. [heir presence there was a token that they approved of his invitation, and whatever their own ecclesiastical preferences might be,they deemed it a fitling thing that the Mayor should thus specially honour the Church to which he belonged. Toe worshippers in this church offered them respectful welcome. They rejoiced to see the day when such a service as this within, these walls was not only legally permissible (which before 1867 it was not), but also that it was sanctioned by the general approval. They at Wycliffe appreciated Cie Christian courtesy of their proseaco, and would reciprocite it to the best of their aoility. Might the acre of still wider tolerance, still larger charity, still deeper sympathy. between men of differing creeds and churches be hastened by such incidents as these! Let them, then, call to mind the fact that public service of the com- munity was a high trust,</p>
      </div>
