      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HIGH STEWARDSHIP.</head>
        <p>A HIGH STEWARDSHIP.</p>
        <p>" It is required of stewards that a man be found faithful." He could not he acting rightly who sought or held such position for purely personal ends. Not to make a name for himoelf, uot to augment his personal influence, not to secure any private advantage, but in and by his position to serve his fellows, and in serving them, to serve his God ; such should be the supreme inspiring mutive of the public servant. That especially should be the inspiration of municipal service. That service was unquestionably one of the most valu- able lines of public usefulness. What was the great pur- pose of the municipal corporation ? Was it not to dili- gently conserve the general welfare of the whole com- munity? To minister to peace and order, to promote health and prevent disease, to regulate the social con- ditions of the people, te develop their comfort,and make mere possible their progress,to care for them in all their best material, external, and eveu intellectual interests, to provide increasing means for aeneral improvement of individual' and; social life? Indeed, it became, from the constant and healthy progress of legislation, increasingly difficult to diseern the limitations of municipal service and enterprese, for they touched the life of the people at every point ! Outside the domain of the spiritual, municipal authority could act in every direction that its material resources, its intellectual capacity, its public spirit, and its humane devotion could cover. All this was a great and sacred trust ! Few lines of service were conceivable —beside those of the surgeon or physician who sought to mitigate human suffering or disease, or of the Christian minister who sought to deal with the spiritual welfare of his fellows—few lines of service besides these could be named in which a true man could be of greater or wider usefulness thee in municipal life. They must all agree that one of the most hopeful features of our nation at the close of this 19:la century was that presented by the</p>
        <p>GREaLT DEVELOPMENT OF MUNICIPAL ENTERPRISE. In all parts of the country, corporations were vieing with one auother in seeking to promote mor, fully the public good. The condition of our towns and cities to- day might still be far from what they should like to see. But what an incalculable advance was their present state upon what prevailed not only when the century began,but even when the century was half way through! Legislation had almost worked miracles! and the in- telligent fidelity with wlich town councils, in many cases, had employed the powers put in their bands was exceedingly praiseworthy. Still, even yet, neither here nor anywhere. bad musicipal authorities been able to give perfect or adequate action of the ancient maxim, " the health of the people, the supreme law " : health not only physical, but mental and moral. The Mayor well said in his inaugural address on Tnursday that there were necessary educational developments in this town which should receive early attention, and that there were important and essential sanitary improvements that were waiting to be made. Sometimes his (the preacher's) duty took him into parts of this town— courts and alleys and slums—which called loudly for transformation and which offered a very</p>
        <p>URGENT FIELD FOR SANITARY REFORM. That deadly enemy fever, which had recently been so busy in our midst, laying many on beds of languishing, and even sending not a few to premature graves, needed grappling with and quelling. Typhoid, the doctors told us, was a preventable disease, which ought not to be allowed to exist, and if so, did it not become a supremely urgent duty that its causes should be asc.ntained, and the means for its prevention be used ? They had reason to be grateful for the assiduous labours of their competen-, officials in the past,but might they not hope for even greater successes in the early future ?</p>
        <p>"THE HEALTH OF THE PEOPLE IS THE SUPREME LAW," supreme over the interests of individuals; supreme even, might be say, over the pockets of the ratepayers, supremely to be sought, supremely to be served, by those who had entrusted to them the care of the town. In making these references to municipal work,he trusted he did not overstep the limits of his present duty. He was utterly unfamiliar with the official function of the Mayor's chaplain, and never before had he had similar warrantable opportunity of speech on those matters to thEir civic rulers. But he was the continual preacher of a gospel which was the geapel for humanity, and he remembered that the great Master of them all busied Himself with man's temporal conditions as well as his spiritual, and sought to im- prove them. Was not Christ the great inspirer of true citizenship? and was it not the Chr;stian spirit of in- telligent humanity and true philanthropy that should be the inspiration of municipal life ? He suggested, there- fore, whether, among other duties and privileges, it did not belong to the province of a Town Council to put all preventab.e sources of public and private evil and sin as much out of the way of the people as possible, to make it less and less easy for men to get either physically or morally wrong, and on the other hand to maka it more and more possible for people, even in the humblest, poorest walks of life, to live cleanly, healthy, sober, intelligent, and harpy lives? Such it see.rneet to him was the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>TRULY SACRED SERVICE</head>
        <p>TRULY SACRED SERVICE</p>
        <p>assigned by Divine Providence to the" powers that be" in such communities as theirs. Must they not believe that that mission was likely t 3 be more faithfully accom- plished if they who were called to these offices remem- bered their ordination and appointment of God It was that ordination which was the supreme sanction of public service. " Ministers of God," St. Paul called the civil officers. The use of that high title showed that God's miuitters were not alone those who might be members of an ecclesiastically sacred order, but might also be those who were members of a service which, mistakenly and shortsightedly, men called " secular." What man holding office thus as servant of his fellows, especially anyone who was called to be a leader in such service, would not feel the uplift insp,ration of the sense that he was a minister of God? The whole purpose of this present service would he gained if that great, inspiring truth could be fastened on the mind, as" a nail in a sure place by the Master of assemblies." "The powers that be are ordained by god." The chief magistrate and all his brother jtistices. and their valuable coadjutors and servants, the officers of police, were eervants of God, in beivg servants of the people : to Him they were sup- remely responsible, in Him should their trust be placed; to Him their obedience given. The "guardian of the poor" had often both a difficult and thankless office, but he should nover forget, amid his proper care for the interests of those who elected him, that the title of his office was the definition of its supreme duty, and that he had a sacred responsibility to care for the poor and the helpless, even though many of them had shown no true csre for themselves. Their military volunteers, the members of their fire and ambulance brieadee,might add trueldignity to their invaluable lines of public service—a service which might sometimes entail exposure to danger, suffering, or even death—by regarding it as a sosrvice to which God had called them, and in which, therefore, they became his ministers. Surely this was the sopect of life and duty that possessed the noblest of all inspira- tions ! Might God help them to get it! Then sheuld they be gable, all of them, whether powers municipal or powers religious and spiritual, powers corporate or powers individual, to combine for the trneet welfare of their town. It should be the desire and aim of every- one to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>SHARE IN THE PROGRESS AND DEVELOPMENT,</head>
        <p>SHARE IN THE PROGRESS AND DEVELOPMENT,</p>
        <p>in all that was highrst, and best, of the community to which he belonged. Recent years had done much for it. Coming years mu,t do more. Along with withr streets and better modes of transit, with cleaner dwellings and purer atmospheres, along with growing populations and extending education and advancing trade Eh Mid come a purer habit of life, a purer language than was some- times heard. finer courtesies and richer kindliness ; our people should b. uplifted to s nobler character. Homes and haunts and habits shoold be reformed upon more comely and Chriatian lines, and they should pray and work ro tiring in the time when, as of Jerusalem in Vie Holy Volume. it should be said of Warrington," War- rinEtou shall be called a city rf truth "; " Behold I create Warrington a rEjoiciag and her people a joy." This should be the him and aspiration of all citiznts,and they should loyally and devoutly conspire to bring it about.</p>
        <p>A coll.,linn taken on behalf of the Mayor's fund realised £2B 104 01. At the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>There was an evening procession to the P4rish Church. when the Mayor was again accompanied by large num- bers of his fellow townfqiien. The church was crowded, and although many vies had been reserved for those in the process:on, some of the volunteers, firemen,police and ambulance brigale emit.] not be accommodated. The proneqaion waq met at the church do,)rs by the Rector and the Rev. E. .T. Willcocks.and the members of the choir. Mr. W. S. Woods Pro;iclqd at the mon. The anthem "Let every soul be subject to tho higher pleeers" was efficiently sung by the choir, the solo being taken by Mr. Parkinson.</p>
        <p>4. * THE NAM *fri,</p>
        <p>geniality, for his readiness to oblige wherever possible, for his courtesy to everyone, for his willingness to under- take all kinds of work, and to the Mayoress for her sweetness and dignity, which had captivated them all. He also wished to pass a tribute of praise to the new Mayor. They knew his ability,they knew his coaracter, they expected great things of him,and they thanked God for his first act—the ren)enitien M God,the acknowledg- ment of what was due to the great Divine bAng by his preseece in the House of God. They felt grateful to those who had to fill such important duties that they should be the very first to set an examplu of the recognition of God in all things. He wished on behalf of their clergy and officers to express his appreciation of the pres-nce of so many distinguished and prominent people, especially the pre- sence of his respected friend the chief minister of the great Congregational body, who had been appointed by the Mayor to be his chaplain during his term of office. He deeply valued his presonee and the presence of so many who ordinarily worshipped elsewhere,but who had wished to do honour to the Mayor and to the occasion by coming to the House of God. Now let him speak for a minute upon the great work whic'l was before them. As he read the papers he saw the vast expenditure of meney to be wisely and carefully hid out. Be knew that the Mayor felt that he had committed to his care, aided by those who would work with hino, the health. the prosperity, the happiness and the good conduct of the town, and he felt the responsibility which had been laid upon him, and teat he would seek from God that help which would alone enable him to perform his duty. He wished to speak plainly for that was what God called upon Amos to do. There were evils and he wished to speak of thr.e. Firs', of all, and that which was,perbapa, not in their power to remedy, namely, THE POLLUTION OF THE RIVER.</p>
        <p>!OVE</p>
        <p>It was a very serious matter. They all recognised the immense importance of fresh air, and when in summer ttry were obliged to go to rest with their windows closed lest this foul atmosphere should deprive them of sleep, as it did—it was not simply a matter that was unpleasant and disagreeable,—but injurious 0 health. They felt that they must do their utmost to get the nuisance abated. Next he would speak of</p>
        <p>SMOKE. He knew that he should be told by many that smoke meant work, but if smoke was necessary to work let it be far greater than it was, but he did not believe that in all cases it was necessary. He thought it might be abated, and he would earnestly ask those who held important and prominent positions, and who had the care of large works, to thmk when they went away to their pleasant residences, in the country of the smoke which fell upon their houses in the town, and invaded their rooms. It was a most terrible nuisance, but if it was necessary for men to work let it cpntinue. The third and last great evil he would speak upon was that of DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>He would ask them Co listen to these statistics. There was much to be thankful for. Ten years ago the popu- lation of Warrington was 55,000, arid there were in that year 800 convictions. This year. when the population was 63.000, there were but, 400 convictions—less by half, and a matter for treat thankfu'ness. Yet there was an inerease of 100 upon the previous yeer-400 as against 300—and though he knew it would be said the population had increased, and that some people had bean convicted two or three times, he did not think it was eroditable that there should be an increase of 100. The population was said to be 63,000 and there were 212 licenses, or one for every 300. Now, many impartial and competent judges said the proportion should be one license to every thousand people. He would earneety commend that to them. He could speak very impar- tially upon this question. He did not believe in total abstinence being pressed upon the people, though he be- lieved it was good for many. For many peers be had hardly touched drink himself, and be never felt. better ; still when he thought of their Lord's example, by giving intoxicating drink in large quantities for enjoyment—and this was the opinion of auch men as Dean F trrar,Larchbishop Trench and Bishop Wordsworth, learned men and great scho!ars—be dare not say it was wiong, to make use of thorn. It was the abuse that was wrong, and what they bad to do was to lessen the temptation. He would earnestly commend to those in authority the importance of seeing that the licenses were not increased but; decreased, and that the proportion should not be one to every 300 but every thousand. He had now touched upon these three primes nuisances—the terrible smell of toe river,the all-pervad- ing smoke, so depressing to them all, and the terrible sin of drunkenness—drinking to ex- cess, lowering themselves to the level of beasts. It was the duty of everyone to try to practice great abstinence, so as to set an example to others. People were watching them and it was not only their words and actiona,but their example which was of the greatest value to all.</p>
        <p>THE WAR</p>
        <p>Proceeding to allude to the heal campeign relief fund and to the war in e4outh Africa and its results the Rector said he was sure that the Government had a clear eonscience in the matter, believink it to be a just and necessary war, sad and evil as it was, but he could contemplate it with a clear conscience. He also spoke of the marvellous patrietiqn the war bad called forth, of the loyalty of Englishmen and of those living in the colooies, the splendid courage which had been displayed by our soldiers and of the wonderful magnanimity shown by the General in taking all the blame upon himself in connection with the me- morable reverse. As a reeult of the war, the Canon said we should have a good Government in place of a bad and corrupt one, and increased trade and commerce throughout the world. The people would have noticed the change in foreign opinion owing to the words of weight uttered by the Prime Minister. Peace was, in- deed, a thing to be longed for, but very often to speak bravely and firmly and to act firmly brought peace, where weakness might bring war. The celVction realised £3O 21. on behalf of the Mayor's fund. A verse of the National Anthem was sung at the conclusion of the service, the Volunteer Band playing the accompaniment. The Mayor's Thanks. On returning to the Council Cbamber, the Mayor thanked all who had taken part in the proceedings of the day. The attendance had far exceeded any expecta- tions that he could possibly have formed, and he felt most grateful to everyone who had aesis'ed in the inter- estin, and he hoped, useful functions of th; morning and evening. On behalf of himself and wife he thanked them most sincerely hr their generous assistance that day.</p>
        <p>SOMETHING FOR THE CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>When children meet at birthday and other pa:ties,the hostess is naturally anxious to provide the twist suitable and acceptable "fare. Ch.ldren do not require heavy meat food,yet they need varietv,and the mother would do • well to selore—amosiv, other ,ppetising things—a supply of Chiyers'G-Dld M dat Jellies. These excellent Jellies are refrshing and cooling and also nouriahing Chivers' Gold Medal Jellies are flavoured with ripe Fruit Juices, expressed by the firm them el vy s from the freli fruit. Their sale and popularity are ever in- creasing. It is merit tat does it all, the merit th-it takes pains to do the best. Children like Chivers' Jellies—there is no doubt about that. Put it to the riot,. where-they have been used and the" Ayes " have it unanimously. The Medical Press, after careful analysis, Faye : "Chivers' Jellies are to be commended for the "delie,scy of their flavour and for their absolute purity " The Woman's Signal gives this tei,timony : " Chivers' " Jellies can b. confidently recommended, they are Der- " fectly pure, absolutely clear and pleasant to lock at "on the table, and the flavour is simply perfect." The Christian World says "In these village inclus,ries " there is no room for deception. Yu must turn out a " genuine article if you would defy competition, and "t-hi.s is why the firm in question are now at the top of " the tree." , Sold by Grocers and Stcr-s in P&amp;ckets—Half-pints 21c1., Pints 40., Qtr,..es 81, A free sample will be sent 'on receipt of no,'-csrd S Chivers and Sons, Histon, Cambridge. Please mention this paper.</p>
