      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>AMBULANCE.</head>
        <p>AMBULANCE.</p>
        <p>Competition for the Crosfield Challenge Shield.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the Warrington Gym- nasium on Saturday afternoon when a competition for the Crosfield Challenge Shield and prizes presented by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, took place between teams in number 4 district of tha St. John Ambulance Brigade. Amongst those present were the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs.Reberts),Coun- eillor and Mrs. John Crosfield, and Captain Waring. Teams from Warrington.Rochdale, Oldham, Preston,and Nelson competed, the judge being Dr. Molloy, of Black- poo', who was accompanied by Deputy Commissioner Lieut.-Colonel Trimble at Preston and Mr. L.Whittaker, of Accrington, Superintendent of No. 4 north-western district. The men manipulated the splint.; and bandages in a manner whicn did them great credit, and the judge bad a difficult matter in adjudicating. The shield was awarded to the Preston squad who obtained eighty-nine points, Oldham being second with seventy-two Points and Warrington third with seventy-one points. Each member of the Preston and Oldham squads received a cicck and the Warrington men were the recipients of a pair of carvers. The shield and prizes were presented to the winners by Mrs. Crosfield. The Maeon proposed a vote of thanks to Dr. Molloy for judging and te Mr. L. Whittaker for assisting him in the arrangementa. Be was sure that while in one respect they were sorry that the shield had not remained in town they thought the decision was perfectly right and that the best men had won it. They hoped the Preston men would be able to keep it till Warrington were good enough to take it from them. Be had pleasure in moving the resolution. Councillor Caoseteen seconded. Like his friend the Mayer, he was sorry that Warrington were not first. They most hope that in the future Warrington might come to the top. (Hear, hear.) They must not be dis- couraged, but work until they succeeded. There was no doubt, whatever, that the Ambulance Association was of great value to the county and to the country, and it deserved the support of every mar. and woman in the town. (Applaune.) He had very great pleasure in seconding the vote ef thanks. The resolution vats heartily carried. Dr. MOLLOY in respAlding said that the whole of the men had given a most creditable performance, and there was net a single man who was not thoroughly efficient in ambulance work.</p>
        <p>Mr. WHITTAKER also re;ponded. Lieutenant-Colonel TRIMBLE proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Croefield for distributing the prizes. He said that the family and firm of Crosfield had given all the assistance and encouragement they could possibly give to the ambulance movement. (Ap- plaueo.) Th.. 57 owed to them the shield and prizes pre- sented to the winning teams. It showed that they took a great iuterest in the work in which they were all en- gaged. As Deputy Commissioner for the district be thought he was voicing the sentiments of his staff when he said that they were very much indebted to the Cros- field family and firm. The ambulance brigade was sup- plying men for active service in the Transvaal. Twenty men and three officers would proceed to London on Mon- day and on Thursday would sail for the Transvaal on the ship known as the" Princess of Wales "the Red Cross hospital ship going out to the war. (Applause.) It was the first time the Brigade had been called upon to pro- ceed to active service and as one interested in the work he had every confidence that the men going to the front would be a credit to the Brigade and themselves. That demonstrated very fereiblv that ambulance organisa- tions were rapidly coming to the front and it further demonstrated that the Government of the country looked upon them for assistance when a time might arrive to demand it. He was positive that whatever demands the Government might make on the Brigade they would always find willing, capable and ready workers to go forward. They were quite prepared at , any time. _ _</p>
        <p>t'aptain WARING seconded. The resolution was carried with acclamation. Councillor CROSFIELD responded briefly on behalf of Mrs. Crosfield and the proceedings terminated. DESCRIVPION OF THE SHIELD.</p>
        <p>The shield is of v?ry maabive design, being composed of pollard oak. In the front, at the top, in solid silver, is a ribbonion which are the words "St. John Ambulance Brigad,...." In the centre, under this ribbon, is the solid silver badge of the associAtion, three inches in diameter, comprising the ribbon, the Maltese cross, the lion, and unicorn interspersed between the points of the cross, while immediately below, in sawpierced silver, is the scroll with the words " Crosfield's Challenge In the centre of the shield and underneath tbs. scroll is the cross of Jerusalem. a sacred and beautiful plaque re- preeenting the Good Samaritan relieving the man who had fain among thieves and had been stripped of his raiment, wounded, and left half dead. It is beauti- fully represented, and embossed in silver, measuring from point to pint eight inches by six. There is also a. very fine solid silver shield with the inscription, "Challenge Shield presented by Messrs. Joseph Orosfield , and Sons Limited, for competition in No. 4 North- Western District." Around the border are ten small shields, also in silver—and there is room for ethers , should they be required—which will contain the names of the winning teams. On the reverse side is &amp;silver plate bearing the inFcription, "Designed and executed by Messrs. W. Woods ond Son (Alfred Woods)." The shield is to be held by the winning team for twelve montimand it is to be delivered to the csntral brigade in time for the ctmpettion every Tear.</p>
        <p>Latchford Improvement Society.</p>
        <p>At the last weekly meeting of the Latchford Im- provement Society held in the Temperance Hall, Councillor Ellison read a paper upon some as- pects and abuses in church life. He spoke strongly upon the" facts and fancies of the Americans,' which the English were imitating in having social entertainments in which musical evenings, church parlour pastimes, theatrical representations, ice cream suppers, and dancing in the most modern style were introduced, and by which the simplicity of the Gospel of Christ was destroyed. The essayist dealt with the responsibility laid upon ministers of the Gospel in allowing so much frivolity and foolish- nes; among their congregations. Once upon a time ministers of the Gospel were venerated as men of God, and remarkable for piety and good works in pastoral oversight,caring for the young men and women in the best ways of religion, but now there was the easy familiarity of everyday life. Mr. Drewitt pleaded for common sense in dealing with things pertaining to entertainments and amuse- ments, and the Catholic Church had a right to cater in a proper way for the people. Mr. Walter Hill spoke forcibly upon the practical part of life, and urged Christians to act as the great Exemplar did. Mr. Arther Ford thought the cardinal doctrines of religion ought to be most inculcated in order to touch the consciences and hearts of men. Mr. Henry Bleaere ass-rted t hat religion was a solemn thing and needed to be understood in a sensible-manner. The Pre-ident (Ur. Ford) did not like the American style a; described by the essayist. With many churches and ministers there was a sort of latitudin- arianism which was not useful or profitable in real r-ligion. No moral compripmises ought to be allowed with the truth, and no pandering with deleterinue things which were questionable. No right-minded man would object to recreation of a healthy kind, but the danger was in the opposite way. Heal religion would never be advanced by worldly and unchristian ambitions.</p>
      </div>
