        <p>IT is important just now that Some Wise Restraints. speakers and writers should im-</p>
        <p>_ pose upon themselves a little more restraint than usual. A readiness to believe every rumour to the detriment of our political and military antagonists is almost as bad as a deliber- ate intention to influence the public mind by angry arguments and personal attacks. The public mind is already sufficiently excited by the incidents of the war, disjointed as many of them a-e, and by the apprehensions of an undeveloped military situation. Movements take place which we can- not understand, unverified impressions are offered to us as facts, aed situations are discussed which may never arise. We have borne ourselves bravely in disaster, and we ought to be able to conduct ourselves with restraint under whatever is likely to follow. It is important we should admit that difference of opinion from us does not justify the accusation of want of pataiotism. Patriotism is seldom in complete agreement with the prevailing mood of the time, and it is still more rarely to be found the exclusive product of ono political creed. The man who is "the friend of every country but his own "is the fiction of controversy. He does not exist. The exactions of the more in- temperate partisans are not to be regarded as the standard of right and wrong. If the Opposition were in power, and conducting the present war, there would be angry protests against the idea that all critics were traitors and that all opponents were unpatriotic. The behaviour of the clamour- ous is often most offensive to the quiet politician, whose opinion is twice as valuable, because it is more carefully formed, and it more frequently turns out to be correct. It would be a misfortune if we were to gemerally regard shrieks and hysterics as the signs of the true patriot, and it would be a calamity if we could not venture to express doubts, balance facts, and preserve a sober and an analyti- cal attitude without being called a Boer and boo- ted as a traitor. The situation is not one to be improved by despotism:of this kind. There never was a war in which it was possible to take so keen 'an interest and to hold so diverse opinions. It is an invasion, and it must be resisted. It may be an attempt at domination, and it must be dealt with as if it were. But every Boer is not a monster because he is making war against a British colony, and every politician is not a fool because he does not agree eye to eye with every proposition or statement made by a supportqr of the present Government. We must be fair all round. When incidents Occur that are favourable to us or to the Boers let us give them the same weight. We have to remember that our kinsmen at the Cape will have to live with many of the Boers when the war is over, that the Afrikanders have power as well as ourselves, and that, ably as the present Ministers may conduct the war, it was a Liberal War Minister who gave us our present army system, and that the country, as a whole, will be called upon to pay the cost of it. Several Union- ist speakers have been eloquent over the warm aupport given to us by the colonies. It is self- government which has made them free to do these noble acts, and self-government is always a healer of discords. We cannot wisely use one self- governed colony to totally suppress the customary liberties of another; and if we praise the loyalty of our colonies it is bad form to question the patriotism of people at home who think, it may be erroneously, that war should have been rendered improbable. Without wise restraints there will come a stupendous reaction.</p>
        <p>Mr. ARTHUR SHERWELL, who The Consumption has devoted much time and of Alcohol. care to the study of methods of dealing with the drink question, notifies the depressing fact that "the consumption of alcohol is far greater now than when the temperance movement beg4n." If he were not a master of social statistics, we should unhesitatingly say that such a statement was an exaggeration. It cer- tainly looks like it. But he arrives at it by a searching examination statistics, and they ought to be accurate enough forsome general statement.</p>
        <p>The statement is not equivalent, however, to say- ing that drunkenness is en the increase, or that the demoralisation caused by excessive drinking is on the increase also. If it were, there would be some reason for consternation, and for a review of all our methods and proposals. What he main- tains is that the increased consumption of alcoholic drinks makes greater inroads on the incomes of the wage-earning classes. He puts this consumption amongst the working classes at "six shillings per week per family, including teetotal families." That is a very large sum. It means that rent and drink take almost two-thirds of the income of a man who is earning El a week, and two-fifths of the income of the man earning 303. a week. Obvious:y, such an expenditure means that the money left for good food and clothes and incidental expenses is very small indeed. The problem is how to reduce this large expenditure or hew to abolish it alto- gether. The quality of the drink consumed is not</p>
        <p>now in question. It is lighter than it used to be it contains less alcohol, and there is less general intoxication in every class, from the wine-drinkers downwards. That is a gain in some ways. There are countries in which the consumption of drink is quite as large as it is in Great Britain, and one or two:in which it is probably greater. But they show considerably less drunkenness, and there is less social demoralisation, less poverty. The rents are lower, the household arrangements are better, and the amount of pauperism through drink is unques- tionably smaller. Mr. Sherwell has hardly made sufficient allowance for these outside considera- tions. They indicate facts of almost as much moment as the consumption of alcohol. How is it that the cooking and domestic arrangements of the working-class household, speaking generally, are not vastly better than they are? Defective education and lack of domestic training and spirit must he assigned as causes. But if foreign work- men spent one-third or one-fifth of their incomes in drink, would they be any better off than our workmen are? Probably not. Drink is cheaper and it is less intoxicating, in many countries than it is in our own. Sitting in public-houses to soak, without any other kind of amusement than is to be got out of the company present, is less frequent in other countries. But if the temperance move- ment is making so little real headway, there must be some defects in its methods. What are they? The enlistment of the young is excellent, and the movement in connection with the churches and chapels is healthy. The inducements to save increase every year. Moderation is the example set by all the educated classes. The fallacy that drink is food, however, is as prevalent as ever amongst the less educated, and this is one of the causes of increased alcoholic consumption. Until it has been destroyed there will not be much hope for the growth of abstinence. There have been grave doubts as to the methods of our politica reformers, and Mr. SHERWELL 23 himself respon- sible for some of them, as witness his facts as to the failure of prohibition in the towns. It seems to us that the whole subject must hi. 3 re-examined and re-argued, and that this is the only way of meeting the increased consumption of alcohol, and curbing the political power of its makers and vendors.</p>
        <p>The Government is calling atteeton to one of the great benefits to mankind which the Twen— tieth Century will herald in. air minds have been stirred with visions of the noLile deeds that will be done in those days, and to crown all the President of the Board of Agriculture, the gentle- man who guards our fields and pastures. hastens to give us a New Year message. Premature it may seem, but it is one that, on account of its importance, he could no longer keep to himself. The Sale of Food and Drugs Act will come into force on January Ist, and from that time it will be next to impossible for any unscrupulous parson to sell the innocent margarine as butter.. Our legislators nobly wrestled with this subject during the dog days of the present year, in order that we might start the new. century in a becoming manner, and the fresh Act of Parliament is believed to be an able measure. Not, of course, that it condemns mar- garine which is a nutritious substance in its way.. What it does strike at is the mixtures that tend to fraud. The novel feature of the Act is, "You shall not make margarine too good"—for this is the substance of the restriction as to the quantity of butter fat that may be included in the composi- tion. The theory is that if the essential qualities of the two kinds of foods are kept distinct decep- tion will be avoided. That rule in favour of clear distinctiots needs to be affirmed more than ever in trade. • The way in which philanthropy, for example, is being engineered nowadays as a means of advertisement and increase of business is dis- creditable to those concerned, and threatens to strike at the root of charity as properly under- stood. Several firms now are offering to devote ten per cent. of their profits out of all purchases. of certain articles to the funds for the Reservists' families. It is to be hoped that the committees will refuse to be exploited in this way.</p>
        <p>In the days of the French Monarchy the decease of one King and the accession of his successor generally called forth the popular cry "Le roi et snort; vive le roi"—a concise but comprehensive expression of loyal sentiment. Perhaps we may be allowed to paraphrase it and say: Le mire est naort ; vive le mire, for Alderman J. E. • Wright's term of office expired last Thursday, and that of his successor, Alderman Henry Roberts, com- menced amid expressions of the utmost approval and loyalty. Alderman Wright has carried out the duties of his office during the past two years in an eminently fitting and praiseworthy manner. He has been a popular Mayor, which carries more with it than the mere phrase sug,gests, and he has been—almost above everything—an im- partial Mayor. Impartiality in the hands of a man raised to the Chief Magistracy of a borough is nothing short of a virtue. Were he to lean ever so little either one way or the other eualcss trouble would ensue, and the dignity of his office would be seriously imperilled. Mr. Wright has recognised this fact fully, and he has striven hard and successfully to maintain the balance evenly and fairly. The task is by no means an easy one, even to a man of high principle and courage, for we all of us have our failings and most of us possess an inclination to lean this way or that in regard to matters of importance. Mayors have their inclinations and failings like other people, but instead of yielding to them they have to suppress the one and rectify the other. Mr. Wright has had these difficulties to face, and he I,as faced them boldly, and with an amount •of success which his fellow-men are willing to testify to.</p>
        <p>In Alderman Roberts, the town has, to use a hackneyed phrase, got "the right man in the right place." He is, perhaps, young for the honour, but then Mr. Roberts has been young for most of the honours he has obtained, and the circumstance cannot in any sense be termed a fault. It is rather an advantage in this particular instance, for Alderman Roberts has had till the experience necessary to qualify him for the post of Mayor, and we are convinced that he will fill it conscientiously courageously, anb well. There was an uncommonly :are,e3 gathering of the general public at last Thurs- day's ceremony and great interest was taken in the honouring of a man who has been before them since—and a little before—the attainment of his majority. The services he has rendered to the t )wn and to his party need no re-capitulation hero, but we may recall the fact that Alderman Roberts has received a special vote of thanks from the Council for one portion of them,—an honour in itself to be proud of. The year upon which Mr. Roberts enters as Mayor is likely to be a memor- able one in the history of the Borough for schemes involving the possible expenditure of £360,000 are in embryo and some of them will undoubtedly be proceeded with hefo-e the municipal elections come round again. Perhaps the most important of all— not in a financial sense—is the County Borough question. This is a matter in which the new Mayor takes deep interest and it is evident from his" address from the throne " that he will leave no stone unturned in order that Warrington may take the rank it ought to do arreeng the towns and cities of England. The task before alderman Roberts is undoubtedly a severe o.le hut no one could be better fitted to cope with it than he is. We heartily wish him tee health and strength he will so urgently require.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Ratepayers' Association, or at least one O its members, has again been in evid- ence. At the Local Government Brd inquiry held in the Town Hall, last Wednesday week, into the application of the Corporation for sanction to borrow certain SIMIS for purposes of electric light- ing and the provision of a central depot, Mr. S.</p>
      </div>
