      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>Gough took occasion to show his want of confid- ence in the enterprise of the Corporation by op- posing the electric lighLing scheme. The opposition at its best was of a half-hearted, peurile kind and it is very improbable that it will have any weight with the Inspector ot the Local Govern ioent Board But it just serves to show what a narrow-minded and timorous class of individuals would have entered the Council Chamber this mcmth had the nominees of the Ratepayers' Association been successful at the poll. With them low rates are the one ideal to be'striven for,and progress un- thought of unless it" pays" in £s. d. Warring- ton is suffeling from mai-administration of this kind in the past, and we welc,-otne the new era of municipal enterprise. We do not agree with Mr. 'Gough's gloomy forebodings that the new electrical works will not pay. Of all the schemes which the Corporation have at present on hand, that of supplying electricity promises to turn out the best investment for the ratepayers. Smaller municipalities than Warrington have had the courage to provide electrical installations and they have been rewarded by handsome profits. There is every reason to believe that Warrington will be equally successful.</p>
        <p>Colonel Ford, the Chief Inspector of Explosives, attaches blame to no one for the terrible explosion at St. Helens in May last, and there must be some comfort in this, for the United Alkali Company at least. The disaster seems to have been entirely due to an explosion of potassium chlorate, which, never having exploded before, was not regarded as particularly dangerous. The explosive properties of it under certain conditions have now been demon- strated, not only by careful scientific investiga- tion, but in the unpleasant manner that St.Helens people experienced six months ago. Potassium chlorate has lost its good name and will in future be regarded with suspicion by all manufacturers who have to make use of it. That precautions will have to be redoubled goes without saying, and the probability is that most manufacturers will take the greatest possible care without waiting for legislation. One warning of the St. Helens type is enough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB</p>
        <p>Presentation to _Mr. E. Pickavance</p>
        <p>Jun.</p>
        <p>A smoking concert in connection with the Warrington Cycling Club was held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Monday evening for the purpose of making a presenta- tion to Mr. E. Pickavance. junior, one of the secretaries. Mr. " Pa " Davies, one of the vice-presidents, presided in the absence of the President (Councillor Pierpoint). There was a large attendance, amongst those present being Mr. A. E. Wright, the hon. secretary of the Liver- pool Centre of the National Cyclists' Union. An excel- lent musical programme was provided by several of the members whose c mtributions were well received. Songs were rendered by Messrs A Fieldhouse, W H Ireland, E Whitehouse, W Whittle, J Holmes, G E Clay and J Emson ; Mr A Clay recited, and the quartette party con- sisting of Messrs Scattergood, Davenport, Whitehouse, and Holmes, gave several selections in good style. The CHAIRMAN made the presentation on behalf of the members, the present taking the form of a solid silver smoker's combinaticn,con,ikting of acigar case,cizarette case,card case and match box,sach bearing an enamelled centre shield of the Warrington Cycling Club badge, 'which is a reproduction of the borough arms in correct colours. They were in a morocco c,se with a silver plate on the outside bearing the following inscription " Presented to E. Pickavance, jun., by the members of the Warrington Cycling Club in recognition of his valu- able and gratuitous s.rvices to the club. November 1199." The articles were designed and supplied by Messrs. Eustane.e and Co. Shonly before the intnval, Mr. G. H. SMITH, the captain of the club, said they were pleased to see Mr. Wright, and they would alwlys give him a very hearty welcome. He had to leave before the time appeinted to make the presentation, and,there- fore he would at once call upon him. Mr. A. E. WRIGHT said he was rather surprised at the remarks that had fallen from the lips of Mr. Smith. He bad not come to the concert to enjoy himself, but for the express purpose of seeing the beautiful token of respect presented to his esteemed friend Mr. Pickay4nce. bear.) It was very pleasing to find that the work done by Mr. Pickavance in connection with eveling should receive the appreciation it did. There was no body of men who appreciated the work of Mr. Pickavance in connection with the Roads' Committee more fully than the National Cyclists' Union. They knew what he had gone through with the Committee in order to improve the roads, and he was not at all surprised that they should snake him a preseW ation. Another item of the pre- Igramme having been gone through, Mr. G. H. SMITH said that the presentation had been finbscribed for by the members of the club as a token of respect in recegnition of Mr. Pickavance's valuable ser- vices. He had great pleasure in calling upon the Ch,ir- Man to naake the presentation. The CHAIRMAN said after the remarks of Mr. Wright, and Mr. Smith there was hardly anything lett for him to nay, for they had said everything. He had been associa• ted with the club during the whole of his (the speaker's) membership, and he was at a loss what te say that *would convey his kindly feelings towards Mr Pickavance. Be had great pleasure, on their behalf, in offering Mr. rickavance the magnificent present they saw before them and he hopod that in years to corn° he would think of the many friends that cycling bad brought him. (Applause.) Mr. PiCKAVANCE who was cordially received said the reception he had met with that evening quite over- whelmed him and words had departed from him. He valued the remarks spoken in his favour even more than the present in one respect. From their remarks it was evident that he had a very large number of friends in Warrington, and h. hoped that their friendship Would never he severed. He thanked them most heartily. Ho did not expect such a thing as a present. He had be- longed to the Cycling Club for about thirteen rears, end during that "small "term he had made a large number of pod friends who he honed would stick to him—(bear, hear)—and who would back him up as long es he couk? ride a bicycle. He might say that he had only done his— duty (hear.hear)—and now they had a stronger club than ever. He hod that as long as he was able they would allow him t perform his present service) to the benefi: of the club. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN ORCHESTRAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN ORCHESTRAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>-- It is to be regretted that there was such a poor audience at Brunswick School, on Friday night. to hear the first concert of the season, given under the auspices of the Runcorn Orchestral Society. Once more the players demonstrated their ability, and proved, beyond doubt, that they only required the steady assistance of the music loving public to en- sure complete success. There were no fewer than ferty-five instrumentalists, and these performed on almost every description of musical instru- ments. The vocal artiste, Miss 0. Clare, is a lady well known in Runcorn musical circles, and therefore does no'3 require any introduction. It is enough to say she fully sustained her already high musical reputatioreher fine soprano voice being in its best tone. The overture " Merry Wives of Windsor "is, owing to its chromatic construction, a most difficult piece, and indeed can only he per- formed by the most expert musician. The full powers of the orchestra were tested to their utmost but the balance and tune were excellent, and they fully deserved the burst of applause which followed the magnificent closing bars. In " Butterflies." Miss Clara delighted the ears of the audience by the fine expression which she imparted to the piece,and subsequently heightened the impreeeion in the negro melody" Be ole Unbrella " (Conley Grain). Though almogt pitched in a monotone and afford- ing but little scope for her full powers she imparted to it just the necessary vivacity and secured a well .merited recall. The dreamy weltz " Hydropaten " and " Chaconne " were beautifully and sympathe- tically rendered. The climax, however, to the first part of the programme was found in Gonned's thrilling March "Romaine." In this selection the orchestra reached the high water mark of success and the enthusiastic demands for an encore fully testified to the merit of the performance. Tile second half commenced with the standard overture Pirche Mignon " (Langley) which was highly, appreciated. The best item of the evening was per- haps Mr. M. Kruver's violin solo entitled Beethoren'e " Romance in G." His execution was perfect and the soul of the music was most faithfully interpre- preted, needless to tay he was vociferously recalled. ' The Children's Home," was next rendered by "dies 0. Clare, and it was made apparent to all that she had correctly caught the spirit of the piece. The closing item Sullivan's " Gondoliers " was a splen- did finish to a high-class concert. The able con- ductorship of Mr. H. W. 14andley, was a feature of the proceedings. Mr William Cooke, A,R.C.e., accompanied with his usual ability.</p>
        <p>THE GLEN-SPEY AND '4I74ATHNIILL DI STILLERIEq Situated in the finest Whiskt,producing district of Scotland are the property of W. aro A. Gilbey, and the Whisky made from Horn, -grown Barley only. Glen-Spey," 3s. 6d Ind —Strathrnill." 3s. GI sod by W. and A. GILBEY gents in every town.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATS ItS,—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>AN EIGHT MILE RUN. The Runcorn Harrier pack were not deterred by the fioxls oi he past week. Tney rolled up iu good num- ness on Saturday to take part in an ordit:ary run from headquarters. The trail was laid over aight miles of good country by Messrs. W. li.ty and S. Clart, includ- ing a few water jumps. The pack kept close tether until half a mile from home, when the final burst was made, i5l which S. Hopkins was the first to arrive home, closely followed by T. Snallcross as second, and W. Thomas third.</p>
        <p>ItUNCDRN MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN. The weekly entertainment in connecrioa with the above mission was given en Tuesday evening b• 7 Mr. G. Orme's concert party. Mr. J. K. Digges presided over a large attendance. All the items were well rendered, several of the items being uncored. The following took part :—Misses Garrie, Douglas, Dyer, and Mt-esrs. H. Darlington, Perchard, Mack, Parker, Wakefisid. The sketch "Condemned on Suspicion " was very well received by the audience. The usual votes of thanks brought a most enjoyable evening to a close.</p>
        <p>THE IMPROVEMENTS OF THE RIVER WEAVER NAVIGATION. £70,000 EXPENDED. On Tuesday the second of Me new electrical swing bridges at Northwich was swung across the river Weaver for the first time, although it will not be permanently opened to traffic for a week or more. The completion of this bridge practically concludes one of the ino.4t exten- sive undertakings in which the Rivn. Weaver Tiustees have ever engaged. In all thy have expended upwards of £70.000 of which sum £25,000 has been spent on the two bridges. THE FREE LECTURES AT THE LIBRARY. The second of the series of lectures at the Runcorn Free Library will be delivered at the Technical Institute on Monday next. The lecturer (the Rev. C. W. L. Christien) has chosen as his subject " My Friends on the shelves "and will doubtless introduce his licar.u.3 to many interesting books. The chair will be taken at 8 o'clock by Mr. W. Handley (chairman of the Free Library Committee). For further particulars see advertisement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LECTURE'S AT THE PRIMITIVE METHODIST MI sroN.</head>
        <p>LECTURE'S AT THE PRIMITIVE METHODIST MI sroN.</p>
        <p>Oa Sunday night a large cougregaAon assarnbled at this Mission to hear the first of a series of lectures which will ba given weekly. The lecturer was Mr. W. Blinston, of Widnes. and his subi.2ct " The Prodigal Son." During the evening Miss A. D ivies sang the solo " Jesu, Lover of my Soul" (Jude) with pleasiug effect. The accompaniment was played by Mr. W. Pritchard, organist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>VIOLENT PRISONER AT WIDNES</head>
        <p>A VIOLENT PRISONER AT WIDNES</p>
        <p>At the Widnes POTi-ce Court. on Monday, before Alder- roan J. W. Waring and Alderman S. Sadler, James Sherlock was tharged with being drunk en Saturday night,and with assaulting P.C.'s Falkingham and Smith. P.C. Falkingham deposed to apprehending prisoner in Victoria-road, for druqkenne3s. When they were taking prisoner tg) the cells he became violent an 1 c iught hcl 1 of the witness by the throat and struck him about the face. They took him back to the parade roan and got his clogs off him, and when they were taking him to the cIL again prisoner again became violent and assaulted P.C. Smith.—P.C. Smith and P.S. Ratcliff: corrob-irated. —Prisoner was fined 2i 61 and costs for the drunkenness or seven days' hard labour, and 20s and ciss for the assault, or 14 days' hard labour. FUNERAL OF THE LATE Mn. WILLIAM GITTENS,</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. William Gittens, of Low- lands-road, whose death we announced last week, took place at Runcorn Cnnetery on Friday afternoon. The solemn procession formed at the deeoased's tat resid- ence, and proceeded to the Primitive Methodist Ceapel, Greeuway-road. where a special service was conducte by the Revs. W. Fidoe and C. Finch (Warrington). Dur- ing the proceedings two favourite hymn:, of toe deceased were sung, viz. : "Rock of Ages," and " Jesu, lover of my soul." From the chapel the deceased's rernains p:e- ceded by a number of representative public men, the trustees of the chapel,and representatives of the Sunday School and other institutions. At the graveside the burial service was hsipiessively read by the Rev. IV. Fidoe. The coffin was literally covered with tributes from the family, personal friecd:, the office bearers at the chapel, &amp;a. Tile deceased was 62 yyars of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>On Slterday evening a meeting of the above order was held in the Greenway-road School. Mr. Thomas Cooke occupied the chair,and in his address emphasized the importance of extending the cane:: eection, as it was sourc3 of strength to the adult order. He furthcr said that the Lifeboat Crew Division owed its proEpority largely to the fact that so many young members were transferred, thus materially strengthening the ,livisien. Mr. Thomas J. Ho:t said that he had received a letter from the Western division, inviting the Runcorn mem- bers to join in a church parade te beheld in the Wesleyan Chapel. The invitation was accepted, the hope being expressed that it would be a success. Mr. Holt gave an interesting account of a visit he had paid to a meeting of the Manche.ter Grand Division, where 70 representa- tives were present on behalf of the 1,500 members. He spoke of the business transacted. The report was well received. The following officers took part in the meet- ing :—Messrs. James Moores, Daniel Bisbrown, Thomas Whyte, and James Johnson. A very pleasant meeting tLen terminated. MAYOE/IL PROCESSION TO CRURCH.</p>
        <p>The newly-elPc;ed Ityor of Widnes (lider:l33.n G. I Neil), accompanied by a large numb ,r of CoueciHors and other leading residents, attended divine service at St. Luke's Church, Farnworth, on Sunday. The procession formed at Farnwarth Grammar School, and was headed by Gossage's Priz3 Band, after which came the Faro- worth D3tachmrrit of Royal Volunteer Engineers and 2nd Volunteer Battalion (Widnes Detachment), under the command of Major Lewis, Captains Fraser, Oppen- helm, and Lieutenant L3WiS, the police, Councillors, Schocl Board. Free Library and Technical InQtraction Committee, officials and the Fire Brigade. A large con- course of people witnessed the procession and the sacred eeifice was filled. The service was conducted by the Vicar (Rev. J. Wrigle•-Williams) and the Rev. W. Thatcher, B-A. The Vicar made a spirited appeal on behalf of the Transvaal War Fund, to which the offer- tory was to be devoted. The congregation rarponded generously, £25 Els. 91. bling collected. The offertory in the evening was devoted to the same object.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CLAIM AGAINST HELSBY CHAPEL TRUSTEES,</head>
        <p>CLAIM AGAINST HELSBY CHAPEL TRUSTEES,</p>
        <p>His Honour Judge Wynne Fiouikes, presided at the Runcorn County Court, on Tuesday. A case in which the Holslay and District Water C,-)inpany, Limited, Heisby,sued James Brandreth and Benjamin Parker,twe trustees of the Zion Chapel, Helsby, for the sum nf £2 10s. ld. in respect rif work done by them was hearl. Mr. Pearson, solicitor, Chester, upheld the plaint, whilst Mr. A. Browne, Warrington, defended. From the evi- dence of William Wood, secretary of the Helshy District Water Company, it appeared that the aeconnt was made up of four items. The first two being 95 91 and 53 91, for articles connected with the laying on of water, and two subs squent ones of 153 9,1 and 13s 101 for similar work.—Mr. Browne said Mr. Brandreth admitted order- ing the first two items, but both the defendants denied knoa ledge of the other items.—His H mour after a patient hearing, remarked to Mr. Pearson, there was no proof or any evidence of the trustees having given any order for the work, other than the first two items, and he would accordingly enter judlinent against Mr. Bran- dreth for the first two items (Iss 6 1) and non-suited the plaintiffs with regard to Benjamin Parker altogether and awarded him full costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>DISPUTE OVER A GUN</head>
        <p>A DISPUTE OVER A GUN</p>
        <p>At the Widnes County Court, on Friday, before his Honour Judge Qhand, Thomas Henry Thomas, iron- monger, Hale Bank. sued Robert Smith, also of Halo Bank, for 153. Mr Poole apneared for the plaintiff, and the defendant was represented by Mr. Knowles.—The complainant said that in the course of his businees he dealt in guns. The defendant Smith was a gamQkeecer. Some two years ago, witness had let defendant have a gun to try it, and then some time after he offered wit- ness 303 for it and 20 young rabbits. He paid the 30 but had received no rabbits. Witness had met him on one occa.ion, and aske I about the 20 rabbits, and he replied" I ,only promised 12."—Two witnesers were called who said they had heard the plaintiff ask defen- dant in the Ohilde ofEfalepublic house about the ra'.bits, and defendant said he did not promise 20 but 12 —For the defence, Mr. Knowl,s said the defendant was the gamekeeper for Colonel Thackburne. Ho bought the cun bat bougto- it for Thomas Joynson, of the lighthouse. aod paid 30; for it. When it was bein.l, sell plaintiff asked d4enriant if he could get him a nest of rabbi, and he said he thought he could. Tile rabbits had nothing whatever ta do with the traosaetion.---The de- fendant was called and bore out Me. Kn stlte- ment.—Thomas Joynson said the F.:an was boo7ht for him. It was a bad one, and he was afraid to use it.— His Honour found for the defenriant with costs.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Percy Milton. We regret to announce the death of qrs. Percy Milton, reliet of the late Mr. Percy Milton, who wae a co-proprietor at the Warrington Theatres, which took place at her resilence, Ashfield, Latch- ford, on Friday evening. The remains were internd on Monday at the Warrington Cemetery, the Rev. G Roberts officiating. The chief mourners were Master Herbert Milton (son), I"ifessrs. C Pigot and W Pigot (brothers-in-law), Mrs. Arrywsmith (sister), Mrs. Charles Pigot (sister-in-law), Mr. G Wareham (son-in-law), Mr. and Mrs. A Milton, and Mr. J Potter. A large number of wreaths, c., were sert.</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Band of Hope, Sharp- street.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, in connection with the above, a limelight entertainment was given in the Mission Room by Mr. H. Taylor, P.C.M. Air. H. 0_ Bennett presided over a larg, attondance. The pro- gramme included illustrations of several stories amongst which were "Her Benny,"" The stolen shirt," and "How Uncle Pompey joined the pledge." The proceedings concluded with the singing of the National Anthem. Miss Wildman presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 17, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 17, 1899.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PRIMITIVE METHODIST</p>
        <p>CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Grand Bazaar.</p>
        <p>From a short resume of the hiseore of the Primitive Methodist cause iu Runcorn we see that ;—Primitive Methodist Preachers first visited Runcorn in tee year 1821, from the Tunstall Circu-t ; bus no beinF.,, s promising as other places to waicn they were invited it was soon relinquished. A second effort was made to establish a Society in 1823, by preachers from Preston Breerik,butfer some reason tee place was again abandoned in 1828. Missionary operations were renewed by Mr. R. Turner, who commenced his labours on May 11th 1834. Open-air services were held until the cold bla,ts of winter rendered it necessary to secure a building in which to worship. A schoolroom was then used until 1838, when E. Chapel was erected in Regent-street, and the society acquired additional ;strength and stability. Teie was occupied until 1870, when the present Caapel ia Green way-road was built, whieh, with subsequ at alterations, cost £1,812. The old Regent-street premises were afterwards used as a &amp;relay school until 1832, when the present sceool was erected on land adjoiiiing the Chapel at a cost of L 1,570. On the entire premises a debt of £1,420 still remains. The ordinary expenses amount to about £250 per annum, and in addition to this £235 has recently been raised for the renovation of the Grapel without adding xi the original debt. With the object; of reducing the burden of £1,420 upon the chapel and schoolroom, a two day's baz tar was held on Welnesday and Tharsday, in the latter place, which was well patronised, and proved highly successful. Tne scheolroom lent itself admirably to the purposes of the bazaar. Aloag toe four sides were ar- ranged the principal stalls, with the flower stall as a strikingly pretty centre piece. Overhead hung festoons of flags and bunting, and an all sides there were tasteful decorations, forming a pleasing and harmonious setting to the heterogenous array of articl displayed far sale. Etch stall was heavily laden, and there were articles ornamental, useful, pleasing, and fascinating, and of such variety as to embrace everything froio the ' pin " to the " completest o Afit." Nor was that all, of " other " attractions there were lets, : " A fine Art Gallery," " The phonograph,' " The grame- phone," "The perfexscope " and numerous other side shows were all " get-at-able." and the mystic threshold crossed for the " paying." Competitions such as hat trimming and metal polisaing afforded diversity enough, to say nothing of the numerous amusements thrown in. The refreshment stall wassa bountiful board, and temptingly laid out. In short every condition an] palate had something to its taste, and aIL c 'Wel purchase either some useful or valuable article, whilst at the same time forwarding s good cause. THE FIRST DAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>At the opening proceedings, on Wednesday, Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P., presided. There were also present :—Mr Ssmuel Taylor, J P., Miss Taylor, the Revs W Moe, C W L Christien, S Wright. J A B Malvern, B M n.rison, Mrs F J Norman, Messrs A R Norman. J.P., innleS Wilson. J Spruce, F Bsck, D Bisbrown, J W Legritourn, J M Frith, J R Frith J D Jones, W Hough (Frodshein), W Grounds; alsa a large number of ladies, the sveeral atallholders, &amp;c., Ese. The singing of hymn 815 "Not heavens wide range of hallowed space," and the reading of the 84th Psalm by the Rev. S. Wright, was followed by the Rev. C. W. L. Christien leading in prayer. The Rev. W. Fidoe expressed first how very plea7ed they were to see so many presmt that afternoon. They regarded the presence of so many of diliereet denomina- tione as illustrative of the true unity which underlay all their differences. Be then referred to the object of the bazaar,which was to raise at least £2OO tewards the liquidation of the debt, and spoke wita thenk.felness of the great amount of sympathy they had reee iced from members of the different churches,not only, of their own churches but also of the Anglican Churches in the town. (Applause.) For that,he would assure them, they were devoutly thankful. (Hear, hear.) He was sure they all welcomed Miss Taylor to their bazaar that afternoon—(applause)—ind he was sure they re- }Aced in her growing influence, usefuleees,and popular- ity for good in the town and district. (Hear, hear.) He would say nothing further, but reiterate the great plea- sure all felt at the presence amongst them of Miss Taylor, her father (Mr. S. Taylor). Mr. Norman, and all those ready to sympathise with them by contributing to the success of the afternoon. (Loud applause.) Mr. F. J. Norman also spoke, alluding in flattering terms to Mr. and Miss Taylor, who,he said, were always ready to help a good cause irrespective of creed. (Applause.) Mies Taylor, expressed very great pleasure at being present, and in a neat speech, wished the object every success, hoping that the ladies would succeed in dispos- ing of all their goods, and declared the bazaar open with infinite pleasure. (Loud Applause.) Miss Taylor was then preseated with a beautiful bouquet by Master Stanley Pilgrim. Mr. J. Wilson on behalf 7of the trustees and members of the church, said he begged to move that they accord to Miss Taylor and to Mr. Norman—for he understood I tooth were to be coupled in the vote of thanks—their ' hearty vote of thanks for their kindness in coming amongst them to do and say what they had done. Miss Taylor was in very good company, and it was not, the first time in the history of their church that they had names of ladies who had done them good in various ways Is their church's hist sry they would find coupled with it the names of many good women. They were very proud that Miss Taylor had consented to come amongst them and to wish them " God Speed "in their enterpriFe but, of course, they could not let the subject go like that, they ceuld not help mentioning—as Mr. Norman had done—her h moured father (Mr. S. Taylor). (Applause.) Having paid a high compliment to Mr. Taylor, Mr. Wilson pro- ceeded to say be did not know a man who had done more tor the best interest and welfare of the town than Mr. F, J. Norman. (Hear, hear,) He had worked man- fully to secure the loaf for the workiegm en of the town at a time when it appeared that the broad was going elsewhere. (Loud applause.) Mr. Wilson ceacluded by proposing that their best thanks be given to Miss Taylor and Mr. Norman for the pan they had taken in the pro- ceedings. (Applause.) Mr. J. Spruce seconded, and heartily endorsed all that had been said. He took it that the presence of so many Irearesentativee of different Churches was an augury for good for the future carrying on of the work of the 11 Master. I Mr. Norrnan,in putting the vote, obs?rved his position I was a little embarrassing, as he did not care for votes oh thanks.—The motion was, however, carried with accla- mation. Mr. Samuel Taylor, on behalf of his daughter, thanked them very much for the vote of thanks they had acc ded her. He was delighted to see so many inople cos nected with other places of worship pre: int. When there was an unanimity of that kind amongst the people of the tewn good must come out of it ; and, also, it showed that their friends had Come there to help in a practical form. Their room would be worth more than their company, if they simply Caine to show their faces and say a few words. (Laughter, and hear, hear.) Be was sere, however,that those around him had come there determined to help in a practical form. (Applause.) He would like to say the room was very beautifully decorated, and by the way, he could imagine Vie ladies saying"l wish they would shut up and give us a chance" —(laughter and applause)—therefore he thanked them for the vote of thanks. (Applause.) AS far as he (him- self) was concerned he was very delighted to find Mr. Norman was such a bashful youne man regarding vot-s of thanks. (Renewed laughter and applause.) The practical business of the bazaar then commenced. THE SECOND DAY'S PROCEEDINGS. Interesting Speech by Mr. F. Wiswall.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the opening ceremony was again well attended the chair being filled by Councillor S. Owens (ot Widnes). There were also pe.--sent Mr F Wiswall (chairman of the Runcorn Urban District Council), Mrs and Miss Wiswall, Revs S Might, W Fidoe, M El Chap- man, H S Targett, T Sidney Morris, Messrs James Prit- chard, D Bisbrown, J Stubbs, etc.</p>
        <p>After devotional exercise, The Rev. W. Fidoe, extended to Mr. F. Wiswall and Mr. S. Owen the same . cordial welcome they had ex- tended towards Miss Taylor and Mr. F. J Norman the previous day. He had nothing new to add te the state- mat made previously only that their effete s had already lesulted in the accumulation of £165 16). 44., the pro• cerds of the first day's bazaar. (Hear, hear,) Mr. S. Owens, in a speech brimful of remineseences of his early days in Runcorn, spoke feelingly of his con- n'Ttions with that place of worship. He was v ry pleased to be with them on such a .ery pleasant day when the weather was so nice and propitieus, and everything in that room so bright and so lively. He was pleased to meet the gentleman who had so kindly consented to come to open the bazaar. (Applause.) He believed Mr. Wiswall was a churchman, and it was a pleasure to see members of different committees work- ing hand in hand. Having referred with p'f-asure to the amicable way in whieh all parties in Widneh worked to:;ether for common good, Mr. Owens refer:ei to the fact that he had met Mr. Wiswall before, though per- haps he did not recollect it. That occasion was on a committee which had for its objent the inducement ot the C, unty Authorities to build a vehicu'arbridee across from Runcorn to Widnes. (Applau,e.) He spoke with feelings of sorrow of the fact that with only such a small stream dividing them, Runcora and Wide. es dideer meet and mingle more together for good. (Applause.) The good people of Runcorn, he was bound to sly, were always good at according all the help and sympathy and practical support they could to any gned cause, and he was sure the same favour wou,d be accord, d to Mr. Feeoe by the good people gathered or eind him, to sup- port that great and laudable moeement. (Applause ) The bazaar was an institution by which their lay ftiends could rally round and help. Havieg e,encleded with eulogistic reference to the valued work of the ladies, Mr. Owens intimated the pleasure with which he canal upora Mr. Wiswall to open the bazaar. (L led applause.) Mr. F. Wiswall, on rising was cordially received. In the course of his address, he, said he had never occupi-d a nesition of that nature before and had asked Mr. Fidoe to get someone else ti take the. duty but he would not he put off, ac that he could not after all refnse Mr.</p>
        <p>Filoele request. Oae cause for his presence was be- cause he knew they were a struggling church, unosten- tatious and intensely protestant, going on in the same old ploddin; way that they had known for the last 30 years. He credited their chairman with a fine bump of perception for he had pereeived one thing that not one in a hundred in Runcorn were aware of, although they knew the town and its people 100 times better than he (Mr. Oa ens) di 1. Teat was that he was a Churchman—chat was a member of the Caurch of England. It was a wonderful thing but it was a fact, and he felt sue that net one in a hundred knew it. He was brought up as a member of the Church of El:eland, and had never been a member of any other Ceurch. Most of those preseet would be astonishel to hear it, and most especially the Ceurch of E Iflatid people, because tiny did not know him. (Leaghter.) They might say lee,was a very poor churchmao. Speak- ing with regard to the Church of Eagiend he felt that there was a great cri is coming that would be a blew to Protestantion. Now that Church (the Primi- tive Methodist) and other institutions who were as he had said intensely Protestant, although they w re doing their work quietly and unostentatiously were going to come tithe trout because they were go- ing to be the backbone of Protestantism. It was a most serious matter. Kipling had told them of the " abse it- minded" soldiers. There were also absent-minded churchmen, who did not see their danger. (Hear, hear.) He had only seen it that day, and he felt it was sure to come and there would be disestablishment not from without but from within, and when the rupture did come the Pro- testant section of the Church of England would want the support and help and sympathy against the Roman Ritual element. He did not want to say hard tnings, and did not want to hurt anyone's feelings, but he would call one section the Church of England Ritualists. That portion of the Church of England would want to go over to the Cetholic Cnurce to take the property of the Church of England with them. Tney had to watch that they did not do so. ED did not intend to say so much but he was a Pro- testant himself and as intensely so as any of them— (he ar,hear)—encl did not think be would be doing much harm in calling attention for he did not think when he slid it that they all had good intentions towards the Church cif Eegland—(applause)—and further he hoped to see the day and hoped to live to see the day , when he would feel and be a devoted member of the Church of England. (Applause.) He urged that all the Protestant sections should be united in one great effort ' to support Protestanism as against Roman Catholicism. (Applause.) It was a delicate matter to speak about at a bazaar because as Mr Fidoehad stated teere were repre- sentatives of ever denoiniaation present but he hoped they would excuse his remarks. He did not however, think there wee .tnu th of that objectioaable error, he had spoken about in Runcorn, but they had it in large towns and among places that were little known. He hoped they would excuse his reference to the matter. He was pleesed to be with them because he felt at home, and because they were going steadily along with litle or no ritual. (near, hear.) It was the simple Gospel ;hat they promulgate], and he thought the more they had of that the better it would be. (Applause.) Flavin congratulated all on the success of the previous dayeand r •ferrrd to the admirable conditions of the bozear Mr. Wis wall formally declared it open. (Lend applause.) Mt. James Pritchard moved a hearty vote of thanks to M r. F Wiswall and Mr. 5 Owens for their services. The Rev. S. Wright seconded, and the motion was , heartily accorded aad responded to. Mrs. Pilerim pinned beautiful buttonholes in both the chairman's and Mr. Wiswall's coats.</p>
        <p>The officials responsible for the very efficient arrange- ments of the bizlar were :—The Rev. W Fidoe,minister; M,turs. T Booth and J Wilson (treasurera), and J Fleet, and E H Hillidge (secretaries). Th!stalthold.rs were as follow F. :</p>
        <p>Enterprise Stall : Mrs Thomason, Miss A J Congregational Stall : Ms Wilson. Mrs James Stubbs, Mrs Withington, grs Robert Stubbs, Mrs Hill, Mrs Richard- son. Beehive Stall : Mrs Booth, Mrs jabez Stubbs. Mrs Cotterill, Miss Lunt, Miss Shingler, Mrs W Rilcross, Mrs Woo lvisrcl, Miss Sidley. Ban Marahe Stall : Miss Filo% Miss Fleet, Misses Davies, Miss Sayle, Misses Hillidge, Miss Hardley. Misses 030 e, Misses Stubbs, Miss Jessie Withington, Miss Wilson. Advertising and Specialities Stall : Mr I Wi!sari, Mr W Gittins, jun.. bltr A Davies, Mr S Parkinson, Mr W Prit- chard, Mr T Hillilge, Mr L Ball, Mr A darilay, Mr 2 Saylo.</p>
        <p>, Flower Stall : Mr and Mrs Pilgrim, Refreshment Stall : Mrs Collier, Mrs Hardley, Mrs Sayle, Mrs Horton, Miss Dora Wilson. Knick-Knack Stall : Miss Florrie Hill, Miss Beatrice A Stubbs, Miss M A Hortsn, Miss Annie Brockbank. Bran TO, : Miss Wright, Miss B Fleet, Miss Dora Stubbs. Ice Cream Stall; Mr Rudd, Miss SA Stubbs.</p>
        <p>We understand tqat the object of the bazicir was more thau fully attained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTESq---- [By A CORRESPONDENT.]</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTESq- ---- [By A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>Qaite a little sen ation was caused by the logical discussion of " Nemesis" whic'i appeared in these columns last week. Many opinions regar,ling it are rife, but the most general one is that it was a " poser." There used to be a good " riddle" wni wentsowethinz after this wise :—" Say, friend, there is one thing you can't gat over!" " Well what is it ?" " The Moon !" But these last few days I have head a new rendition of the" riddle," and by the way the answer is " Nemosis." Changes come about so funnily you know!</p>
        <p>What has been voted a general mistake was tie fact that at the meeting at the Drill gall, for the support of the wives and families of our gallant soldiers in South Africa.there was no collection. No arrangement was made for pasJing round the has! Why no one knows; and it was not until after the show—the day after —that it was suggested. An oppprtunicy was missed, and now the people will have to be visited. I understand Miss Taylor, Rydal Mount, has an entertainment on foot, the proceeds of which will go to the tund, and I hope hr efforts will be well supported.</p>
        <p>Some interesting reading is afforded in matter of Mr. IP Wiswall's speech at the opening of the baziar on Thursday. We see Mr. Wiswall in an enrirely new light, end are glad to hear that his inclinatior.s to Protestantism are so "intense"—to use his own word. Possibly after the lapse of a little time we shall hear that Mr. Wiswall has been offered a churchwardenship or some such com- pliment to secure his silence. But I think he is too bluff and straightforward to be easily influenced.</p>
        <p>Toe ultimate decision of the Local Government Board with regard to the application for loins by the Runcorn Urban District Council is a mattsr up- on which little or nothing new is apparent. In well informed sources I hear that the a'xitude of the Inspector is decidedly favourable and the Coun- cil are likely to get the permission asked for. Now that the final die is cast in this long-delayed scheme a calm has settled over the town and the majority of the people are prepared to accept their "burdens" as a matter of course.</p>
        <p>Allegations Against a Warrington Husband.</p>
        <p>An inquest was cunducted at the Warrington Police Station on Monday, by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, relative to the death of Ann Rench, 79, Longford-street which took place on Sunday, under circumstances detailed below.—Mr. T. R. Granger appeared on behalf of deceased's husband. —The Coroner, in opening the proceedings said that deceased and her husband were married in January 1898. She was a widow and he was a widower with f• ur children. Oa accuun% of the children they had often quarrelled. At seven o'clock on Tuesday the 31st of last month deceased said she was going away. Same words passed between them and it was alle:ed that the husband gut hold of his wile, put her on the flo3r and knelt upon her. A neighbour, hearing the woman scream went and knocked at the dour. Deceased came out and went to her sister's house On Wednesday the first of this month she complained of feeling ill. Dr. Fox was called in and attended her for a few days,when Dr. Bowden took charge of the patient She gradu- ally became wore and died on Sun-lay last. A:sum- mons had been taken out against her husband but she was unable to attend the Court on account of her condition. Ilts (the Coroner) had had a coa- versation with the Chief ()unstable and they were both satisfied that the husband had nothing to do with the cause of death. As the summons was issued and allegations were made they had had a post modem examination to settle the matter.— Juseph Bench, 79, Longford-‘tvet, husband of the deceased, gave evidence of identification.—Dr. Bowdon said that Mrs. Rench came un4 his care a week ago, having previously been attended by Dr Fos% She was semi-conscious and paralysed in the left arm and leg. He was told that she had been illtreated by her husband, but he could not find any bruises on her body except on the left hip. He at- tended her up to her death which took place on Sunday. He had since made a post mortem examin- ation. Deceased appeared to be a woman of forty- five. She was wasted but otherwise apparently healthy. There were two slight bruises on the left hip. He opened the skull but there was no frac- ture. Death was due to natural causes.—A. verdict in accordance with the medical evidence was re- turned. PUBLIC BBIEFIT U7D131/ THE CLOCK roa 8143 BOOTS Ii t lANCASHIRE,—Advt.</p>
        <p>Agri or CUNNINCrIAM! MINH itIL WATERS.—Advt</p>
