      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN RELIEF FUND. Town's Meeting.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RELIEF FUND. Town's Meeting.</p>
        <p>A largely attended town's ineting was held at the Driil Hall, Runcorn, oa Nl)n-lay eveuing, for the pur- pose of raising a fund in aid of the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families Association, the Patriotic, and other funds. The hand of the Runcorn Dtaehment 2nd V.B.E.C. Regiment paraded the town playing martial airs, and opened the proceedings at the Drill Hall with a spirited rendition of the " Soldiers of the Queen."</p>
        <p>The chair was taken by Mr F Wiswall (chairman of the Runcorn Urban District Council) and he was sup- portel on the platior n by Sir Richard Mareus Broke, Bart., Lady Brooke, Revs Canon Maitland Wood, C W L Chtestien, C K Killick, J Osborne, W H Stables, Mesprs F J Norman, A R Norman, E Imison, C L Evans, S Taylor, F A Like, Dr McD3ugall, W Timmins, J Wilding, C E Linaker, J W Lightburo, and C Marshall (secretary). Mr. F. A. Lake read several letters of apology for non-attendauce :—Sir John Brunner, Burt., M.P., enclosed a cheque for £25, for the Run- corn fund, and intimated be had forwarded a sino'lar sum to Northwich. Mr. Joseph Handley enclosed £s.—Messrs..Archibald Banning and Samuel Joynson, of the Royal Oak, Westoo, forwarded £4 13 6.1 collected in that locality for the relief of the fami.ies of reservists. —Mr. R H. Posnett, of Highfield Tannery,intimated he would be pie tsed to contribute £lO 10s; and that th, men employed in the tannery were taking a lively in- terest in the fund and last week had collected about £2. They had decided to continue the cellection weekly 80 long as the necessity existed. He also intimated the hope of forwarding a further subscription from the firm. —Captain G F. Ashton of the Runcorn Detachment of the 2nd V B.E.C. Regiment regretted his absence through business engagements, but put the Drill Hall at the disposal of the Committee of the Fund for the pur- poses of meetings, concerte, or anything of that nature. The Volunteers he was also desired to say would he de- lighted to give thdr services on any occasions.—These ann mncements were most enthusiastically received.</p>
        <p>- The Ch sirrns,n added to Mr. Like's rem irks : The Ship Canal Company had already arranged a concerted effort where by from the highest to the lowest in its employ every individual could contribute something to the cause. In the department With which he was immediately connected the work nen hid resolved to contribute an hour's wages per week—(applause,—which would be handed to the fund for the town of Runcorn in due course. (Loud applause.) Any reserve men taken from the Company's employment would also have their places restored to them up m their re`urn. (Applause.) There had never been such a wave of enthusiasm and p itrioism going over the country as 6,t the present time. The d ,plorable war in whim they were eagaged WAS one thee must make the hest of and one they were bound to go through with. Referring to the letter written by our glorieus Q3el to Lady White ,the speaker characterised the action as one of the best and vvjgcst things the Queen had ever done ; for, in a tirn like tee present, people would try to put " wet blanket," and find fault, and dictate what ought and ought not to be done. (Applause.) Their gracious Q leen, the first lady in the land, started the movement. The Princess of Wale; and other ladiee of the Royal Family in- terested themselves init. The first lady in the country the Duchess of NY Agnainster, took an active part, and they bad present Lady Brooke—(loud applause)--and Sir Richard to assist in the greit movement. (Loud and continued applause.) He thought when the ladies throuehout the country took an interest in such matters, and were prepared to ge sheulder to shoulder and act in committee with them success was assured. (Applause.) It seeemed to him that the movemeet was going to be such a one as had never been equellel in the history of the country. (Ap- plause.) Let their menhood and womanhood know and recognize their liability in that respect and there would be n 3 need to resort to conscription to fight their countries battles; (Loud applause.) He had pleasure in calling upon Sir Richard Brooke to move the first resolution. . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Sir Richard Brooke, who was received with loud ap- plause, moved the following :—" That having regard to the suffering and distress occasioned by the war in the Tranivaal to the widows and orphans of the soldiers who fall, to the wives and children separated from their husbands and fathers by the exigencies of the war, and to the wounded and disabled soldiers, this meeting considers it a duty and most necessary, that a public fund be at once opened for their relief." Be added that the object for which they had met together must appeal to every man and woman. (Applause.) The war was one which they had 13z.en driven into. It was not one whic'a they wisbcd to embark upon, but now that it had been begun he was afraid they would have to go on with it. Be had the greatest confidence in the people of the district, who had always responded most gener- ously to any appeal for a good purpose. 111 was neces- sary that the money and assistance should reach the people promptly. (Applause) He favoured the hand- ine of what money was not required in that district t the county fund. (Hear, hear.) He had been asked by Lady Brooke, as president of the Runcorn D vieion of the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families Association—(applause) to announce that she had received substantial subserip- tioes. These included £lO from Major Hazelhurat, £lO from Mrs Hazellaurst, Halton Grange, and £lO from Miss Hazelnurst. (Applause.) The Rev. Canto Wood secluded the resolution, and in the course of his remarks, alluded to the willingness of England to eater upon the war, which indeel had been forced upon them. Speaking of the necessity for help- ing forward the fund,he did not think there was a single person who deubtei it. oi the Continent there was a most extraordinary wave of dislike towards England, apparent from the perusal of the Continental news- pipers, and there was no doubt that the Coutineatels looked upon them with a considerable amount of envy, Comp 'ring the Continent al system with our own, he said on the Continent every man was obliged to be a soldier for some p art of his life, whereas in England it was optional whether a person sp eat any part of his life as a soldier or not. Tne conclusion there was thee ought to be extremely thankful to their soldiers who voluntarily were fighting their battles for them. (Applause.) He wan glad to find the workingmen had shown so deep an interest in the welfare of the Army at that particular time. He argued that Runcorn being one of those plecee from which very few soldiers were called, there was all the more reason why they should give a great deal more. In conclusion he appealed to all to bury the hatchet, and be as one in the face of the difficulty confronting them. (Applause). Mr. Samuel Taylor iu supporting the resolution before them, felt after the able way the Chairman, Sir Richard Brooke, and the Rev. Canon Wood had dealt with it there was not much for him to say, but he wished to express the great pleasure be felt in taking part in that meeting. (Applause.) He was delighted when Mr. F. Wiswall (as chairman of the Urban District Council) decided to call a public meeting, as he felt sure the Rencern people would respond to such a noble object, as they did in every other object which was for the good of the people. (Applause.) Tbey had met there that night not to discuss the quention as to the Cause of the war or anything in connection with what led up to it ; they had met there t 3 do their duty. (Hear,hear.) Teat was in providing maintenance and a fund to ad- minister to the wants of the wives and families of their noble and gallant soldiers. (hoed applause.) If they—he ought not to say they—bat if the young men of their country in days of war had to be called out for service compulsorily, they might well look upon it as a great hardship, but, as they knew, they had an army of gallant and noble men to look after their interests bath abroad and at home. (Applause.) No greater favour nor anything they could possibly do would make their soldiers' hearts rejoice more than to know that they were taking care of their wives and families kr them at home. (Loud applause.) He was sure all of them on reading their papers every day were delighted to sofa the enormous sums which had been cal- 'octet It was v.ery gratifying to everybody, and he felt they in Runcorn would not be behind any other part of the United Kingdom of equal size to the town. (Ap- plause.l 'rue resolution was then put from the chair and car- ried with great enthusiasm. Mr. Norman said the resolutions which he bad the honour to propose read as follows :—"That the follow- ing gentlemen form a committee (with power to add to their number) for the purpose of carrying out the previ- ous resolution, and of deciding the disposal of the fund, also that Mr. T C Haddock be appointed bon treasurer, and Mr. E Mil-shall hon. secretary to the fund—mem- bers of the Urban District Council, Sir Rmhard Brooke, Bart • Rev Canon wood, Rev C W L Christien, Messrs. F J Neeonan. J.P., A R Norman, J.P., J Handley, J.P., C L Evans, W Timmins, J.P., R Frith, P R Allen, G F Ashton, W R Earp, J Oekleston, A Robertson, Bateson, Dr. McDougall, and Mess.s. F A Lake, and T C Had- dock." They now approached the practical part of the question and that success or non-success must largely depend on this Committee. The Chairman had spoken of the enthusiasm which swept the country at the mom nt. This ne (Mr. Norman) submitted would be best appreciated by contrast with what had been done in the past. What was the fate of their old soldiers? The men who had made their Empire. The killed he feared were little more thoueht of. The disabled had hard work to raise capital to buy a tin can and beg charity at the street corners. The Balaclava heroes—(loud applause,)—they die well to cheer ? "When should th4r gloty fade ?" Yes,but some of them died in the workhouse for all that. Then the WiVPd and childrsn. He feared that Kipling was right when he wrote, " They lived on half of nothing." " Paid 'em punctual once a week." He had asked an old soldier at his own works the other day for his experience. Experi- ence tee witty Irishman replied "There was I foighting for my country in India yonder, and the old woman struegiing at home with the childer wid, nothing but what she earned by the sweat of her brow and obtained</p>
        <p>from Charity." li'ant of thought did they @ay ? Well, perhaps yes ? But none the lees was it a f ml blot, on th national escutcheon,and he was proud that they were going to wipe it out. They must all play their part. The committee must so organis that while" dukes' sons, cooks' sons, sons of the belted Earl," were standing shoulder to shoulder fighting their battles at Mafeking„ Kimberley, and Ladysmith yonder they at hip:tie, baro- nets, trades folk and workmen would stand shoulder to</p>
        <p>NAT AN l'ku), good plain LOOK-GRNISRAL; able to manage V a small .i,dry ; also a House Parlourmaid ; ages not ~less than 23 preferred.—Address, stating wages requlred and • eferences, to Sirs. GORDOY, Clhetwynd Rectory, Newport,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>Fos BURNS, BOILS, BRUISES,</p>
        <p>BITES, AND BROKEN SKIN. FOR CHAPPED HANDS, CHILBLAINS,</p>
        <p>CUTS, AND COLDS USE</p>
        <p>HOMOCE,4.</p>
        <p>FREELY.</p>
        <p>shoulder raising funis to alleviate S 3 far as money could the dreadtul horrirs which dogged the track of war. So far as his company was concerned he thought he might safely say that the directors would keep open the places of the men called out, and not forget their local organi- sation when they apportioned the subscription which they would surely give. With regard to the works with which be had the honour to be directly associated he was proulto report that they had decided to raise a sum of from £2OO to £250 during the neat 12 months to be devoted to charities. He need hardly tell them that their War Fund had the first call, and until it was over and God grant that it might be over soon the weekly proportion of that amount would be paid over to the local committee. The balance they intended for toe proposed Cottage Hospital and poseibly in that institution they might have the pleasure of nursing 50t11,3 wounded soldier back to health. They might be pear but ,they were willing and there were mane sourc, a in the town which if properly worked would yield substantial sums. He (Mr. Norman) was cenfideet that he voiced the sentiments of all whet' he said that the homes, the wiees, the widows and the chi!dren should he helped in no niggardly spirit, but in right royal fashion worthy of the proud and generous sons of a mighty Empire. Rev. C. W. L. Ceristien said all could join in a move- ment like this irrespective of party feeling. (Applause.) As it bad already been said by one speaker taey were not there to argue the war They had their own opin- ion or cenvic!i on on one side or the other. They did not say that war was right or wrong, for that was not the place or the time co argue the matter. As Russell Howell said in one of his poems" If a man does wrong, God will send him ii the bill," and it would come to those who had the bdagine about of this war. Mr. E. linison, in supporting the resolution said a letearer duty was never put before a public audience. The duty Of loolr;-eg after these who had hien ;eft hi.- hind was that which they were nit to consider that night. In many a home there would go up a cry of orr many a British soldi,r would be laid low, many brave men would be wounded. That was the natural remit of war, and that was the sorrow they had to ' avoid. But the sorrow and trouble that came from the , empty cupboard and fireless grate they could and met prevent. .(Aaplawe.)</p>
        <p>The Chairman said kr. Norman had told them what vary excellent arrangements had been male in some of the works. lie did not think that it would bi at all difficult to organise similar arrangements for the whole town, and he did not entertain the lea•zt doubt that Runcorn people would respond right heartily. The resolution was carried urianiin nasty. Sir Wchard Brooke proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall se:3onded, and observed that this war above all others seemed to have inspired a feeling of patriotism that he had never known in the district for the lost 30 years. He thJught they were very much indebted to all who had inspired the calling of that meeting. Be had reason to believe that the initiation in this matter came from Lldv Brooke in a letter addressed to the Chairman of the Urban Coanail. (Applause.) Their duty was simply to their soldiers' wives and their chil- dren, and if they Ind been backward in times gone by in doing their duty, it was no reason why thy should be backward now. (Applause). The motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, in responding, said Mr. C. L. Evans had kindly undertaken to act in the matter as regarded the towmships of Halton, Norton, and neighbourhood. The proc,wdinzs termin ited with the playing of the National Anthem and" Role Brittannia " by the band.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. JOHN GRIFFITHS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a presentation was made to Mr. Jehn Griffiths, who has been connected with Bewsey Forge (Pearson and Knowles Goal and Iron Company) for 29 years, for a considerable period as manager over the forge and mills. Recently he severed his connection ' with the works and benme the tenant of the Cheshire Cheese Inn, Latchford, and his friends decided to make him a presen:alon to show the respect entertained for hill:. The ceremony took place in the large club room of the Cheshire Cheese lon, the chair being occupied by M. Samuel WakefiAd, ani the vice-chair by Mr. R. T. ' Smith; about 20 other ironworkers being in attendance Tne presentation took the form of a timepi-ce, which is a most artistic piece ,ef work, and was supplied by Mr. Thomas Podm ore, of Hightown, Manchester. It is of black merble and is copied frcm an ornament in tho Champs E:yees33. The timepiece, which is surmounted with a tigare represecting Britannia, contains thefollow- ing insription :—" Presented to Mr. John Griffiths, by the melmen of Bewsey ir meeorks, as a token ot respect and esteem on his retirement from his position a:s forge manager. November, 1899." It is a2l day clock. There are two figures of Marti horses to match. The CHAIRMAN. having propo:ed the health of the Queen, and briefly alluded to the war in the Transvaal, spoke in high terms of Mr. Griffitbs and his connection with the works. He was very sorry to have to part with him, and he believed that that vr as a feeling which would be shared by all present. rhey all regretted to hear of his ill.laaLth, but trusted that he would soon be restored in his now sphere of lablue He hoped that whoever took his place would do his work as wel as Mr. Griffiths had done it, and work as hard for the firin and the men. There was not even a lad on the ground who was afraid of being snubb,d by Mr. Griffiths, and that was Saying a great deal. (Appliuse.) Mr- Themes CRITCHLEY was called upon by the Chair- man to make the preeentation, and he did so in suitab'e terms, aliu Sing to his 30 years' connection with the re cipient, during the whole of which time he had never received an uugracious word fro:n him. The recoire- meats of the men had been attended to as expeditiously as possible' and by Mr. Griffiths' departure the firm had lost a good servant and the heads of the different de- partments a friend. (App!ause.)</p>
        <p>The VICE PRESIDENT' corroborated all that had been said by the previous speakers, and added that his con- nection with Mr. Griffiths dated as far back as 1875, and during the whole of that period he had always found him ready to further the interests of the company and the men with whom he had been breueht into coatact. EIA hoped that be and his family would be spared for many years, and that he would be very successful is his new sphere of labour. (Applause ) Mr. GEORGE BEARD (who was formerly employed at the works) was one of the speakers, and be oWerved that he could endorse every word that had already been said. He knew no one worthier of a testimonial. Speaking conscientiously, he had always found Mr. Griffiths to be thorough, and that was the chief point in his character. (Heir, hear, atid applause.) Speeches of a complimentaey character were made by Messrs. James Ashton, Whitehouse, Walter Ward,George Morrell, and Thomas Pod more (who supplie I the time- p'ece), after which Mr. Griffiths, who was overcome with emotion, responded, thanking all his friends very heartily for the token of goodwill presented to him. It would, he said, be highly prz d oy himself and his wife and family. (Applause.) On the motion of the VICE-CHAIRMAN, seconded by Mr. BEARD, a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Critchley and Mr. Morrell for selecting the timepiece, and 'Abe pro- ceedings terminated.</p>
        <p>7.HE _HEALING 2 OUCH.</p>
        <p>"One Much 01 Nature makes the whole world kin I"</p>
        <p>Thus saith the sage, and sayeth well,</p>
        <p>Since it is so when homocea's rubbed in, For, unlike things made but to sell, 7/is salve of Naturetouches quick the spot, And, touching, soothes the aching part.</p>
        <p>Therefore be wise: rest not till you have,got A tin of homocea for that smart.</p>
