      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>1 NOTES ON GAMES AND I ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>1 NOTES ON GAMES AND I ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. ---- [By "Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>Warrington sustained their secoad home defeat on Saturday, and as on th 3 first occasion, it was at the hands of a team they were particularly aaxioui to beat. Brouzhtou Itan,gera seldom pro,e an "easy thing," and on Saturday thry were as still a lot as Warrington are likely to eLcounter for many a long day. They are not a brilliant team, and notwithstanding their frequent importations of internatnals frlin this country and that they do not attain to the all-round excellence and cleverness of their frien.is from Spindledom. It is a!waya weight, strength, and detetm uaiion that tell in their case. Warrington possess a fair amount of the latter quality but they are conspicuously lacking in the other t No. This fact, however, was not the sole cause of their defeat last Saturday.</p>
        <p>As usual they had only themselves to blame. Fate was kind to them in the sense that they wer- iavoured by the choice of positions on a day when such an advantage was of supreme importance. There was a toll Nizei gale cavorting rouad when the time for commencing hostilities arrived, and the winning of the toss was naturally a source of anxiety to players and spectators ou both sides. Warrington were ceith-ut their captain, bht his well known luck win the spin; of the coin re- mained behind. Ishersrood named the uppermost de- sign when the" brown" dropped and he quickly took up the position favoured by the elements. Warring had reason to feel glad, for with all their energies un- tried they had a glorious opportunity of piing up points for a full th•;ty-fl,,e minute-. The first few exchanges settled the wher:?abouts of play for the first half,and the Rangors quickly retire lto their own line. Then War. rinton's onsl4ught commenced.</p>
        <p>-- Beth forwards and backs lt,2pt at it warmly and it seemed a million to one that toe Bangers' citadel must fall. They were favoured by s.,lne luck, and exceeding hoseness by Warrington. Isherwood had bard luck in not profiting by the full back's blunder. 'Then the home threeqttarters exchanged passes iu the) tlovenliest fashion with the result that the hall was sent backwards instead of forward. Some time was wasted in driving the Rangers home again, but once on the line and the home team's chances came thick and fast. Barret the plump. dropped the oral right at the feet of the Warrington co. tres, both of wirmn went for it and prevented one another scoring. Jones appeared to blame in the matter. 'then Fish had the hanest of luck after a beautiful sprint, Hairy keeping him off the geouud when over the Line. So it went on and the tbifty-flv., minutes_; elapsed without any score.</p>
        <p>-- Warrington have the reputation of playing better against the wind than with it, and they maintained this reputation imme,-iiat..ly on changing ends- Toe forwards dashed up the fi2lut in magnificeat style, and soon made more progresi than ever the Rangers did. The play became exceedingly keen and exciting, while the tack- ling generally was of a fearful description. Time paK.ed rapinly and Warring ci:a quite held tdeir own. Jackson was watched closely, too closely it proved, for while th 3 home b icks were marking the " big un " Messenger slipp d over unobserved, and the game wan won. 'Twai bard lines, but one could not feel very sympathetic. Had the home team only play:id from the start with the dash they showed after hali-Aine, the Ringers would not have had alo :4; in. I will go so far as to say that had Warrington 13•. t the tosi they would have won, for the defence woJ,d have held gcol up to the interval. Afterwards the men would have seen clearly what they had t 3 do and they wJuld,havajdone IC.</p>
        <p>However, I must congratulate the Rangers on a vary praisevvrthy win. Good generalsnip and prompt, a3cep- Vance of chances won Ole game. They were not the bet- ter team, in fact they were the worst co3siderably in open forward play, lot:t the backs were sf_aand and reli- able at alt times. Individual brilliance was not wanted. Pair combination an I good judgment prayed sufficient Co overthrow Warrington, whi lacked an able, ex- perienced leader, atm were far fr,fn perfe3tly organiscd on the attack.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>OUR</head>
        <p>OUR</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL CO-MPETITION.</p>
        <p>(COUPON ON PAGE 7.) £5O Offered Weekly—Xioo Aggregate Prize.</p>
        <p>_ . RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION, THE PREMIER PRIZE WON AGAIN. PIE STRUCCLE FOR DIE £lOO. The following were the results of the matches in our eleventh Football Competition run in conjunction with the competi:on Key Notb. Fore.st beat Notts County. Bury beat Glossop, DIM, Derby County beat Stoke. Sunderland beat Blackburn Hovsrs. West Bromwich A beat liverton. Sheffield Wednesday beat Woolwich A., Walsall beat Chester- field, Broughton U. Seat Warrington, Cardiff beat Glouces- ter. Swansea beat Newport. The following competitors gave 1U correct results And tnorefore divide the £5O prize : Mr A Hooder, 7, Clyde-3treet, Ford, Devouport. Mr Mann, Moant Hill Lodge, Springfield-20cl, Chelms- lord.</p>
