      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>THEFT OF A SHIRT-.- WOMAN SENT TO GAOL,</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday. before Messrs. Birchall and R. T. Fairclough, Eliza Williams, Back Stamford-street, was charged on remand with steaiing a shirt, value 24 from the shop of Ttiam..s Fitz- patrick, general dealer, 19, ?inners Brow, at 5.45 on Tuesday ev ning.—The Chief Constable said that at the_ time in question, prisoner went to the shop of the prose cuter and offered s chair for sale. Fitzpltrick said that be did not buy sir gle chairs, and she went away Presecutor then went into another portion of the</p>
        <p>pr?mkes, leaving the shop with no one in charge. The woman was seen by a boy named Hughes to go into another shop and alterwards come back to the shop ot Fitzpatrick, pick no a shirt, put it under her shawl and go out. Flughes informedthe prosemtor and he followed ber. Prisoner was apprehended by Superint ndent DrlonaeLan. She said she was willing to pay for the shirt if the prosecutor did not have ht,r locked up. It appeared that the wcman sold the shirt to a man named Brown a labourer employed by a showman on the fair ground for sixpence. She said she was "hard up" mad wanted something to eat. She made a slight discrepancy there as it N., a; something to drink that she wanted. She had been before the Court 18 times before, seven times for theft. She was one of the most persistent 8114) lifters in tip town. She seld everything she could get to buy drirk.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for thiee months with hr.' labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.—Beforo Alderman WHOA (In the chair), Conucillor</p>
        <p>Smethurst. and Dr. Gornall.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For being drunk and disordorlv 5n Bridge-stre-t at 6.20 on Thursday evening, Mary Newton, adytligoe s-htae. was tined sa. WIFR DESERTION. —ln the case of 'flrnest George Chap- man, 47, Fender-row, Woodley, Stockport, versus Theresa Chapman, 18, Priestley-street.—Ar. A. Browne appeare t for Mrs Chapman and s,aid that defendant was summoned for deserting his wife and the matter was left in abeyance be- cause defendant, undertook to look after his wife. He had again left her and therefore they were compelled to take out another summons for desertion. I4e had gone t Birming- ham and he (Mr Browne) had received a letter from him man- senting to a separation order and offering to par 8s a week.— The order was granted, and defendant was alse ordered to pay a guinea advocate's fee. UNJUST SCALF.S.—Sarah McCoy, Pierpoint-street, was summoned for having unjust scales in her poosession on November 3rd.—She pleaded guilty.—lnspector Place said thet on the date in guestioo, Inspectors Wallington and New- all called at defendam's shop and found the pair of scales produced On testing them they found them to be an ounce out of balance against the purchaser.—Defendant said that a man came into the shcp with a sack of flour and broke the scales.—A fine of 2Cs including costa was Imposed, the s'ades to be forfeited.</p>
        <p>SEPARATToN ORDER ALTERED.—Joseph Rigby Vernon, 31. Pinners Brow, was summoned to show cause why a separa- tion order from his wife Blizabeth Vernon. made on the 2nd September. 1898 for 16s a week should not ba altered.—Mr A. Browne appeared for the complainant. —Mrs Vernon said she would se-apt 153 a week if ker husband would &amp;low her to have the shop.—Defendant said be could not possibly nay that amount.—The Magistrates subsequently made an order for 123 a week ; Mrs. Vernon to have the shop. THREATS —Archie Robinson, 99. Fairclough',avenne. Was summoned for making use of threats towards Hobert 'Natant, Wellington-ateeet, on November 4th. —Complainant gave evidence to the effect that de:ens:hint got a big piece of wood and threatened to dash his brains out —Defendant was bound over to keep the pone and ordered to pay the costs (10S). ASSAULT ON A evrEst-itf-TA.W. —Na'tbit R;Chlrlson, Ellesmere-strel, was summonel for aisaultinz Ann Richardson, her mothex-in-Law, on the 4th November,— Complainant stated that defendant hit her in the face. —The Chief Constal:le stated that he hid reeeived nu-norous com- plaints about defend tnt's conduct. Bilesmerf-street was made simply b,..11 by her conduct,—Defendant was fined lOs and Costs. WIFF Asektn.T.—David Buckley, 13, Iteg,nt street. was summoned for assaulting his wife, Emma Eine .ley, on the Bth inst.—Complainant stated that her h isband hit her on the head and ran her out of the bouse.—Cornplatuant said his wife neglected hina.—A. fine of 10e WAS Imposed. SATuzbAr. —Before _Messrs. Birekall (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>B. T Fairelouvh.</p>
        <p>INDECENT BEE( svioufi.—Mary Jane 'Mulvey ws corn• mitted to goal for fourteen days for Indecent behaviour in Wilson Patten-street at eight o'clock the previous night.</p>
        <p>110Finar.--Before Mavor (in the chair), Aldermen Sut- ton, ierancornt) and Fairclough, Councillors Smethu-s, Pierpoint and Shaw Green. Dr. Gorman. and Messrs. Hay.</p>
        <p>ward, Hepherd, and S Wright. THE Fumy PaleceNEN BeFoRE THE NEW MAYOR DI9- CHARGED.—Kate Mannion. 5, Bakehouse-yard. was charged with being drunk and disorderly at Bridge-foot at 5-45 on Saturday night —After hearing the evidence, the Chairman said it was an old-fashioned view that the first offence tric I before the new Mayor should be allowed to be dismissed with a caution. It was a view with whicti he entirely agreed. He hoped he would not have the unpleasant feature if seeing her there again on that or any other charge. OBSCEY.g IdsGUAGE —A SLIP OF TIE MOUTH. —ThOnlaa Bayley, 5, Town Hill, was charged with snaking use of obscene language in the Market Place at 4.40 on Saturday afternoon.—P.(l. CartPr proved the r .se —Prisoner said it was only "just a slip of the mouth '' that made him use bad language.--Fined lOs and costs or 14 days--Sarah Crool.s. 11 Albert Terrace, Howlri, was fined 5s and costs for making use of obseene language in Buttermarket-street, at 8.15 on Saturday night. DRUNEENN ESR,—Walter Wilson. Challinor-strcet. was fined Si and co-ti for being drunk And disorderly ;a Sankey-st., at 7.300 a Sata-day night. OBSTRUCTION—A WARRINGTON PAWNS ZOK ER FIN ED. Edgar Valentine Morton. Winwick-street, pawnbroker, was summoned for ohstructinz the footpath in 77::nwick-street the 18th inst by leaving tin bores and other articles thereon —Defendant did not appear bur. was represented by his manager Mr. Pye—P.C. Baxeadale sail that at 2.10 on Ihe afternoon of the data in question ha was on duty in win. wick-sti•eet and 0.. passing Mr. Morton's pawnshop he n tie d some furniture and abet articles on the footp .th. They re- mained there for two hours. Amongst the articles of furni- ture were a kitchen dresser, a large table, some bedstead!, and a number of kitchen chairs. He Called the attention of the assistant Mr. Pyc, to the matter, and he said he was going to send some of the things up Winwick-road. The goods cr:mapied a space slant tow* or five yards squa.e —The Chief Const able said that was the fifth time defendant had been summoned for obstruction. He was fimed 23s and cods in February.— The Chai man said the Bench were found to maintain the ri) ht of the Co:po,a- tion to the land, awl IR face of decisions already given they were Very much surprised and pained that the case should be brought before th m. Thee had no option but to fine defendary 20s and crists. They thought he had treated the Court with a little want of courtesy in not appearing. INDECENT BEliaviora.—Margar t Grimes, of no fixed address, was, on the e'sieflC Ol P.C's. Turner and Clough. Committed to prison for fourte-n days for indecent behaviour In Wilson-Patteu-street, oa Saturday niAlit.</p>
