      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>TUFSD y.—Before Mr. Birchall(in the chair), and Councillor</p>
        <p>Shaw Green</p>
        <p>A. PAR neut. an PILTPER.—THE FOOD WAR: NOT SUBSTAN- I TIAL.—JoI:n Smith was charged with being an inmate of the casual ward at the Warrington Workhouse and refusing to perform the task set him by tho rn‘ster o;t the previous day. —Mr. Basil Sheerer prssecutad on behalf of the Guardians. He sail that the man was admitted to the Wore house on Saturday and when put to work on Moncley morning would not do anything at all. He (Mr. Shearer) went to see him in the afternoon and he still refused to do anything' He add he did'nt go there to work and the food wee not good enough. He (Mr. Shearer) told him that he would have to go te got If he did not work aal he said " That will suit me for the time being."—Prisener said he did not think the toed was substentiat enough for any men to perform a task on. It was the first time he had been in such a elire. The master asked him if he cemd bre ek stones and he toll him that, he could not ea he had a catr.ra,A in his eye, He then se d can you elo anything else?" in snch a" bull-ragging "manner that he thought it would be best not to give him env civility Shearer : He refased to see a doctor.---•risoner : I have been in St. John's Hospital and aid not went to see arty doetors.—The Chairman : Why dient you de the evr;s ? - Prisoner • Dry bread Is not suostantiel enough for any men to do it on. I should not do it for eny man. —I he Chairmai;: You cannot expect very rich food.—Prisoner: Dry breed is not much substa ce.—Prinsuer was sent to gaol for fourteen days with hard labour. INDECRNT Bertsvi ;17R.—Por the above offence Annie Ash- all was sent to gaol for 14 days. A SENTENOR INCREASED.—Margaret Flannaghla Was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street, at 10-5 the previous night.—lt being her 22nd appearance she was committed to gaol for 14 days. —AA she was being -e- -moved from court she shouted to a witness I hope the Lord will stiffen you for the lie you to;d " whereupon she was brought back Lu the dock and sentenced to a month's im- prisonment. LONG TOM" WANTED IN DIAL STREET.—Thomas Knock- ton WAS charged with being drunk and disorderly hi Dial Court at 2.1 S that ntorning.—P.S. Williamson proved the eaae.—The Chief Constable said that WAS prisoner's 24th appearance. Dial-street had got unquiet again, and the police had to go tnere night after night. It hart begun to redeem itself for a time. hut a lot of old characters had e•-et back again and Indulged in the old et nduct. They would do with the Boer's "Long Tom" there for half an hour.—A fine of 6s and costs was imposed. WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. Geddes (in the chair) and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Birchall.</head>
        <p>Birchall.</p>
        <p>OCCASIONAL LICENSE.—James Standish of the '' Horse Shos " Inn was granted an oceasional liceese to sell at the Drill Hall on the occasion of WA, non-commissioned officer's ball on Monday next. DOMESTIC INFELICITY.—The adjourned case in which James Lawrenson was charged with assaulting his wite on the 14th Oetober. again came up for hearing.—Mr. T S. Steel (Jenkins and Co.) appeared for the complainant, and the defendant was represented by Mr a. Browne.—Mr Steel said that the cute was before the court four weeks ago. tie was not at that time Instructed in the case. An agreement had been prepared by Mr Browne, hut the terms of the agreement had not been carried into effect -and complainant intended to proceed with the case.—Mr Browns: You are being misled. The agreement has been signed by both parties so the terms have been arrived at but unfortunately, owing to the stupidity of e ‘mplainant and my client tha terms have not been carried out. The defendant went last Saturday to pay he money and they would not let him in. He went three times.—'4r Steel said complainant had been subject o annoyance from the man since the case was ad- journed. He went to the house and reqaested her to send for drink. She said she had not the money to buy food end on account of his conduct had to turn him out of the house. She hal r :fused to let him in. The arrangement was that the woman should call at Mr Browne'a ofibe for the money. She went on Monday last and was told that the agreement Was not signed and there was no money for her.—tr. Browne said it was arranged at his offlae that the defendant should pay his wife 13s a week.—Mr. Steel : The complainant signed the agreement on the 24th of last month, but so late as Mo aday last it WAS not signed by the defendant. I submit I can proceed with the charge of assault. The agreement has not been carried out and there- fore it is of no effect. This woman has litd no money for five weeks, and she has nine chi drea to maeatain.—Continuing, Mr. Steel said he was instructed that the defendant Was a drunken man and neglected his work. He had only been in the habit of giving his wife 10a. and 15s. a week. If a separation order was granted it shoola be granted by tae court.—The magistrates decided to adjourn the case for a week to see it the parties would come to ternes.—.lll, Birchall : It has been a perfect muddle. _</p>
        <p>r InDhCENT BEEWTIOUR.—For this oft3nee Mary Bran:Lett and Annie Burns were bent to gaol for 14 days.</p>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions. WEDNESDAY.— Before ?deems, ta. Cleanter (in the chair), C. Monde. and if. J. Bleckly. - -</p>
        <p>DRUSYMTNESA.--Jobnlbam FairciOugh and olin Haslarn were tined fis for being drunk and disorderly at Burtonwood on thetl,h inst. ELLrNG UNWEIGKED BRRAD.—Charles Drummond. Orford, was summoned for selling a two pound loaf without welizhing it on November Ist, C. Maitlartl and Insnector Sheppard proved the case and a fiae 01 53 and costs was im- posed. illiam earringtoa. Poulton. WAS tiaedSs and casts for a similar effence.</p>
        <p>QUARRELLING ABOUT THE BOERS AT CloLHcßNE.—Thomas Travis was charged with assaulting William N4den at Gol- borne. ea the llth inst.—He pleaded guilty.—Complainant . .</p>
        <p>raid that on the night of the dte 1.1 question he had a quarrel with defendant about foothill and defendant knocked him to the grontici and kicked him. The doctor had been attending him since and he had not been able to work. PAsoner said ha and complainant were ha7lng a row in a chip shop and co,nplainant challenged him out to fight. They were quarrelling about the Boers (Laughter )—Defendant was lined lOs and costa with the altsrul,ive of 14 days' im- prisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Sir Richard Brooke, Bar. (chairman), and Mr. John Ocklest on.</p>
        <p>SQuABBLIyG NiuGHBOURI.—A Ncial Ben OF Wolf EN Hama) Oven —Jane Cow tp was summoned by Mary Whittaker. tar using obscene laneeage towards her ort tee Bth of November, 1899.—Frern the ev device of a numb-r of witoesses on either side the Benc, conniticie both comelainant and defeeda .t had behaved in sueli a mariner as to justify them being bound over in £5 for six months to seep the ponce. and aloo ordered them to pay 103 6 I costs each.—Emily Hocknallomi Emily Shollcress, both of Howard-street, took o it cross-sum- monses for aiseult upon ea. th other on the 10th of Nove,nber Shtlieroas state a the bother bat :ween them had com- menced sever.) days prior to the 10th.but on that date it cu"-I minated in the defendant (Hocknall) striking her on the f.tee with her hand. She (witness) had a brush in her hand, and tbrretene4 to strike Brolly Hellman, only her condition pre- vented it.—Th.s story was borne out by Ma-the Houghton and Matilda Limburg. and admitted by the defeadant. —The latter, however, called Henrietta Bolton and Hannah Holton, to prove Mrs. Shillernss had threatined to strike her nti the head with a brush.—Betind over for six 'months in £5 and ordered to pay 103 Gd costs each. TaF. UN,DUCLTED.—RiahArd Robinson (2 easel) boats Timothy Garvow, Jackson's-lane ; James O'Niel. 18, Gran- v:lle-street; Twigg. Halton-r 'at; William Milling- ton. boa's; and Joseph Taylor (3 cases) b3sts were all ordered to pay ss. 'trate:ling costa for Irglooting thl edqoi- tioa of their sere-al children.--Til the coarse ot Chi heertn•Y • Mr. J. W. Lighborn (clerk to the Runaorn School Board) said It was all very ve..all for the people to suppose thee co I'd get the children to work their boats for the payment of the Sc fiaa,brit they wol'i be summ3ned so frequently that it would soon be worth their while to sett i the children to school. TEIEFT oF UNDERCLDTHING AT RUNC )11N.—Aary Glutton, married overman. who dwelt with her husband on the b tat. was charged w th stealing a chemise, the property of Emi y Finney, on or ahout the 4th of Oct ;b-r —tCrnity Fieney Waterloo-road, Remcorn, stated on about, the middle of September lest she made a number of chemises of whlch,the one (prodoced, she Ideatifiert as one of the five she made About five weeks ago she missed the artic'e from her washing, and. on Tutsuay lest from what her daughter told her she wmt down to a neighbour's haute where she found the naissln artiaie.—Mre. Cavannah, the neighbour referral to, said she washed for both parties. On the 2nd of November last Prisoner -brought a bundle of elothes to the "wash." On the 7th of November she untied them and washed them, putting them out in the yard to dry. Mrs. Cavannah's daughter happening to come i *to the yard Paw the chemise upon a step ladder and identified it as her mother's. She was sure it was prisoner's bundle of clothes that the chemise came out of.—Mand Cavannah,deposed to seeing the underclothing as silted and telling her mother of it.—Con-table Harry Fry deposed from information received he went and asked prisoner about the chemise, and she told him she had pro- cured it from a little shop near the De:ph Bridge, somewhere about May. She had Oven 2s 61 for it. She said she would show hint the shop and leek him to Ars Rizabeth Siddley'a in Pennington-r aw. Mrs. Siddley said she knew nothing about the chemise and had not sold prisoner one. The Constable then took he- to the Police Si.ati in and charged her with the theft but she replied ' I never stole it ; I g)t it from the little shop,"—Mrs. ltiizabothi Siddley, corroborated the Constable's statement. She had kept a shop as des- cribe Iby the priemer from July 1898 to July 1899 in Pen- raineten-Itne. ls,it had never slid or seen the chemise pr clue-d. —Prisoner eleete I to be summarily dealt with, and pledged gallty.-3aperinteadent D Pearson intimated the prosecntrix had no wish to press the case and th re woe no previous coevictien S.aaling az tinst he--Prisoner wit hound over in 40s ti areirier for judgment if cilia.' udon within the next six months.</p>
