      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington v. Broughton Rangers.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of these two clubs took place on the Wilderspool ground on Saturday,amid surroundings the reverse of asant. "Tnere was a aale of wirri prevail- ing, and the air was bitterly cold, but the fact that the rain had ceased made football more endurable for spec- tators and players than had appearad probible earlier in the day. Both t ,am s were strongly represented and there was equal determination to win on both sides. Warrington were without J. T. Bate,their captain,whose knee was stated to be unfit for the game. Abut 4,000 spectators assembled, ani a cheer went up as it Was seen that Isberwood had won the toss for the homosters. 'fir, teams lined out as follows :</p>
        <p>Warriagtol : Boscow, full back; Fish, Jones, Isher- wood, aad Harris thre; quarters : Duckworth and As- burst, half backs ; Tas lor, Naylor, Wirrison, Blardman Lien, Morrywew:her, Swift and Schol:z forwards. Broughton Rangers : F lltrry, full back; Jackson, Barratt. Walsh, and Dam 'n, threequartt.rs ; Mesenzer and Nelson, half hacks ; Tilompson, Whitens td, Stick- ney, Clemincon, Winskill, Lamb, Robinson, and Wolfen- den, forwards.</p>
        <p>Broughton grafted the game in the teeth of the gale, and a couple of punts by Duckworth and 13')PCOR7 carried play to the line. Som, keen play followed, Warrington trying hard to get through the d:Uenee. Duckworth tried to get between Messenger ani Nelson, but toe lattorcaptured him and play remained in the same place. The Brotihton backs kicked for touch and each time Boscow judiciously dropped the ball wider the goal. Once Harry mulled a ci.tiM and Ltierwoo I ut3 its tiino to ground but the hall was in touch by the merest trifle. Ashur,t neat sent a bad pas; which Barran very cleverly intercepted and tempor- arily raised ttie seige. The same player was at fault a moment later and pre -onto i Isherwood and Jonos with the simplest of chanced, which they both went for and both missed. The groan of dis s ppointment hid hardly died down before there wae a huge cheer as Fish took the ball from Dackworth an] dodged his way over the line, only to be hell up by Harry. This as the hardest of luck. Broughton made a brief visit tl centre, but were sent back bv Morri3on. Warringto pissed the ball along and Fi .11 had a nice chance when Jones traniferrei clinniily and the wing man just lost the second neees- wary to take him over. He was barely kept our, but it was sufficient for Bronghton. The Rangers had done practically no'hing bat tackle, when Jackson secured and contributed a glorious run from the line to mid- way whore superiority ii numbers proved too m :eh fir him... Their stay was of the usual brief durition. Fish</p>
        <p>tackling Barratt near his line. Harry conc9ciezl a five yards acrimmaze where the Ringera were pmaliied. Fish to)k the kick and f alai from the easiest p-siinte place. Warrin.gton foeinol doom, dto dialopointmenr, tin WAS friltered away and the interval arrived with a blank score sr-mt.</p>
        <p>Taylor resumed. and if the R angers thought they were going to have all their own way they were mistaken, as the home forwards quickly took up the attack. Play rule:l even for a time, but Messenger and Nelson soon established th€inselves in the home quarter. Barrett made several good openings for the lengthy Jacksnn,but Harris watched his b',g foe rp'endidly. and he never travelled far. Tee game wsxed hotter anl !inter, each aide straining every nerve. In th open Warrington were very quick, but it was next to impossible to make mach headway against the wind. The forwaris followed the ball lilts hounds and Harry was =Sit with more promptitude than rratletiess. The home te6.m were fie,hting a forlorn hop) however and when one of Broughton's rushes camel play to the line one expected a score. The b ill came out on the visitors' sicle,all eyes went to Jackson, and Messenger feinted cleverly and scored easily. It was a good *try, but too far out for Jackson to improve upon • E:en now Warrington did not give up and with Bescow as safe as" houses" they made headway again. E len broke away and the bill was carried to the Ringers' line. Warrington made a dalo-rate effort, the ball travelled quickly,and Jones eet as far as the corner flag but no further. Broughton kicked powerfully back and the gam) concluded in the home ha'f. Final : Broughtoa Rangers/. try (3 point-) Wa-riegt cq. Runcorn V. Millont</p>
        <p>Mtthin paid thsir first visit to Hui-icon:l on R4turda7, and wEle welcomed by about a thousand spectators and a rain, hail and wind storm. Both tetras were representa- tive, and the elements were in favour of Millom the first half. and 'hey found the Runcorn defence full employ. tnent. Throughout they playrd with a dash and a vi.glur that was ,turpriinv,,contddering the lowly positi -tu th.* Club t.ccapiei in he Lancashire CompAilim. Toe fi Ming of the Curnh,rland men was clean, Flntrt, and eff c ive,and thrir defensive play in the second hallo gainst half a hurricane, and a powerful attack, wai a sp:endid fsature of the tame. During the secancl half Millom pl yel five three-quarters, Buckitt corn,nz from the tor- w 4rd rstiki,and he was the most pr uninent man on the fl 11, althoue.h he has played in firr-class football for closi an sixteen years. The game was well c,.ntfs'ed throu,,,,hout, and Runcorn recoived a feght.</p>
        <p>Runcorn started ayainst a strong wind and brilliant sun, which wal quickly hidden. Millom attacked with , vi2our, Houghton being compelled to touce down. Warder driehlei well, bul the ball went into , touch, and from the kick out, Jolley got ,1 plase,sien and made a dashing run, heing thrown into touln at the corner. Milk) n forwards rushed to centre where Whi-ehead picked up in a locos scrimmage and passed to Nerthnote, who tricked licughton, and had a clear ru ain behind the posts. Buckitt added the goal. Houghton now ar ailed enthusiasm by a grand run, but retu-ned to the attaek. Butterworth relieved 1.'9 a sma-t run, and pacs but L 'thorn put Millom again on a good position by a run along touch. Walker gained much ground by a bautiful dribble ani after some neat pa.ssing Warder do iged several opponents and scored at the corner, Hoirentoe's shot at god striking the upright. Rancorn now prassed and when the interval arrived were on Milken line. Turning round Itineorn had the advantage of the wind, but Millon from the first five minutes kept play in Runcorn quarter. Jolley startAd an attacking movement, which was repelled. The same player crossed the line a inMute later, but the ball was scrimmaged and Millom rushed play to centre. Millom were penalised for off. side, and Houghton missed the goal from an easy plaee. Runcorn now tried all the moves on the board, but thry were met by a clever and sound defence, although the Millom men were facing a downpour if hailstones. Then Faulkner executed a go id mow-merit at the scri n- maee, an 1 handing to Butterworth, the latter dart-cl over at the corner, putting Runcorn ahead be a point. The kick at goal was a failum A couple of -dead balls now were orreliPeri to Runcorn and Jolley tried a drop at goal ,the ball falling wide. Rencorn ha I now got the meoser of their onporients and a good past.ing m rnent encb'ed C. Butterworth to score a neat trv, Houghton again f4ilti! to pilot the boll over the bar. The game ended Runcorn three tries (9 points). Millom one goal one try (five points). Swinton v. Oldham.</p>
        <p>An immense crowd witoefrvd this contett at Swinton, the yisi-ora gaining. a vict ni by a goal awl a try to a goal. S,m Lels landed a goal from a free kick for Old- ham, and Dtyi4 gained a try. For the losers Goalman landed a P plaid d goal.</p>
      </div>
