      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>NATAL IN 1850.</head>
        <p>NATAL IN 1850.</p>
        <p>At that time, however, the sphere of public acti- vity in Natal was as contracted as it could be ;at any rate in Durbin, the seaport. The little inland towa of Pietermaritzburg was the seat of govern- ment, and the centre of official authority,but on the roast signs of administrative activity were almost imperceptible. The collector of customs acted as magistrate, and he was suppotted by a badly paid person in plain (and very shabby) clothes, known, politely, as a policeman. At first there was no place of COLlfillernerit for prisoners, but ere long a tiny cottage was secured as a gaol, Its walls were built of clay and twigs, and c3uld easily he broken through by the hands of, an enterprising inmate; but the rigours of existence there were alight and escapes were rare. Unruly captives were clapped into the stocks, or handcuffed, while the certainty of a flogging if caught again acted as an effective deterrent upon efforts for liberty. Munici- palities and juries were all unknown. Postal facilities came first as acts of grace on the part of the Worthy old Baron who was good enough te receive and to dispease the correspondence of the community. Commerce was transacted in an easy and dignified fashion which mocked any thought ot vulgar eompetition. Storekeepers—there were no" shops "then—were gentlemanly an" friwndly persons, who did no disdain to exchange for coin or kind anything that their motley stocks might include,whether food, hardware,or dress. Wrapping paper and twine being as yetsuperfluities,purchases were carried away in canisters, baskets, or bags by the grateful buyers. Civilisation in its rudimentary Fettoes implies unconstrained equality, artless con- fidence, and cheerful content,. It is sad to think how soon these qualities disappear a, the community advances, never to exist again. it is something, ' fifty years later, to feel that one has witnessed lite under such pri Litive, if not Arcadian conditions, and to know from actual experience that it is possible for European men to live, not unhappily, with so little to help, to guide, to serve, or to equip them in the struggle of existence. One's fait ein human nature is strenethened,one's disdain of mere conventionalism is Quickened by the memories of those early days.—From " South African Reminis- cences" in the " Ccrnhill Magazine" for November</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Warrington Burgesses.</head>
        <p>Warrington Burgesses.</p>
        <p>The analysis of the new burgess roll issued by the Town Clerk shows the total number of bur- gesies as 10,125, and the number of vwerm for each Ward as follows ;—Town Hall Ward. 1,032 ; White- cross Ward. 1,080 ; Bewsey Ward, 758; Orford Ward, 1.031 ; St. John's Ward, 1,633 ; Fairfield Ward, 1.133; llowley Ward, 1.113; st. Austin's Ward, 1050; Latchford Ward, 1,286 ; total, 10,125. Tue number of electors on the Parli tment &amp;try lteg s- tar, including the towa,hip nf Latchford Without and Winwick-with-Hulme is 9 360.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>!KILLED AT WALTON JUNCTION</head>
        <p>!KILLED AT WALTON JUNCTION</p>
        <p>NOT AN INQUIRY FOR A WARRINGTON JURY. Mr. Brighouse has something to say.</p>
        <p>In the Parade Kenn at the Poece Stetion,Wareington, on Mereley forenoon, Mr. S. Brigueuse, county coroner, conducted an iequiry ceaceraine tee death of a erek -s- -, elan teamed George Lloyd, of M nli Jeweils, Flintshire, who was killed on the Lon lin and Nerte-Weetera Rail- way at Waltou Old Junction early on Friday cf lest weak, brirfly renereed in our last iasue—elr, reoeke, statientnaster, Warringtora, representel tee L ,ndon and Nerth-Western Railway Company, and Mc. Philips of Ceester. the Great Western Railway Company. The Coroner said that neither he nor thejury hal any right to be put to the trouble of hceding this inquest. This peer follow was killed at Walton in the county of Cheshire,rsearly two miles from the Warrington borough boundary, and the L nadon end North-Weetern Railway Company would have to explain to him sooner or later why they brought him out of Cheshire into Lenciseire. It should have been a case for the Coeihire coroner and jury. Where a fatality occurred a few yards from the boundary a mistake mieht be exeusable, but this acci- dent happened two miles from the boundary, and the man was then brought to the Patten Arms Hotel. Mary Lloyd, of 44, Mold Junction, Flintshire, said the deceased was her hueband. He was 60 years of age,acd was a brakosmao. She last saw him alive on Thursday night at nine o'clock. Tee Coroner said that no one seemed to have seen this accident. It was reported that the deceased werkcci tne 9.50 p.m. goods train from Mold Junction, which arrived ec Walton Old Junction about ten minutes to two on Friday incoming. Deceased there discharged two waggons and gave the signal to the driver to go for- eeerd. Nothing further was seen of hien until Philip Chilton, a brakesman, arrived at the junction with the 3.25 train and bound the deceased lying in the six foot. The back of his head was injured. The man ex smined him, and found that life was extinct. The body was then removed to the Patten Arms Hotel at Warrington. It was supposed that the decease:l after desp stoning the waggoes gave the signal to the driver to procred,and was going back to his van when he was struck by the engine of a Great Western goods train. The engine was examitied at Chester, but no marks were found upon it. Owen Jones Williams, eneine driver in the employ of the London arid Nortn-Western Railway Company, said the deceased was the brakesman elites eeeds train. The train left Mold Junction at 9-50 on reursday night and arrived at Walton Old Junetion,about a mile from Bank Quay, at ten past two on Friday morning. Deceased had been employed on tne reitway ferty years. When the train arrived at Waltoe. he took two waggons off, and having hooked the engine to the remainder gave the signal "right away." He was then standing by the waggons in the six foot. There was a red light at the signal bee ah,ad, and witness remained stationary for about fifty minutes. Deceased had of course, ample time t get hick to his van. He (witness) had drawn ahead aoout a train's length, where one of the shunters asked him where the brakesman was. He replied that he was in the van. Tee shunter said that lea was not and added that a man was lying lead on the bank. Wit- ness went to the spot and found ths deed body of the brakesman. Witness remembered that two or three minutes after deceased had given him the signa: to pro- ceed a Great-Western train paesed on the up line. It , was a dark night. Philip Henry Chilton, brakeman in the employ of the cempany, said he arrived at Walten Old Junction with ' a goods train at 3-25 on Friday morning. He had to , change engines, and while riding on the step of one he saw a body lying in the six feet. He went to the sienal trix and a young fellow want back with him. They examined the body and found that life was extinct. Deceased was lying on his back and had svide.ntly been killed by a passing train. In answer to the Coroner, Mr. Cooke, the Warrington stationneasteesaid the night stationmaster had the body brought to Warrington. Ti.AB Coroner thought the officials should know that they had no right to bring the body to Wearington. Mr. Cooke observed that it would heve been a uifficult matter to get it off the railway to any other place. The Coroner: That may be, but there is a law and it must be obeyed. There are a hundred reasons why it should be obeyed. It is fir better that a jury in the locality should inquire into the cause of death. Then the juries ought to have their laborers equally divide!. A , Warrineten jury has no right to inquire into a Cheehire case and vice versa. The point, however, is that the law says a body should not be removed. You have done it and it cannot be helped, but don't do it again • Alexander Marshall, engins driver, in the employ of the Great Western Railway .Company, said he easeA Walton Old Junction on Friday morning at 2 25. lee recollected seeing a train on the down line. He saw a man standing by the waggons. At Chester he received a message to the effect teat a man had been lelled at Walton, presumably by his engine. He examined the engine but found no marks upon it.—The Coroner : There might be no mark even if the engine did strike the men ?—Witness : Yes.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death." . The Coroner (addressing Mr. Ccr,ke), said he was not finding fault, hut hewould like to co Ivey to the officials the information that if he had carried out Elia strict legal duties in a matter of this kind he wuuld hive cent the body back to the place it had come from. Of course by the courtesy of coroners who kad districts adjoining hia they had agreed, rather than give pain and sorrow to the relatives in eases of this kind, to break the law and hold the inquiry. Otherwise it might create a scandal which vroaald reflect not upan them, but upon the rail- way company. Mr. Cooke said this seemed to }VIVI bell a case of in- advertence, and he could premise that it would not occur again. The men seemed to have thought that the best plan was to bring the body to Warrinegin instead of carryihg it to some distant place in the country.</p>
      </div>
