      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>?..tttcrs to the 'Q.7,3ttor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended frr putdication must in al cases send their names and addresses to the Editor. atters intendr d for publication must be writt, n on one side only of the paper. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>The Political Millennium in Warrington. Sir,-1 hasten, with your kind p:rmission, to correct a mistake on my part. My tetter to you of the 4ta inst was written from memory, and in it I attributed to Mr. Councillor John Crosfleld words which he did not utter. I beg to apologise for the error. It was Mr. Alderman Sutton who, at the joyous luncheon connected pith the new Police Building, declared that the Mil- lennium had come to Warrington. I have always respected the worthy Alderman as a straight- forward and cousistent Couserva'ive and great was my surprise, therefore, to find him jubita- ting over the advent of a radical Mayor. It I rnem- ber rightly, there were some very sudden and start ling conversions in the Council Chamber when the great Water Bill was in hand, and it may be that the p )litical milennium has been brought about by similar changes of opinion. To outsiders like my,elf it is only given, to imagine, not to know, the powerful influence for good that Tories and Rauicals can exert up ,n one another when their delitx rations are not disturbel by indepen- dent and truth seeking minds. The outcome of the new millennium should be watched with great interest.— Yours truly, Novembzr 13, 1899.</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>CYCLING AND SOCIALITY.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND SOCIALITY.</p>
        <p>[BY " QUILP Nr]</p>
        <p>We are a stolid, stupid nation ; at least, so we are given to understand, and much more ; by our vola- tile French neighbours. Stolid, we certainly are, but stupid—well, we do manage to come our On top, so to speak, don't we? 'this is not the subject on whichl have to talk to you about, but I'm not a bit ashamed, and shall not make the slightest apology ; in fact—this is in coatidence you know— I'd say a lot more in the sums strain if it was'nt for the editor. I have already commi•ted myself to an expres- sion of opinion, in regard to our national tempera- ment,—ie., that we are a stolid race. But you and I know that there are oecasioue when this stolidity of Ours can be " skimmed " off us, like cream from milk, exposing what. we may call, the more useful article oeneath. These occasion 4 are when we have got to face trouble,—don't be alarmed, I'm not going to wander into leeperialistic sentiments—and when a convivial crowd of fellows get together severally appreciating each other. The other night I went to a smoking conceit in connection with the 'lf arriugton Cycling Club. Rh? Oh, no; I don't wish to make any secret of it; Monday night it was and, of course, the event came off at the Patten Aims, the headquarters of the club. Now. I kmew that the members were a— well, sensible iot of fellows—but I was hardly pre- pared for the reality. There is often one feeling lacking in a cotelie of this kind, and its absence makes all the difference between a successful or- ganieation—l am speaking now in regard to good feeling—and the otter thing. Amongst the mem- bers of the Warrington Cycling Club the best feel- ing obtains—the true spirit of comradeship—and it necessarily follows that they are a gentlemanly set. In the company of a fellow-pressman I entered the room dedicated to the gathering and when my eyes had got accustomed to the smoke I made out that there was a large number present, well sptinkled with red jackets. she room was of good size, but in face of the constant arrivals one began to have doubts of its capacity to hold them. We are told by some eerious persons that all things have their end. I am a cautious man and would not like to tie myself down to tt at statement, but at any rate, an end came to the arrivals. The first fact I ran up against was that " Ps " Davies was in the chair. When no particular pro- gramme has been decided upon on such occasions as this the chairmaa may call upon you or me for a song or anything else by means ot which we may achieve notoriety. If you have no desiro to dis- tinguieh yourself in any way ane are an unassum- ing man, you begin to wiiegle in your seat, make lame apologies and endeavour to evade the eye of the chairman. All this is indisputable evidence that you do wish to do something and therefore more pressure is put on by the company, and aulees in- spieation in the form of an excuse comes to your t aid you are lost—provided always you are conscien- tious in your desire not to seek publicity. This is all by the way, however. There was nothing of this kind to give anyone uneasiness, for an excellent programme had been prepared, em- bracing song- (humorous, sentimental, and patrio- tic), recitations, &amp;c, But the surprising pare of it was that the artistes were chiefly members of the club. All alike—l don't mean to mention any ,ieames, here—were to be congratulated on their "efforts to entertain the company. I should say that to join a club made up of :etch manly members would be the beet thing one could do to bring out any latent sccial abilities. Before I bile been long in the room I was shown a large case containing a handsome set ol—silver, I think—cigar, cigarette, and other cases for presen- tation to some one in the company. Now I under- stand why one of the worthy secretaries, Mr. E Pickavance, junior, was so—er, guilty looking. He had done nothing bad I assure you, except, work unselfishly for years in the cause of the club, cyclists, and cycling generally. Had Iby any fortunate chance been in his position, I should have posi- tively beamed with guilt. When the interval came the presentation was made. There were speeches made too, all express- ing thanks to the donee for his unflagging efforts in the cathe of cycling, but —without any reflection on the speakers—one felt they were all insufficient to express fully the feeling in which Mr. Picka- Vance is held by his brother cyclists. It is alwaye a trying moment for a man when he has to acknow- ledge such remarks, but he did it simply, naturally and in terms which were understood. The evenieg's programme was continued with unabated zest, though one could not but admire the restraint the members exercised in some directions. One member had with much thoughtfulness selec- ted as a recitation Rudyard Kipling's stirring poem "The Absent minded Beggar." lt was given with both vigour and expression, and at the conclusion when the reeiter did " pass the hat" it was made the target of &amp; perfect fusilade of silver. The coine, it is nee lle,S to say, will go to swell the local fund for the iieservist3' WireS and Families. The amount collected was £4 12s. Gil. and the sum was male into £5 by a gentleman who in- sisted on his ironic being kept secret. I don't know who it is, but I have it strong suspicion. One an- nouncement was made in regard to the collection which was received with great applause—the ser- vant girls had all contributed to it. The dear girls! How they love a reel coat. The evening rite happily concluded by the sing- ing of " Rule 'anti the National Anthem; the verve with which the choruses were taken up was enough to drive a Little Englander wild with frenzy. flOwevPr, the niemberA of the " arrington Cycling Club are to be congratulated on having the right ideas of what is 13.r..,per in regard to sociality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BULAWAYO EXPLORATIONS.</head>
        <p>BULAWAYO EXPLORATIONS.</p>
        <p>The run on Itholesian prrernes is one of the mcst etr king incidents otthe tinanciai ear. We usdeistand that the ino:4 pr:pular undertalng juit n-,w is the Buts- way* Exploration C, mpa.ny,whoP.e £1 shares an quoted at a prem um, as well they may be considering toe'x- tent and value of its prlpe,tie,on one al'ino of which— tha Shamrock and Rose of Starol—provad and de- v"loPed gold roe ever-10 for tiearly th,ee and a I a,lf moes,averaging 18cwt, of gold to the t,n of ore through- out. This makes h, char's look elmo at £5. and wet founded rumocr prf-dics diuble this figure at an early date. That this will b reachel in the present humour of the market there is Pvery reason to I:lieve, and a purchaso of sh,uel new may easily result in a small fortune fur the tucklv p.osiP=sar of them. But, on the principle of th,. early bird, he or she who buys to-day will do better tham the laaga,d wh•-, ry,rries in when the price is a pound or two hivier.—Whztehall Review.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Tragedy— Funeral of The Victim.</p>
        <p>At eleven o'clock on rift. urday morning, the re- mains of the late Jame,' Poole. who dild from injuries alleged to have been it,flicted upon him by Thomas Kinsey ,40, Wellington-street, were interred in the Warrington Cetnettry. In crier that the service could be conducted a, quietry as posAble,the body was taken to the mortuary at the Cetnetsry on rhursday evening. A number of deceased's re- lative were present at the funeral, the Rev J W Dickinson cfilciating. Thera were no wreaths. P S Hollingworth and P 0 Corbett attended on behalf of the police.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly me-elin-g-of the Llarlestown and District P.S.A. was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Sunday. Mr. J. Halton presided a-id there was a large attendance. The principal speaker was the Rev. W. 0. Evans of Birkr:nhead. Vocal items were rendered by Miss Woods and re- citations by Mr. W. Morris. Miss Toombs presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Hope of Earlestown Lodge a public limelight exhibition was given by Brother Francis P C T in place of the usual weekly meeting entitled " A tour through Ireland," the slides for which had been kindly lent. Som, splendid views were shown of the various place of interest around Killarney, together with some local views, views in North Wales, and a view of the last publ,chouie in White Coppice, near Chorley, whioh is one of the few places in England enjoying prohibition , and has neither policemen nor pauper owing t) the action of Bro Eccles, P G treasure?, th owner of the place. Sister Francis gave the descriptive read- hags of various places. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, NEWTON.</head>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>in connection with the above and in ad of the school restoration fund, the Sacred Cantata " Captures of Babylon." was given in the school- room on Sunday by the Church choir, under the control of Mr. A. Knight the organist. This is the second occasion within 12 mouths on which this cantata has been given by the same choir. On the last occasion the weather was unfavourable. and there was not a large audience, and a tequeAt was made for a repitition. On Sunday,there was a large audience, and the various vocal items comprising the cantata were exceptionally well given, and thoroughly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN' CHITROM The annual special:services in connection with the Wesleyan Foreign Mission were held in the Wes- leyan Chapel, Earlestown on Sunday. The special preacher arranged for was the Rev Graham Bell, a returned missionary from Honduras, but owing to illness he was unable to be present. Consequently the Rev P R Passmore, resident minister, occupied the pulpit at each service. On Monday evening the annual missionary meeting was held in the chapel, when there was a very fair attendance, Mr. J Bate of St. Helens presided, and he wos supported by the Revs G Lunn of Prescot and F R Passmore Toe incidents of the Missionary address shonld have been fixed round Honduras,but owing to the absence of the Rev Graham Bell, the Rev J Lunn gave the principal address, dwelling upon.his own residence as a Missionary in the West Indies. The recital of various incidents connected with his lab Ours as a Missionary were most interesting, and he succeeded in rivetting the attention of his hearers, though- out his address. At the close a very cordial vote of thanks was accorded the rev gentleman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Six MONTHS FOR A WIFE-BWATER. At St. Helens on Monday, John Hart,coliter, 13, John- son.street, Park-road,was sent to prison for six months' hard labour for an aggravateJ asiault on hi,. wife. The charge originally was one of unlawful wounding. Toe offence was committed just after midnight on October 21.. The wife yesterday was very relucr..ant to give evi- dence. Mr. H. L. Riley, who defended, appealed to th- bench not to send the man to the asszes, and pointed out that the drunken conduct of tho wife 'mad caused him to decide upon giving her a good thrathing. The Vench agreed to reduce the wounding to one of common assault, and S9nt him to prison as stated above.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING DEATH AT A COLLIERY. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner. held SG inquest at, the Griffin Inn, Peasley Cross, St. Helens, on Monday night, on the body of Peter Garvey, aged twenry-one, of 41, Wright-street, who died on Saturday in the St. Helena Hospital, after being run over by three loaded coal waggons at the St. Helens CDllTries. Mr. J. 0. Swift represented the colliery cimpiny.—Robert Walker, foreman shuntlr at the Grove's Collie!y, said that on Saturday morning the regur brakeiman did not come to work, and Garvey was pot to the work He told him to take tour loaded waggons down an incline to the machine, about four hundred Tads away. Gar- vey started off, riding on the waggons. It was nece!- sary for him to ride, and that was the ordinary way of doing the work. John Penney, a youth, spoke to seeing the man under the waggons. Three of them ran over him, mangling his left leg and shoulder.—Joseph Naylor, engine driver, said decensel told him the brake •• over- got" him, and he slipped 6ff.—The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death"</p>
        <p>BURNING FATALITIES AT Sr. HELENS. Mr. Brighouse, county cproner, held an inquest at Peas ley Crois, St. Helens, on Monday right, on the body of Edwin Shields, son of William Shields, labourer, of 31, Victoria-street, Gerrard's Bridge, wllo died in the St. Helens Hospital on Saturday night after being shockingly burned early in the day. Mrs. Shields saids he was nursing a sick ctrld, and after she had fallen asleep on Saturday morning ceased and a brother, aged nine, got up and made the fire. They lit a candle, and the little fellow's night- dress caught fire. He was very badly burned when he got to him.—A verdict of" Accidental death "was re- turned.—The inquest on the body of Emma Forrest, the three year old daughter of Joseph Forrest, of 32, Clock Face-road, who died on Friday mornin, fr,m the effect.; of burns, was held at the Bull and Dog Inn, Marshall's Cross, St. Helens, on Monday. The evidence showed that on tbe Bth inst. the uhild went into a neighbour's house with her sister. She lit a pirce of paper, and her clothes caught firs. She Was badly burned about the head and arms, and died two days later. A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. R. BRIERLEY, NEWTON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. R. BRIERLEY, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>It is with sincere regret that w have to chronicle the death of Mr. Richard Brierly, Sanitary Inspector to the Newton Urban District Council, which took place rather unexpectedly on Wednes- day morning at his residence, Crow-lane, Newton, at the age of 61 years. Exactly four years ago 10,4 Monday the deceased gentleman was found in hisl office at the Town Hall, suffering from'an apoplectic seizure, and it was fully six months afterwards before be was able to resume his duties. since then he has had other attacks, but it is a peculiar fact that all the seizures have occnrred on a Monday, and during the present month lie has had three attacks, being under the influence of the last 48 hours, subsequently succumbing at the time stated. About 35 years ago Mr. nrierley came to Newton from Warrington, to succeed a Mr. Poulson, as Gas Engineer and Road Surveyor under the Newton Improvement Commissioners. Newton-in-Makerfield at that time was a perfectly rural spot, and as it grew,so Mr. Brierlev's responsi- bilities increased. He was appointed Clerk and Surveyor to the Commissioners, and he saw very many changes in the constitution of the Board and many improvements in the physical nature of the district, not the least being the remarkably rapid growth. For some time he had a olnsiderable private practice as an architect, and all the building plans were drawn under his supervision. By an order of the Coramisiioners, however,he had to give up this practice, and devote all his services to the township. He continued in office a.,4 Clerk and Surveyor, up to the introduction of the Parish Councils Act, and on the completion of the Earles- town Town Hall, the business of the townships was transferred there from Newton. Mr. W. W. Shirley was appointed clerk, and Mr. Brierley retained the position of surveyor and gas and water engineer. Owing to failing health,. Mr. Brierley was induced to give up the duties of surveyor, some few months ago, and la e was re- tained as Sanitary Inspector, a post he held up to the time of his death. He was a prominent Free- mason, an ardent Churchman, and a Cmservative. He leaves a widow and family of five grown up sons, and two daughters. The funeral will take place on Saturday afternoon at Newton Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Good Templary.</head>
        <p>Good Templary.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the " Temperance Mis-ion" lodge, of the 1.0.G.T. was held in the Academy-street Mission Room on Wrdnesday. Dur- ing the ciurse of the evening, it. was announced that Bro. Briers had received instructions to rejoin his regiment on Monday and be in readiness to pro- need to the Transvaal. It was decided to express the lodge's regard and hope that he would come back safe and sound and to furnish him with a list of lodges in the regiments now on active service.</p>
        <p>ALL COUNTRY PROPLR should read The Bural W)rld id. weekly, which contains articles on Horses, Cattle, the Dairy, Poultry, Bees, Rabbits, Pigs. Gardening, Natural History. Botany o! the Farm and Garden, the Nome, Foreign and Colonial Intelligence, Answers to Correspondents, etc. It is profusely illustrated, and is, altogether, a most interest- ng and instructive farming and country paper. A good medium for servants requiring situations. The Manager offers to send a specimen copy free. The address Is 110 and 111 STALND. LOll4Ol. W.C.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 17, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 17, 1899.</p>
        <p>was sure they would meet all demands made upon th ,m. (Applause.) . _</p>
        <p>Au List ie pniuse 'halting of hands the men sang the '• Soldiet3 of the Queen " and divcrsed to their several employments.</p>
        <p>THE NEWTON HUSSARS AND THE WAR At a meeting of the "C" troop (Newton) Lancasnire Hussars held at the Pied Bull Hotel, Newtpn on Monday evening for the purpose of seeing how many members of the troop wJuld volunteer for active service, the names IA 27 troopers were handed in, and it was decided to make an offer of their services to the War Office. Of course the offer is contingent upon the Government de- fraying the expense of sending the men to the front.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>On Suaday mornirig the Rev. J. Gunnell preached on the words " But I confes unto thee that after the way which Llvy call heresy, so worship I the God of my fathers, believing all thinas which are written in the law and in the proohAs " (Acts xxiv. 14). The pulpit at the evening service was occupied by the Rev. T. Bishop, whose subject was " The Hidden Treasure." The col- lections at each service were in aid of the Trust Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS. Joubert Reported Killed in Action.</p>
        <p>DURBAN, SUNDAY, 7.35 p.m.</p>
        <p>The " Times of Natal" putlislies a telegram from Lourenco Marques stating that General Joubert was killed in action on Thursday. A Laffan's telegram from Estcourt, dated Sunday, November 12th, 9.36 a.m., says :—Report received here state s that General Piet Joubert has been killed.</p>
        <p>Inquiry was made at the War Office this morning by a correspondent, who was informed that na official inti- mation of the death of General Joubert had been re- ceived.</p>
        <p>PIETERMARITZBURG, MONDAY, 4.50 p.m. It is rumoured that General Joubert has been killed</p>
        <p>in action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>TRANSVAAL.</head>
        <p>THE TRANSVAAL.</p>
        <p>Is There a Sacrifice in The Holy</p>
        <p>Communion. The Rev. F. E. Powell, vicar of St. James' Latchford, continued his course of sermons on the present Church crisis on Sunday evening, when he took for the subject of his sermon, "Is there a sacrifice in Holy Communion?" He said Sir W. Harcourt had described the issue of the present controversy as resolving itself into the question whether the clergy were priests of the sacrifice or minis- ters of the Gospel. But Isaiah did not see that there was necessary opposition between these two terms. Speak- of the Kingdom of Christ, which in the fulness of times was to be established throughout the world, he said, "Ye shall be named the priests of the Lord : men , shall call you the ministers of our God." The whole c m- ception of Christianity was sacrificial. Self-sacrifice was of the very essence of Christ's life and work. Self-sacri- fice therefoie must be the aim of all those who professed to follow Him. "The Sou of Man is not a.ome to be minis- tered unto.but to minister, and to give His life a ransom for many." In these words they had the two ideas of ministering and sacrifice. Every man was called to be a priest i e., to offer up spiritual sacrifices. He realized his priesthood when he did his daily work in the spirit of Christ, i e., to the glory of God and for the good of mankind. To keep this sacerdotal idea alive in the world, to stir men's hearts and consciences,tbey wanted religion. The priesthood of religion was only one of the oriesthoods (e g., art, commerce, science, literature) of life—a priesthood a hose privilege it was to remind all men of their sacerdotal privilege of turning their ordinary work into a spiritual sacrifice unto God. And what religion was to life in general, they claimed the Holy Eucharist to be to religion itself. In that ordinance they approached the central mystery of our Faith. In it they commemorated the Sacrifice of Christ. People s lmeti me said that when Christ came sacrifices ceaFed. Rather should they say Sacrifice was then first properly uncierstood. The idea of approaching God through sacrifice was right in Hebrew or Pagan systems, . . .</p>
        <p>but th9.only sicri.fice through which they Could- really-</p>
        <p>approich Lind was the sacrifice of the perfect life of the Son of Man on the altar of Calvary. They were taught that the Priesthood of Christ was eternal. His sacrifice was also eternal. They beq,vel that. what the Passover sacrifice was to the Jew, the</p>
        <p>Eucharistic sacrifice was to the Christian. To the Jew it was :—Offer this as the figure of that which was to come. To the Christian it was :—Offer this as the memorial of that which did come. The Eternal Sacri- fice in [leaven was in time prefigured on earth in the sacrifices of the Old Testament from the beginning, and was c )mmernorated in the sacrifice of the new dispensa- tion till he came again. Christ their l'a.ssfiver as sacri- ficed for us, therefore they must keep the feast. St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>There _was a large .congregation at the men's service,</p>
        <p>held in S. Peter's Church on Sunday afternoon, when th.l Rev. P. J. Byrne, curate of S. Peter's, Birkdale, and formerly curate ot S. Ann's, Warrington, gave a stirr- ing address on "The Man of Value." serviee was conduct id by the Rev. W. Bracecamp, and Miss M. Taylor sang two solos, " The Star of Betblehem "and " The Dreim of Paradise" inoit creditably. Welcome Home to the Vicar.</p>
        <p>The Rev. B. Percival, vicar of St. Peter's, Warrington, has returned home from the Canary Islands, where he has been on a visit for the benefit of his health, and on Monday evening a " welcome home was given in the sehoolroom, John-street. The room was attractively decorated foe the occasion, the words " Welcome home" and " Welcome, thrice welcome" being di,played over the platform. Light refreshments were provided, and were served by Mesdames Bracecamp. Betnforth, Ling- ley, and Edgar, and Miss Brookes, and during the even lag an excellent musical programme was gone through, wader the direction of Mr. Percy Randall, and included songs by Messrs. Clayton, A. Burdett, Cooper, and Thomas Reedish ; Mrs Yeomans, and the Misses Ashton and Barton ; violin solos by Mr Fred Randall, and reef- tatioee by Mr Winterbottom. A most enjoyable evening was cencianieci with a short dance. Amongst those pre- sent were the Yen. Archdeacon Madden (Liverpool). the Rev B and Ms Percival, the Rev W and Mrs Bracecainp, the Rev P J Byrne (Southport), Councillor John Roberts and Mr J Bamforth (churchwardens), Mr Peter Edgar (organist). Messrs Stanley, Joseph Taylor, Cowdell, Kay, Spencer, Dr and Mes Adams, Dr and Mrs Curtis, Mr acd Mrs Warner, and Mr and Mrs Percy Randall. TD.9 Rev. W. BRACECAIIP opened the proceedings with a brief address. during the course of which he eaid there I was no one in the room more pleased to see the Vicar than he was, and he prayed that he might long be , spared to work amongst them. (Applause.) Archdeacon MADDEN then addressed the meeting and expressed the hope that God would bless the Vicar and his wife, and that they would be spared for many years to do more good w irk together, (App'ause.) 'the Rev. B. PERCIVAL, who was warmly applauded en risine to return thanks for the hearty welcome which had been extended to him, said it had cheered him very much, and he had also beencheered in the past during his illness by the great amount of sympathy that had been shown to him—the real practical sympathy. It required an illness of this kind ti prove who were one's fiiends. He had found he had 49 out of 50 more friends, than he had ever dreamt of at all, in fact every pari•bionor had proved to be a true friend. He could not help lookiag back upon his sickness as a blessine in diseujse. God had ordered it its His providence for goed for hirucelf and his people, and he would like to thank all who had expressed sympathy on hie behalf. 'fhere were many p ieple who had offered up prayers on his behalf, and he was sure they had been heard, lie wished to tnaak all those who nad worked during his absence. Nothing had suffered, and everything had goue ahead as fully and as successfully as he cou'd have wished ; certainly more than he could hive hoped for. He felt in going away that there was not the slightest necessity for fear, as Mr. Bracecamp would carry out everything in a loyal and satisfactory manner, and he had done so. (Applause.) He was pleased to be able to testify to that fact. He left behind him a wife who had seen after his interests ; she had in fact been kind of second curate. She had worked extremely hard, and he had to thank her publicly for the com tort he had had in knowing that the parish would have someone in the lady line to look after its interests. (Applause.) In conclusion whatever happened to him would be Ged'e will. He had great faith in Gad and in His providence, and he had not the slightest fear that one day would be cut from his life more than He saw fre At the same time he must do his part. He must not venture any- thing, but do what was right. Ho Was in the hands of God, and its His on good time He would restore him to health and strength if it was His will. (Applause,) The Church Crisis. Interesting Address by the Rev. T. H. Sparshott</p>
        <p>Special Protestant sermons were preached in St. Ann'. Church. Warrington, on Sunday by the Rev. T. H. Sparshott, of London, lecturer for the Church Association and National Protestant League. The subject spoken upon in the morning was "The Protestant position "and in the evening the rev. gentleman preached an able discourse on "The pro- test pathway." There were good congregations at each service.—On Monday evening a meeting was held in the schools,when the Rev.T.l.l. Sparshott de- livered an address with reference to the Church Crisis. There was a moderate attendance presided over by the Vicar (the Rev. James Davies). During the course of his opening remarks,the Chairman said that in the Church of England they were no!-, re- quired to accept any article of faith or belief unless it was proved by the word of God. The groat difficulty in the Church of England was that there wero in their own pail clergymen who wire teaching doctrines diemetri- cally pplied to the thirty-nine articles which they had promised to keep laud• observe, and they were introduc- ing tights and ceremonies which were, acccording to the opinion of the two Archbishops, contrary to the Act by which they had been ordained. The result was t'lat they were really breaking their ordination vows, and trying to bring the Church of England into conformity with the Ceurch of Rome. They must take this to heart and be outspoken, but let the outspokenness be in the spirit of love and charity. He would now call upon Mr. Sparshott to address ttem. The subject of his address was" TOO pr,s,mt crisis in the Church of England." The Rev. T. H. Speasaorr said he was in full harmony with the views set up by their Vicar in his opening address. "The Bible and the Bible alene is the religion of Proteetants " was Chillingworth's celebrated state- ment. They maintained that the Bible and the Bible alone was the religion of the Church of England. If they carefully studied their Prayer Books they would fina that they were not asked to believe anything because it s as in the Prayer Book. rhey were only asked to believe that which was proved by the most certain war- rants of Holy Scripture. The first fundamental difference betsv!en the Church of England and the 'church of Rome was that they (the Church of England) believed that the defint d rule of faith and practice was the Bible and the Bible alone. In the Church of Rome they believed that the defined rule of faith ad practice was, first, tradition and secondly, the scriptures. The first article of the Creed of Pepius, INT was on the authority of tradition. The Roman Church stated a great many things which were true. But the contention of the Church of England was that there was not one saying of the Lord Jesus Christ which could be proved to have been said which was not contained in the New Testament. (Hear, hear.) Their second contention with the Chureh of Rome was that many of the doctrines which she endeavoured to establish from tradition were altogether opposed to the Holy Scriptures. Their contention With these who were now popularly</p>
        <p>called Ritualists was that whilst they were gaining a pwition in the Church of England, they were iicensed and paia under one eotitiieon a.na they were acing in dirsct otelosition to it. What should they do at the present time, whatever views they might take of the came or the war if one of our men should turn round in the hour of ioataie and fight against the British troops ? (A. voice : I sneold shoot him.) That was the position of Ritualists in the Church of England. Every time a man was licensed as curate or inducted to a living he must read and sign a declaration in the presence of his Bishop stating that he assented to the thirty-nine articles of religion,apd the Book of Common Prayer and that he believed th doctrines of the Church of England, and would use the forms provided in the Book of Common Prayer and no others except those ordained by lawful aueiszity. It was cot a question of whether the Prayer Book was right or wrong; when he was licensed he had to state that he believed the Prayer Book to be in accord- ance with the Bible and in the administration of sacra- ments would use that form in the Prayer Book and no other except it was ordeined by lawful authority. They would probably be aware that what was now known as Ritualism was the same thing as what was once called the Oxford movement. It was called the Oxford movement because it commenced in the Univer- sity of Oxford. It was now more popularly called Ritu- alism because its object was set forth by Ritualistic practices or Kindergarten lessons. The Oxford move- ment bad been goicg on for sixty-six years. It was in the year 1833 that the society was founded, and they formulated their doctrir.es in " fracts for the times" and worked in secret. That they were in a crisis everybody seemed to admit. They could scarcely pick up a newspaper without seeing something about the Crisis in the Church. This crisis was brought about by certain doctrint s and practices. The dispute was not merely about whether a man should wear ribbons or not ; they would never dispute the matter on that point. The particular dresses adopted by Rome were brought in from the heataen priesthood, but were banished from the Church of England because they were the marks of a sacerdotal priesthood. there was no priesthood between the Lord Jesus Christ and the sinner. The object of the Christian minister was to bring the individual sinner into personal contact by a per- slnal living faith. The foundation of the true religion was a personal faith in a personal Christ. The Ritualistic movement had gone on spreading. In the universities men were trained and cradled in thesystem. Every Bishop who had been appointed had been some- what nearer to the Roman tendency than his pre- decessor. There were only four or five Bishops whom they might call evangelical. He thought it should be a subject for special prayer that a true servant of God would be appointed to succeed Dr. Ryle as Bishop of Liverpool. (Applause.) Nearly every diocese had its own theological college and the men were trained in a special manner. It would be better to have one great college instead of so many diocesan colleges. There should be one college for the whole of the Church of England where men could be taught the doctrines of the Book of Common Prayer instead of the teaching of an individual diocesan bishop. The re- sult of having so many colleges was that men had been trained in the saceid itat teaching. In many colleges, confessional was adopted. According to the Church of Rom'. everyone must go to confession before mass and the priest himself made a confession before celebrating mass. Ihe minds of the people wanted to be openei as to confessional. (Hear, hear.) Dr. Walsh and John Keasit had opened the eyes of the people. (Applause.) Iney had hal plain speaking in the House of Commons, on the 11th of April and 10th of May. The Government was obliged to begin some legislation to put down Ritualism, unless the bishops did something. That was the substance of a resolution pro- posed by Sir Richard Webster. The Bishops were professing to do something. Dr. Temple had sat for a week to hear arguments against the use of incense and the carrying of lights in procession. It seemed to him to be humiliating for a man in the position of Dr. Temple to sit there and hear arguments about such rub- bish. After a long time the Archbishops said these two things were illegal. He (the speaker) thought that burning incense and carrying lights was just air absurd as clergymen playing football in church. They could not:respect the opinion of men like Dr. Temple and others as they had tor years past been taking part in proces- sions where lights had been used, and had been present at the celebration of the mass in the English Church when incense had been used. The Biehops were making a "fuss" about these things to prevent the Government from taking steps to put a stop to Rituaeism. Were they going to cast away all that their forefathers had gained for them and listen to some priestly boy of twenty-five who did not know the first elements of Christian religion, and did not know the Catechism and the Prayer Book ? It was time for them to awaken. They were not going to allow to slip from their hands what they had received from their fore- fathers, and what was to be handed to their children that they might be a blessing to them. (Applause.)—A vote of thanks was accorded to the Rev. T. H. Sparshott and toe proceedings terminated,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Lecture at Earlestown.</head>
        <p>Lecture at Earlestown.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the Viaduct Institute was packed to overflowing, many being unable to gain admittance, on the occasion of a lecture to be delivered by the Rev.J. H. Riddette of H tydock, on" Paul Kruger,his country, his people, his neighleJurs, and his war with Britain: or Life among the Boers." At the present time the lecture appealed with peculiar force te the public, and conse- quently the liveliest interest was evinced in a description of the country about which the incidents of the present war centre, which was increased twofold by the fact that the rev, lecturer was for thret years an Outlander in the Transvaal. The le,ture wail illustrated by about 90 oxy-hydrogen limelight views, the lantern being manipulated by Mr. J. H. Graham. The Rev. E. Bocock presided, and said they were that night to have a lecture on the Transvaal and it was poeeible, though we were at war with the Boer, we did not fail to recognise that they possessed many of those qualities which had made our own race great and strong. There were times in our history when we had looked upon our enemies as our born enemies ; once we so looked upon the Spaniards, and then the French, which was very unwise, nut we did not look upon the Boer as such. We rather looked upon him as a man whose ideas were compatible with our, own. As far as he could see, the Boer had set himself diametrically opposite to the mission of our race, for he, the speaker,was one of those who believed thse we had a mission. He believed we should come out of the present struggle well, and that ' the Boer and Briton would live in amity in the future. (Applause.) But we could not allow the matter to end until we had secured to our fellow countrymen, those rights of freedom which we ourselves hold. (Cheers.) Tee Lecturer said ne had a very great respect for the intellectual capacity of a railway man, and he wanted to tell them one thing before he started, they were go- ing to get Into an express train, and they had a long journey in front of them. Toe lights were then turned down, and the first picture thrown on the screen was a portrait of President Kruger, in his 75:11 year. He was only seven years of age when be shot his first big game, and 11 when he shot his first lion. He was an Outlander as much as he the lecturer, for he was born in Cape Colony, and he was one of those who took part in the Great Tr&amp; k. He was an assistant Field Cornet at 16, and at 31 years old he was leading tee raid into the Orange Free State. He got £B,OOO per year as President, and £5OO per year, coffee money. His next picture was of the Drakerasbarg range of mountains, and the house where the lecturer lived, when in the Transvaal. Other views were of the Witwatersrand district, with a description of its ctaracteristec Johannesburg 10 years ago and 3 years ago, and Church- street, Pretoria. The Transvaal Dutch were originally Settlers in Cape Colony, and that at one time was a Dutch possession. The cause of the migration of these people into the interior was the dislike el British rule, and a determination to govern the natives in their own way. There was a great difference between the Boer and :British idea as to what was the right treatment of the native. The great trek was a venturesome thing, an enterprise beset by many perils, NOt the least being that of meeting with lions. He alluded to the annexa- tion of the Transvaal by Sir Theophilue Shepstoae, and said that had we not have annexed it, the natives would have eaten up the Boers. He believed it was the mis- sion of the British nation to lift up the native and make him a useful member of the community. He marvelled at the madness of the Free States in allying themselves with the Boers. As far as he understood the politics of South Africa, the Free States had nothing to gain and everything to lose, but he laid it to the ambition of President Steyn. Then followed a series of views of Capetown, Natal, Kimberley, Orange Free Scale, Mate- king, Delagoa Bey,Durban,ane Pietermaritzburg,Laings Nek, Amajube Hill. A portrait of Dr. Jameson called forth cheers, and the lecturer said that many people attributed the troubles of the Transvaal to the raid. It certainly gave Kruger the excuse he needed fcr increas- ing his arrnaineuts, and furthering his idea of declaring the Tiansvaal a Sovereign IndepentionaliState.But it was only fair CO state that the grievaucee of the Outlanders were substantial lon before the raid. Spelling of the Bleemfontein Cenfererce, he said, in his opinion he did</p>
        <p>no: bAieve that Paul Kruger had any idea at the Con- ference of making any concession. He hoped and trusted thlt when Sir Redvers Buller had put things right that a lasting peace would be established, and that the Dutch andiEnglish would live in amity together. (Applause.) The Lecturer moved a vote of thauks to the Chairman which was _he4rtily accorded. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. C. W. Einmitt in vad a vote of thanks to the Lecturer, which was seconded by Mr. G. Dring and heartily carried.</p>
        <p>After singing a verse of the National Anthem the audi- OOC3 dispersed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WIDNES MAN'S TERRIBLE DEATH.</head>
        <p>A WIDNES MAN'S TERRIBLE DEATH.</p>
        <p>The Inquest.</p>
        <p>Coroner Brighouse held an inquest at Whines on Tues- day touching he death of D,iniel Hirst, a4ed sixty- four, residing at 30, Albert-road. Widnes, who inct his deatn by falling into a (limed quarry in Peelnouse-lane on Thuisday last. Sarah Ellen Hirst, daughter, said she last saw her father at dinner-time on Monday last week. It was a usual thing for him to go away for a week at a time, leaving no word of his whereabouts. She knew nothir,g of the affair until Sunday, when a boy named Wnarton came to the house and informed her what had happened. Dr. Wtennan and Dr. Edwards were called in, but death ensued shortly after. Miss Alice Caldwell, residing in Rainhill, said she saw deceased about half-past four on Thursday afterooon. Be came into her pul.lic house and galled for a glass of beer, when she noticAl som:ithing strange in his manner.</p>
        <p>Police-Constable Eton also gave evidence as to seeing hinn on Thuraday near the Black Horse, Fara- Worth.</p>
        <p>William Smith, living at the Lodge, Farnworth, said he went for a walk on Sunday morning, and 10-ked down the delph, whEre he saw a body at the bottom The quarry was about 200 yards from the highway. In his estimation it would be ab Jut 901 t deep. Police-con- stable J. Goldbv assisted to get the :body up, and upon examining his clothing found the following letters : " November 9.—Dear Annie an 1 Sally : This is to in- form you that I fell into the quarry on Monday, and has been lying on the ground since. lam dying. Good- bye.—Daniel Hirst." " Friday, 8.30.-1 am still alive, but very weak, and if no one c )mes to relieve M 9 trlay I shall bleed to death. lam badly hurc in the loin, after crawling on my hands and knees in the mud." The Con,table mid his c:othes were very muddy.</p>
        <p>Dr. M'Lennan made an examination of the body, and fout4d it badly bruised. After hear' sg further evidence, the jury returned a verdict of " Accident...l death," adding a ridlr that the quarry ought t's be fenced off. The Cironer said that if he was the owner of the quarry he woula tell them to mind the)r own business as tney could not compel him to fence it,</p>
        <p>A Remarkable Painting at Martinscroft. The ttev Father Lowe, itoman Catholic Pastor at Martinscroft has recently, in this disti let, made a discovery which will doubtless, be of considerable interest to Art Connoisseure. There has come into his possession a painting, evidently of great anti- quity, representing the Redeemer, dre:ased in the costume of a Jewish boy. The details of the broca- ded garments and other accessories of the picture evince the most marvellous delicacy and finish, whilst the life-like countenance of toe Christ-child is wonderful in its expression of sweetness and be- nignity. The arms ot the figure are partially ex- tended, and by some sabtle trick of the limner's art, they seem stretched out towards the beholder, in no matter vc hat po4itim the latter may stand be- fore the picture. The eyes, too, seem to change the direction of their regard with the apparent altera- tion of the position of :the figure This art treasure has beAi inspected by an eminent expert from Man- chester, who pronounced it a genuine work of Fma Phillip° Li ppi, the Florentine painter waose mas- terpieces of sacred art adorn so many European gall leries. He iburished between A.D., 1431 and 1434. Father Lowe has already refused at least one ap- parently liberal offer for his remarkable " dis- covery."</p>
        <p>NATIONAL TOIST.—" Here's to John Bull, Pe, and Sandy in Grant's Morella Cherry Brandy 1" Most delicious and comforting on a damp or frosty day. Try it 341" nip" of It, and you will be pleased. Sold at all Bars, also in bottle by Wine Merchants. Don't be pnt off with inferior makes.— 'lHos. GRANT AND SONS, Maidstone. Makers of Cherry Whiskey, Sloe Gin &amp;c.—AA vt.</p>
