      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>CON FIR MATION NEEDED.</head>
        <p>CON FIR MATION NEEDED.</p>
        <p>The " Pall Malt tirazette "says :—We have been wait- ing for a smeation,and it has come: Reuter's correspon- dent at Ladysmith and a correspondent of the" Cape Times,"telegraphlng by way of Lourenco Marques, both report that General Joubert was killed in action a week ago. What is to be made of the story ? Is it on a par with the ridiculous rumour purveyed to the Paris papers that Ladysmith itself bad fallen ? Or is it true ? Frankly we cannot tell. It comes from thoroughly re- sponsible sources, but then those sources may have been inspired by some cir cumstantially mendacious Kaffir, Againlit may be a ruse of war put about by Joubert himself to lure the garrison into carelessness before the delivery of a last deiperate attack. We may say at any rate if he is really dead the battlefield is the fittest deathbed for him no 1 ess thaa for General Symons. He has fought us scparely and fairly according to his read- ing of the obscure laws of warfare. But he might have a fight or two or even three left in him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>BOERS SURROUNDING ESTCOURT.</head>
        <p>BOERS SURROUNDING ESTCOURT.</p>
        <p>Failure to Communicate with Ladysmith.</p>
        <p>ESTCOURT, SUNDAY, 9 30 a.m.</p>
        <p>The Boers are gradually hemming Estcourt in. Detached b )(lies of the enemy have been seen south of the River Tugela.—This rene we 1 activity indicates a probable at- tack upon the place. Signallers, under the direction a Captain Keyser, have been out ad day ',ltaly endeavour- ing to communicate with Ladysmith. PIETERMAEUTZBUR Gt Nov. 13th, 12.15 p.m. Heavy cannonading was heard in the direction of Lady- smith this morning. ReliablE information shows that the Boars are making for the line below Estcourt and above Mooi River.</p>
        <p>Railway Line Destroyed at Chieveley.</p>
        <p>STO)TIRT, MONDAX.</p>
        <p>To-day a party of Boers, some 500 strong, accom- panied by a waggon, arrived at Chieveley railway station, which lies about twelve miles north o 2 Estcourt. They succeeded in blowing up the line in tw ) places with dynamite, but retired when a patrol of the King's Royal Rifles Mouate A Infantry made its a pear.ince upon the scene.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>BULLER'S PLANS DEVELOPING. Lord Methuen's Departure for the North.</head>
        <p>BULLER'S PLANS DEVELOPING. Lord Methuen's Departure for the North.</p>
        <p>The Cape Town correspondent of the " Daily Mail" telegraphed on Friday :—Lieutenant-General Lord Alethuea and staff of the Firs 7, Di viiion went north to- night by train. They had a great send-off. Stirring News Expected. The Central N3w3 le.irns that the forward movement, having for its object the relief of the ga rrisoa at Kim- lierley, has already commenced. Stirring news from that frontier may be soon expected.</p>
        <p>The Boer Irish Brigade. COLONEL BLAKE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>DURBAN, MOND&amp;Y.</p>
        <p>The " Volkstein "announces that Colonel Blake, corn_</p>
        <p>_ mander of the Irish Brigade, and Commandant Vandarn, chief of th. Johannesburg Police, are among the leading Boers wounded in the recent encounters with the British.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>LANCASHIRE RESERVISTS. D EPARTURES-FROM WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE RESERVISTS. D EPARTURES-FROM WAR- RINGTON.</p>
        <p>Hearty "Send offs"</p>
        <p>All the reservists of Sections A, B, and C of the South Lancashire Regiment, numbering about 600, and about 200 of the Liverpool Regiment are to be mobilized, with the result that the military authorities at Orford Barracks have been very busy during the past week in outfitting and drafting those who have been recalled to the colours. The usual notices were posted throughout the town on Saturday, and the reservists are to answer on or before Monday next, the 20th inst. The South Lancashires are being dratted in the first instance to Pleston, and the Liverpools to Enniskillen. Alter being medically examined the men declared fit will be pro- vided with entirely new outfits, and armed with Lee- Enfield rifles. •</p>
        <p>Presentations at Msssrs. Rylands' Bros.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Works.</head>
        <p>Works.</p>
        <p>On Monday three of the workmen in the employ of Messrs. Rylands' Brothers, reserves attached to the South Lancashire and Manchester Regiments (Sergeant Houghton and Privates Pessol and Warburton), were commanded to join their respective regiments now mobilising with a view to strengthening our forces at the Cape. Immediately this was made known at the works a collection was made, and the subscriptions from the various de partmen to reached a handsome total. Out of the money smoking outfits to suit the re- spective tastes of the men were purchased and preseeted to them during the dinner hour b the presence of Lun- .reds of the workmen. A temporary platform was ar- ranged, and from this the manager (Mr J. J. Nield), ad- dressed the men briefly, and expressed the hope that their journey might be followed by nothing worse than a return journey. He trested that betore their arrival the Boers would realize the futility of their resistance to British arms, and that the fighting services of their comrades would not be required. (Applause.) if, however, such were not the cese no one who few the mere would doubt that they would prove themselves to be typical British soldiers, ,and that to them would belcog a dre share of the honour of restoring peace and tranquillity to that unhappy country. In presenting the men with r'ie souvenirs, he remarked that these " Al33ent-minded beggars " of Rudyard Kipling were not to be allowed t 3 forget those they had left behind for the want of a re- minder, and the beautiful pipes which they would often have between their teeth would help them to remember the many friends they had left behind, and that they would be cheered and stimulated by the very sincere and hearty good wishes which accompanied their departure. Ira cenclusion he assured the men that Rvlands Bros' gates would notbe found c'osed to the n when they re- turned from the ranks to re-enter civil life,but that they mieht, rely upon employment again being found them if sought. (Applause.) Mr. T. J. Huese (secretary) said he had made it his duty to be present to join in the demonstration of good wishes. Time did not allow of speech-making, but after expressing his great appreciation of the sympathe and good feeling shows by the men to their comrades called to the front, and to the patriotic spirit displayed, he endorsed the remarks of Mr. Nield and joined him in the hope that he war might come to a speedier termina- tion than generally expected, and that there would be no call for any active service on the part of their com- rades. If to, however, the three men looked very fit, and would no doubt gallantly respond to their generals' commands. He wished them God-speed and a safe re- turn to civilian life. (Applause.) Mr. SAMUEL BALDRY, timekeeper (ex-sergeant of the company to which two of the men were attached), said he would be pleased to give the men privately some of his experiences as a soldier in South Atriaa and to advise them as to the expenditure of the money which would come to them as a result of the liberal collection made on their behalf. He might say that a few shillings would be found useful on board ship, as they would find it necessary to make use of the canteen to proeure those little luxuries which were not officially included in a soldier's diet. Had more time been given they might have bid good-bye to the men in some other way, but under the circumstances he thought that it had b-en wisely decided to hand over the major portion of the subscriptions to the men to spend in that way which would prove most advantageous to them. (Hear, bear.) He had a good opinion of the men going to the front,and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Latchford v. Crossleys Ist.</p>
        <p>The return match between the above clubs was played at Manchester on Saturday under very unfavourable circumstanc s, the weather being very biisterous, and the ground most treacherous. Latchford had the most of the game throughout, and again came off victorious by four goals to (-me. For the home team Orme, at back, played a grand game, and the goalkeeper also did some smart work. For the visitors Carter was again con- snicucus, scoring tte first goal after a good run. Knight, Grounds, and Albins4n also scored one each. Latchlt4rd ball backs played a very safe game, feeding their for- wards admirably. The full backs asd goalkeeper had net much to di, as the visitors were on the aggressive the greater part of the game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Stockton Heath V. iSouth Manchester.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath V. iSouth Manchester.</p>
        <p>Played at Warrisgtoo, in cold and dull weather. The home team started and played for fifteen minutes with a man short. The visitors seemed to be very much hamperrd by the absence of an oeposing goal-keeper ; oe the other hand, Stockton IleAtli played up hard, and got their only goal (Woods) just before their eleventh man turned up. After this the visitors got more into their stride, the sheet passing tacties of the forwards completely baffling the home defence. Richardson opened the scoring for the Manchester men by neatly turning the ball into the eustodianless goal with the point of ids stick when tackled by Barlow. Half-time score :—One all. On rebullying, the senior club repeated their Halifax performance, Frank Rickitt putting the finishing touch on within the minute. The hall ei as carried back to the centre ef the field, only to be raced again into :he Stockton Heath circle, the same player, with one of his powerful wrist drives, making the score South Manchester, three goals ; Stock'on Heath, one. From this point the play became very raeged,for although the Mancuaiau forwards apparently had their opponents' Real entirely at their mercy on several occasions they failed to score till Leroy took a pass from A Eastwood (who had brought the ball well up the field) and drove into the net. Both teams suffered from the absence of regular players. H. Eastwood and Goss were away from the ranks of the Manchester Club. Stockton Heath however, were strengthened by the inclusion of the Rev. J W Dickinson in the forward line, this being his first appearance on the hockey field since about the middle of last season. rhe well-known and speedy county player made many attempts to get away, but he was will look after by Dackray at left half, who played in his top form. The cnly Must which Sutton had to negotiate was from a pass of Dickinson's; the final effort was,however, no trouble to the visitor? goal guardian, who stopped and cleared the ball well into touch. Players deserv- in,, of mention are Dickinson, Sutcliffe, Hatton, and Piekton for the Nome lot, while the visiting team with the exception of the first twenty minutes, all played well F Leroy, one of the reserve men, making a very promising debut at inside left forward. Final score : Soeth Manchester, four goals; Stockton Heath, one,</p>
        <p>South Manchester A v, Stockton Heath A.</p>
        <p>At Whalley Range. Stockton Heath turned up with only nine men, and to equalise the game the Scuth Man- che,ter captain placed the services of one of his for- wards at the disposal of Stockton Heath, the two teams pla.ving ten men aside throughout. South won the toss, and, placing with the wind, pressed right from the start, aud succeeded in notching four goals to nothing by Stockton Math. kfter half time the game was mire even, but South still kept the upper hand, and added another goal to their score. The final score after a pleasant game resultA in a win for South Manchester by five goals, scored by Whitehead (two), Lomax (two), and Johnson, to none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Concert at Stretton.</head>
        <p>Concert at Stretton.</p>
        <p>In aid of the funds of the "Soldiers' and Sailors' Families msociation a concert was held in Strettm Scnools on Monday evening. The prograccme,which was of an excellent charact3r throughout, was re- ceived with the greatest enthusiasm by a large audience, especially the part-singing of Miss Allen and Messrs. Knowles, White and Bennett. The vocalists, all of whom were accorded a most hearty reception, were Misses Eva Haywood, Isobel Rigby, Lilian Allen; and Messrs. Knowles, White, Behuett and-H. Bennett. Quartettes on the mandolins and guitar were very ably given by the Misses E. and F. Carlisle, L. Allen, and Isobel Rigby. At the close of the concert a hearty vote of thanks, proposed by the Chairman (Hon. and Rev. C. F. Cross), was ac- corded to the artistes,and also to the churchwardens and sidemen of the parish for taking charge of all the arrangements. The singing of the Na•ional Anthem concluded a very pleasant evening's enter- tainment.</p>
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE OP CYCLES, New and Second- band.—J. S. GARLAND 51 to I.l.idge-street, Warring- ton.— A•iv3.</p>
        <p>The "LIVERPOOL WEEKLY POST." of Saturday,</p>
        <p>November 25th, gives each reader a sheet almanac as well as some interesting pictures of the war scenes in the Transvaal besides a new story "A Mill Girl's Wooing." Advt.</p>
