      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-17_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>POLITICAL HISTORY IN THE 18th CENTURY.</p>
        <p>Lecture in the Co-operative Hall.</p>
        <p>Mr. I:4'. E. Smith, (barrister-at-:aw), LiverpAoi, nehvered the fourth of a course of six lectures on fbo pAticel histny of England in the Eighteenth Century," in the Co-operative Hall, Sankec -street, on Wednesday evening. The lecturer dealt chiefly with the - personal rule of George 111. and the politicll c3.reer of Edmund Burke.</p>
        <p>Consistently with the practine ob2erved at previous- lectures, Mr. Smith at the outset; briefly reviewed the precediog lecture, in which he spoke of Pitt's great con- - ception of a Greater Britain, ard pointed out that Pitt clearly recognised that the destiov of this C mntry was no longer to be an insular one. It was by virtue of its imperial pcsition that Great Britain was any'hins. more than an inconsiderable power to-day. If we did nob possess the empire which was the basis of our present' position we should sink to the level of a second-rate continental power. It was by virtue of our imperial peAtion that we existed as a great power to-day and it was due largely to the insight and prescience of Witliana Pitt that we ever attained to that imperial position, the beginnings of which we could trace to his nay. In the lecture that evening he had to deal with the internal affairs of the country. The internal development of a country when looked at in its true perspective was not less important than its outside policies. If the heart of the country were wrong then they would be justified in saying that the , reatest military triumphs would not very long save'it from disaster If they came to examine any controversy on internal affairs which at the time was treated as serious they would generally discover that its results might be treated as possessine interest for the historical inquirer. In looking at the internal condition of England curing the reign of George 111 they must remember that the King started with one enormous advantage over his predecessors. George I and George II were neither loved nor esteemed by the general body of Englishmen.He did not think he would underrate the influence they exercised if he were to say that the coun- try just tolerated them and only tolerated them. They were the alternative to anarchy or reaction to Roman Catholicism. Everyone knew they were foreigners. They made no secret to conceal it. Neither of them spoke the English language wi'h any readiness. It wan not difficult to understand that the personal spirit of reverence for the throne which had been the catch-word of the Tory party fir 200 or 300 years, ceaiei to possess any real significance- But tho accession of George 111. was one which promised much for the nation and the crown. Here was a prince who had been brought up in England and the English. language was his native tongue. At once it became possible for all who cherished the theory of the king's " right divine " to find a target for their mis- directed devotion. It would probably not be an exaggeration to say that the crown for a considerable period during the reign of George 111. exercised a degree of personal influence to which no English mmarch had attained from the days of the S'uarts. This he attained by corruption on a scale—if corruption could he erand —which could only be eh seribed as grand. His political views reached the dead level of average British opinion. There had never sat on the English throne a more thoroughly incompetent and mischievous sovereign. The Honse of Commons was even less representative of the opinions and the temper of the nation than in the reign of Charles I.Proces.ding,the lecturer referred to a remark able series of law suits during the reign of James 111 in which Wilkes figured prominently and- became the idol of the people at large. The article No. 45 in the " North 13ri'on," of which</p>
        <p>Wilkes was editor was referred to. Tte constitutional points arising f rem the Wilkes disputes were chiefly re- lating to the issuing of general warrants which were held to be illegal and dangerous to the people of the country. It was held that in future only individual warrants could be issued in which the name of the per- son to be arrested was specified. Another indirect rehult of the dispute was the admission of journalists ts the House of Commons and the reporting of the debates. This had revolutionised the whole fucetion of the House of Commons. A man now spoke in the House of Com- mons not so much to produce an impression on his immediate bearers but in order that what he had said might b read by his constituents and the public gener- ally. It bad purified as nothing else could have purified the whole tone of parliamentary life. The lecturer pro- _ . _</p>
        <p>needed to read extracts from the letters of "Juniuc" and referred to the fascinating mystery of their authorship, stating that there were grounds for believing that they were from the pen cf Sir Philip Francis. In conclusion the lecturer paid a warm tribute to the genius of Burke.. Whils it was generally admitted that Burke was the greatest writer and finished political philosopher who had ever devoted himself to politics in this country.there never was a greater figure in English history wha derived so few political emoluments or positions. The only position be ever held of any importance was that f paymaster of the forces, and it was the only public distinction he received unless his membership for Bristol and the rectorythip of a Scotch university could be con- sidered as such. Ile was a man who had permanently added to and enriched the volume of political thought which would illumine all ages and all centuries.</p>
        <p>Buckley.street Sunday School Band of Hope. The first limelight entertainment in connection with the above Band of Hope was given in the schoolroom on Monday evening. Mr. A. Jennings (president) presided over a large attendance. Mr. Thomas Coek and Mr. H. Woolley gave two read- ings entitled" Poor Mike" and "A Peep behind the scenes," both stories being illustrated by excellent lantern slides. Miss Carter presided at the organ and Mr. A. Leech manipulated the lantern. e LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK. BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
