        <p>MEETING OF THE TRANSVAAL RELIEF COMMITTEE. A meeting was held in the Town Hall Warkingtca of the Transvaal Campaign Relief Committee. The Mayor (Alderman Roberts) presided, and there were also present :—Majors Molyneux Seel, Smvthies, and Rooms, Captain Hutchinson, Alderman Wright. Mr. F. Monks, the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), and Mr. Taylor (Police Court Missionary) A Het of eubscriptions up to data was submitted showing that the total amount contributed was £1,438 ISA,made up as follows :—Wives and lamilies of reservists, £676 93 61 ; refugees, £144 98 ; widows and orphans, £597 is 11(1; sick and wounded, £2O 17s 7d. The list of persons entitled to re!ief was submitted by Mr. Taylor, and orders were made fo, payment of relief by the Committees. The number of cases is increasing, and some diffi- culty is experienced in ascertaining the where- abouts and circumstances of the dependent i of reservists. The Ce,mmittee request that informa- tion be sent to Mr. Taylor. Accident at Messrs. Crosfields' Works. About three o'clock on Nlouday afternoon a soap boiler named Harry Dagnall, living at 3, Heath lane, and 67 years of age, was working on an empty soap pan at Messrs. J. Crosneld and Sons Works, when he slipped and fell to the bottom of the pan, a distance of about fourteen feet, He was at once taken to the Lifirmary where it was found that he ad sustained seri us injuries to the back.</p>
